-- English: longer explanations for all 770 live questions (ENG-0001 to ENG-0770).
-- Longer explanations: each question's "why" now says in two or three sentences why the key is right, and each wrong
-- option gets a sentence or two on the mistake behind it. Every question was re-solved and each word meaning and
-- grammar rule checked. Stems, options and answer keys are unchanged: every UPDATE also matches on the current key, so a
-- question whose key was edited since is left alone. Safe to run twice.

UPDATE questions SET why = 'Whose is the possessive relative pronoun: it links a noun to its owner. Here the film belongs to the director, so "the director whose film was a blockbuster" is correct. The whole clause "whose film was a blockbuster" tells us which director earned a lot of money.', wrong = '{"0": "Who''s is a short form of \"who is\" or \"who has\". \"The director who is film was a blockbuster\" makes no sense.", "1": "Whom is the object form of who, as in \"the director whom we met\". It cannot show possession, and it cannot be followed directly by \"film\".", "3": "Which is used for things and animals, not people, and it does not show ownership. \"The director which film\" is wrong on both counts."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0001' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"I love Pakistan!" is a short sentence expressing strong feeling, and an exclamation mark is the right way to end it. The capital letters are also correct, because Pakistan is a proper noun. Each of the other sentences has a missing or wrong punctuation mark.', wrong = '{"1": "An interjection like Wow is usually followed by an exclamation mark (Wow! That''s amazing!), and the second sentence has no end mark at all.", "2": "Lets without an apostrophe means \"allows\". It should be \"Let''s eat, Grandma.\" with a comma before the person being addressed, not a full stop.", "3": "\"Is he\" is a question tag, which turns the sentence into a question. It must end with a question mark: He''s not coming, is he?"}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0002' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Head has several meanings, and the context picks one. In "the head of the table", head means the end of the table where the leader or most important person sits. The sentence itself explains this: it is where the person in charge of the meeting sits.', wrong = '{"0": "Head as a body part is its most common meaning, but the captain is standing at a place on the table, not at a part of a body.", "2": "Head can mean the top of a page, as in a heading, but no page or document is mentioned in the sentence.", "3": "Head can mean the person in charge of a school, but here it describes a position at a table, not a person."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0003' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In indirect speech, "can" moves back to "could" and "I" becomes "he". To return to direct speech, reverse these changes: "could" goes back to "can" and "he" becomes "I", and the words go inside quotation marks after a comma. This gives: He claimed, "I can speak French fluently."', wrong = '{"1": "\"Could speak\" describes an ability. \"Am speaking\" is the present continuous, describing an action happening right now, so the meaning changes.", "2": "\"I speak French fluently\" is close in meaning, but it drops the modal can. \"Could\" in the reported version must come back as \"can\".", "3": "\"Was speaking\" is the past continuous. It loses the idea of ability and is also in the wrong tense for the original direct words."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0004' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the reporting verb is in the past (said), the tenses inside the reported words move one step back. The present continuous "am taking" becomes the past continuous "was taking", and "can" becomes "could". The pronouns change too: I becomes he and my becomes his.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Am taking\" has correctly become \"was taking\", but \"can\" has been left unchanged. With a past reporting verb it must become \"could\".", "1": "\"Had taken\" is the past perfect, which is the backshift of the past simple or present perfect, not of \"am taking\". \"Can\" is also left unchanged.", "2": "\"Had been taking\" is the backshift of the present perfect continuous (have been taking). The original was \"am taking\", which becomes \"was taking\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0005' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The phrasal verb "break down", used about a person, means to lose control of one''s emotions and usually start crying. So "She broke down in front of the audience" means her feelings overcame her on stage. (Used about a machine, break down means to stop working.)', wrong = '{"0": "Singing a song is not a meaning of break down. The phrase is about losing emotional control.", "2": "Breaking down does not mean leaving. She lost control of her feelings, possibly while still on the stage.", "3": "Performing well is close to the opposite. Breaking down in front of an audience suggests she could not carry on calmly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0006' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the passive, "could have been avoided" becomes "could have avoided" in the active, and "had been followed" becomes "had followed". The doer, the workers, becomes the subject of both clauses. Both tenses must stay the same, which gives: The workers could have avoided mistakes if they had followed the instructions carefully.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Could avoid\" and \"followed\" change both tenses. The original is about the past (could have, had been), so the meaning shifts to a present or general condition.", "1": "The main clause is right, but \"had been followed\" must become \"had followed\". Using \"followed\" mixes conditional forms.", "3": "\"Avoid\" and \"follow\" put everything in the present simple. This is a general rule, not a remark about mistakes in the past."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0007' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An adverb of place tells us where an action happens. Everywhere answers the question "Where did the cat chase the rat?", so it is an adverb of place. Other adverbs of place include here, there, outside and nearby.', wrong = '{"0": "Adverbs of time answer \"when?\", such as yesterday, now or soon. Everywhere does not tell us when the chase happened.", "1": "Adverbs of manner answer \"how?\", such as quickly or quietly. Everywhere does not say how the cat chased the rat.", "2": "Adverbs of frequency answer \"how often?\", such as always or sometimes. Everywhere says nothing about how often."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0008' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Contentious describes something that causes strong argument or disagreement. Contentious matters are therefore disputed matters, issues that people argue over. Someone known for resolving them is good at settling disputes.', wrong = '{"1": "Secret means kept hidden from others. A contentious matter is often openly argued about, so secrecy is not its meaning.", "2": "Unethical means morally wrong. A matter can be argued over without anyone acting wrongly.", "3": "Private means personal or not public. Contentious matters are often public debates, so this meaning does not fit."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0009' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Deft means skilful, neat and quick, especially in using the hands. The sentence says the surgeon''s movements stayed deft "and precise" even after twelve hours, so the word must describe skilful, controlled movement. Skilful and quick is the closest meaning.', wrong = '{"1": "Clumsy means awkward and likely to make mistakes. It is the opposite of deft and does not fit with \"precise\".", "2": "Slow is the opposite of the quickness that deft suggests. The sentence praises the surgeon, it does not criticise them.", "3": "Hesitant means unsure and slow to act. Hesitant movements would not be described as precise."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0010' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Scathing means severely and harshly critical. The word "despite" signals a contrast: the film drew large crowds even though the critics attacked it. A severely critical review is what makes this result surprising.', wrong = '{"0": "A highly favourable review would not create the contrast that \"despite\" signals. Large crowds after a good review would be expected.", "2": "Scathing describes the tone of a review, not its length. A short review can be scathing.", "3": "Scathing says nothing about how hard a review is to understand. It describes how harsh the criticism is."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0011' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Verbose means using more words than are needed, that is, wordy. The sentence says listeners lost interest before the speaker reached his main point, which happens when a speech is padded with unnecessary words. Its opposite is concise.', wrong = '{"0": "Verbose is about the number of words, not loudness. A quiet speaker would be described as inaudible.", "1": "Verbose does not mean offensive. A speech can be long-winded without insulting anyone.", "3": "Short and to the point means concise, which is the opposite of verbose. A concise speech would not lose listeners in this way."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0012' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To alleviate means to make pain, suffering or a problem less severe. A painkiller is meant to reduce discomfort, so "alleviated the patient''s discomfort within minutes" means it relieved it. Relieved is the closest meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "Worsened means made worse, which is the opposite of alleviated. A painkiller is not meant to make discomfort worse.", "1": "A painkiller treats pain, it does not measure it. Measuring would be done with a scale or a doctor''s assessment.", "2": "Caused is the wrong meaning. The discomfort was already there, and the painkiller acted on it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0013' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Candid means frank, open and honest, saying what one really thinks. The committee expected a "rehearsed and evasive" answer, and the reply surprised them. This means the reply was the opposite: honest and direct.', wrong = '{"1": "Polite but vague is close to \"evasive\", which is the answer the committee expected and did not get.", "2": "Candid describes honesty, not nervousness. A nervous reply would not necessarily surprise a committee in this way.", "3": "Carefully prepared matches \"rehearsed\", the kind of answer the committee expected. Her reply was the opposite."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0014' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Meagre means small in amount and not enough. The families had to ration every bucket, which means they had to share out a limited supply carefully. This only makes sense if the water supply was scanty.', wrong = '{"0": "Polluted water is a problem of quality. Rationing every bucket points to a problem of quantity.", "2": "Plentiful means more than enough, the opposite of meagre. With plenty of water there would be no need to ration it.", "3": "Meagre does not describe distance. A faraway source is a different problem from having too little water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0015' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Meticulous means extremely careful and precise, paying close attention to every detail. The sentence says not a single detail was left out of her notes, which confirms this meaning. A meticulous person is thorough.', wrong = '{"0": "Careless means not paying attention, the opposite of meticulous. Careless notes would miss details.", "1": "Meticulous is about care and accuracy, not colour or decoration.", "3": "Notes that leave out no detail at all are full and thorough, not brief."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0016' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To impair means to damage or weaken something, especially an ability or a function. Lack of sleep makes it harder to concentrate, so it impairs a student''s concentration. Weaken is the closest meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "Improve is the opposite of impair. Lack of sleep does not improve concentration.", "1": "Measure means to find the size or amount of something. Lack of sleep does not measure concentration.", "2": "Reveal means to show something hidden. That is not what lack of sleep does to concentration."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0017' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Precarious means dangerously unstable, likely to fall, collapse or fail. Engineers ordered everyone out of the building, which shows it was unsafe. Dangerously unstable fits the context exactly.', wrong = '{"1": "Completely secure is the opposite of precarious. Engineers would not empty a secure building.", "2": "A building of historical importance would be protected, but that is not a reason to order everyone out.", "3": "Overcrowded describes too many people, while precarious describes instability. Removing everyone points to danger of collapse."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0018' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Reticent means reserved, not willing to talk openly about oneself or one''s feelings. Mehwish rarely mentioned her achievements, even to friends, which shows she did not like to talk about them. Reserved and unwilling to speak is the meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "Boastful means proudly talking about one''s own achievements. It is the opposite of reticent.", "2": "Unaware means not knowing. Mehwish knew about her achievements, she just chose not to talk about them.", "3": "Proud people often speak about their achievements. The sentence shows the opposite behaviour."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0019' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A boon is something that brings great benefit, a blessing. After months of drought, crops and animals need water, so heavy rain helps farmers greatly. That is why it is called a boon.', wrong = '{"0": "A disaster is the opposite of a boon. After a drought, rain helps farmers rather than harms them.", "1": "A warning tells of danger ahead. Boon does not carry this meaning.", "3": "A mystery is something not understood. There is nothing puzzling about rain after a drought."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0020' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To obfuscate means to make something deliberately unclear, confusing or hard to understand. The lawyer used technical jargon so that the jury would lose track of the facts. This shows the aim was to confuse, so make unclear is the meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "Clarify means to make clear, the opposite of obfuscate. Jargon used to lose the jury''s attention does not clarify.", "1": "Summarise means to give the main points briefly, which usually makes things clearer, not more confusing.", "2": "Exaggerate means to make something seem bigger than it is. That is different from hiding the facts in confusing language."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0021' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To abate means to become less strong or intense. The fever abated by evening and the child could eat again, which shows the fever went down. Lessened is the closest meaning.', wrong = '{"1": "A fever that rose sharply would make the child sicker, not able to eat again. It is the opposite of abated.", "2": "Returned would mean the fever came back. The sentence describes the child getting better.", "3": "Spread suggests something growing or moving to others. Abate means to become weaker."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0022' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Frugal means careful and economical with money, spending only on what is necessary. His frugal habits allowed him to save enough for university, which is what careful spending leads to. Economical is the closest meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "Wasteful means spending carelessly, the opposite of frugal. Wasteful habits would not help him save.", "2": "Frugal does not mean unusual or strange. It is about spending little.", "3": "Frugal has nothing to do with laziness. It describes how a person uses money."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0023' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cogent means clear, logical and convincing. The evidence convinced even the sceptics, people who usually doubt claims. Only strong, convincing evidence could do that.', wrong = '{"0": "Weak evidence is the opposite of cogent. It would not convince sceptics.", "1": "Cogent describes the strength of an argument or evidence, not its length.", "3": "Cogent says nothing about when the evidence was found. Recent evidence can still be weak."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0024' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To placate means to calm someone down and make them less angry, often by giving them something. Ali offered to pay for the broken window to make his angry neighbour feel better. Calm down is the closest meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "Provoke means to make someone angry, the opposite of placate. Offering to pay is a peace-making act.", "1": "Avoid means to keep away from someone. Ali went to his neighbour and made an offer, so he was not avoiding him.", "2": "Blame means to say someone is at fault. Offering to pay for the damage is not blaming the neighbour."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0025' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An endemic disease is one that is always present in a particular area or population. The sentence says cases of malaria occur every year in some districts. This regular, local presence is exactly what endemic means.', wrong = '{"1": "Completely wiped out means eradicated, the opposite of endemic. Cases still occur every year.", "2": "A disease brought in only by travellers is called imported. Endemic disease spreads locally and regularly.", "3": "Sudden spread across the world describes a pandemic. Endemic disease stays in one area at a steady level."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0026' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Arbitrary means based on random choice or personal whim rather than on reason or rules. The sentence explains that no reasons were given and no rules were followed. That is exactly what makes a decision arbitrary.', wrong = '{"0": "Fair and balanced decisions are based on reasons and rules. A decision with neither cannot be called fair.", "2": "Final means that cannot be changed. A decision can be final whether or not it is arbitrary.", "3": "Agreed by everyone means unanimous. Arbitrary is about how a decision is made, not how many people support it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0027' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ubiquitous means present or found everywhere. The sentence explains this itself: mobile phones are seen in every home, shop and classroom. A useful memory hook is that ubiquitous sounds a little like "everywhere at once".', wrong = '{"0": "Price is not part of the meaning. Being very expensive would make phones less common, not ubiquitous.", "1": "Extremely rare is the opposite of ubiquitous. Phones seen everywhere are not rare.", "3": "Ubiquitous says nothing about harm. It only describes how widespread something is."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0028' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Lucid means clearly expressed and easy to understand. The professor''s explanation of the Krebs cycle was so clear that even a beginner could follow it. This shows that lucid means clear.', wrong = '{"0": "Lucid is not about length. A long and tiring explanation would be harder, not easier, for a beginner to follow.", "1": "Confusing is the opposite of lucid. A confusing explanation would lose a beginner.", "2": "Humorous means funny. An explanation can be clear without being funny."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0029' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Prudent means wise and sensible, especially in planning ahead to avoid problems. Keeping a copy of the admit card in case the original is lost is a careful, sensible step. So prudent means wise and sensible here.', wrong = '{"1": "Keeping a copy of one''s own admit card is not dishonest. It is simply a safety step.", "2": "The sentence presents the copy as useful in case the original is lost, so it is not unnecessary.", "3": "Risky is the opposite of prudent. Keeping a backup copy reduces risk."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0030' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Diligent means showing steady effort and care in one''s work. The students who complete all the practice papers before the exam are the ones who keep working hard. Hard-working and careful is the closest meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "Gifted means naturally talented. A gifted student may not be hard-working, and finishing every paper is about effort, not talent.", "2": "Careless means not paying attention, the opposite of diligent. Careless students are unlikely to finish every paper.", "3": "Luck has nothing to do with diligence. Completing all the practice papers comes from effort, not chance."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0031' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Abundant means existing in large amounts, more than enough. Plentiful has the same meaning, as in "water is abundant" or "water is plentiful" after the rains. So plentiful is the closest synonym.', wrong = '{"0": "Scarce means in short supply, which is the opposite of abundant.", "1": "Average means ordinary or typical. It says nothing about something being present in large amounts.", "3": "Valuable refers to worth, not quantity. Something can be valuable because it is rare, not abundant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0032' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Obsolete means no longer used because something newer has replaced it. Floppy disks, for example, are obsolete. Outdated has the same meaning, so it is the closest synonym.', wrong = '{"0": "Modern means new and up to date, the opposite of obsolete.", "1": "Broken means damaged and not working. An obsolete machine may still work perfectly but nobody uses it any more.", "2": "Costly means expensive. Obsolete is about being out of date, not about price."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0033' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Lethargic means lacking energy and enthusiasm, slow and drowsy. Sluggish means slow-moving and inactive, so it is the closest synonym. A person often feels lethargic after a heavy meal or during an illness.', wrong = '{"1": "Energetic means full of energy and activity, the opposite of lethargic.", "2": "Anxious means worried or nervous. A person can be anxious and still very active.", "3": "Sleepless means unable to sleep. Lack of sleep may cause lethargy, but the two words do not mean the same thing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0034' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Innate describes a quality that a person or animal is born with, not one that is learned. Inborn means exactly the same thing, as in "an innate talent for music". So inborn is the closest synonym.', wrong = '{"0": "Learned (acquired) qualities come from experience or study. They are the opposite of innate ones.", "2": "Hidden means kept out of sight. An innate quality may be obvious to everyone.", "3": "Sudden means happening quickly and unexpectedly. Innate is about being present from birth, not about speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0035' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To concur means to agree with someone or to have the same opinion. For example, "The doctors concurred that surgery was needed." Agree is therefore the closest synonym.', wrong = '{"0": "Compete means to try to win against others. It is unrelated to concur, even though both begin with \"co\".", "1": "Disagree means to have a different opinion. It is the opposite of concur.", "3": "Conclude means to end something or to reach a decision. It looks similar to concur but does not mean agree."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0036' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ambiguous means having more than one possible meaning, so it is unclear which one is intended. For example, "I saw her duck" is ambiguous: duck could be a bird or a quick movement. So the closest meaning is open to more than one meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "Completely certain is the opposite of ambiguous. An ambiguous statement leaves doubt about its meaning.", "1": "Ambitious means eager to succeed. It only looks similar to ambiguous in spelling.", "2": "Openly hostile means clearly unfriendly or aggressive. It has nothing to do with unclear meaning."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0037' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Meticulous means showing great attention to detail and being very careful. Painstaking means taking great pains, that is, great care and effort, over every detail. The two words are close synonyms.', wrong = '{"1": "Careless means not paying attention, which is the opposite of meticulous.", "2": "Generous means ready to give freely. It is a different quality from being careful with details.", "3": "Nervous means anxious or worried. A meticulous person is careful, not necessarily nervous."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0038' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hostile means unfriendly and ready to fight or oppose, as in a hostile crowd. Friendly means kind and pleasant towards others. Friendly is therefore the word most nearly opposite to hostile.', wrong = '{"0": "Aggressive means ready to attack. It is close in meaning to hostile, not opposite.", "2": "Unfriendly is a synonym of hostile, not an antonym.", "3": "Indifferent means not caring either way. It sits between friendly and hostile, so it is not the true opposite."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0039' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A transparent material lets light pass straight through, so objects behind it can be seen clearly, like glass. An opaque material lets no light pass through, like wood or metal. Opaque is therefore the opposite of transparent.', wrong = '{"0": "Clear is a synonym of transparent, as in clear glass.", "1": "Translucent material lets some light through but blurs the view, like frosted glass. It lies between transparent and opaque, so it is not the opposite.", "3": "Fragile means easily broken. Glass is both transparent and fragile, so the words are not opposites."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0040' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Concise means expressing a lot in few words, brief but complete. Wordy means using too many words. Wordy is therefore the opposite of concise.', wrong = '{"0": "Brief means short, which is close in meaning to concise, not opposite.", "1": "Accurate means correct and exact. It is a different quality from the number of words used.", "2": "Clear is a different quality that often goes along with conciseness. It is not an opposite."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0041' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Generous means ready to give money, time or help freely. Stingy means unwilling to give or spend, even when one can afford to. Stingy is therefore the opposite of generous.', wrong = '{"1": "Kind is close in meaning to generous, since generous people are usually kind.", "2": "Wealthy means having a lot of money. A wealthy person can be either generous or stingy.", "3": "Honest means truthful. It is an unrelated quality, not an opposite of generous."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0042' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Scarce means in short supply, not enough for the need, as in "water is scarce in the desert". Plentiful means more than enough. Plentiful is therefore the opposite of scarce.', wrong = '{"0": "Rare means not found often. It is close in meaning to scarce, not opposite.", "2": "Scarce goods often become costly, but costly describes price, not supply. It is not the opposite.", "3": "Empty means containing nothing. It describes a container, not how much of something is available."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0043' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An optimistic person expects things to turn out well. A pessimistic person expects things to turn out badly. Pessimistic is therefore the opposite of optimistic.', wrong = '{"0": "Hopeful means expecting good things, which is a synonym of optimistic.", "1": "Cheerful means happy and positive. It is close in meaning to optimistic.", "3": "Confident means sure of oneself. It is close in meaning to optimistic, not opposite."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0044' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To acquit means to declare in court that a person is not guilty of a crime. To convict means to declare officially that a person is guilty. Convict is therefore the opposite of acquit.', wrong = '{"0": "Release means to set free. An acquitted person is released, so this is a result of acquittal, not its opposite.", "1": "A pardon also frees a person from punishment. It is close in effect to acquit, not opposite.", "2": "Questioning happens during an investigation or trial, before a verdict. It is not the opposite of a verdict of not guilty."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0045' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Diligent describes someone who works hard and steadily at a task, as in "a diligent student revises every day". Its opposite is lazy, which describes someone unwilling to work or make an effort.', wrong = '{"1": "Hard-working is a synonym of diligent, not an opposite.", "2": "Careful is close in meaning, since diligent work is usually careful work. It is not an opposite.", "3": "Punctual means arriving on time. It is a different quality, not the opposite of hard-working."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0046' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The phrase "not merely hungry" tells us that a stronger word than hungry is needed. Famished means extremely hungry, which fits a child who has gone three days without food. So famished is the best choice.', wrong = '{"0": "Peckish means slightly hungry. It is weaker than hungry, but the sentence needs something stronger.", "2": "Full means having eaten enough. It is the opposite of what three days without food would cause.", "3": "Thirsty is about needing water, not food. The sentence is about hunger."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0047' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Did not just dislike" tells us that a stronger word for the same feeling is needed. To loathe means to hate intensely, which is a stronger form of disliking. So loathed is the best choice.', wrong = '{"0": "Tolerated means accepted something without liking it. It is weaker than disliking, not stronger.", "1": "Preferred is a positive feeling, meaning liked better. It does not intensify dislike.", "3": "Noticed means became aware of something. It shows no feeling at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0048' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All four words describe anger or unhappiness, but at different strengths. Fury is wild, violent anger, so it is the strongest. The others, annoyance, irritation and displeasure, are mild forms of anger.', wrong = '{"0": "Annoyance is a mild feeling of anger, such as at a buzzing fly. It is much weaker than fury.", "1": "Irritation is mild, short-lived anger, similar to annoyance. It is not the strongest.", "2": "Displeasure is a mild feeling of being unhappy or dissatisfied. It is among the weakest words here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0049' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All four words describe being careful with money, but they carry different shades of meaning. Miserly is negative: a miser hates spending money, even when spending is reasonable or needed. The other three words suggest sensible care with money.', wrong = '{"1": "Thrifty has a positive shade. It means using money and resources wisely and avoiding waste.", "2": "Economical is neutral or positive. It means not wasting money or resources.", "3": "Prudent is positive. It means wise and careful in planning, including with money."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0050' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All four words describe someone who holds firmly to a decision. Only determined is positive, because it praises firmness of purpose in reaching a goal. The other words suggest an unreasonable refusal to change one''s mind.', wrong = '{"0": "Stubborn has a negative shade. It suggests refusing to change even when there is good reason to.", "2": "Obstinate has a negative shade, similar to stubborn. It suggests unreasonable refusal to give way.", "3": "Pig-headed is strongly negative and informal. It suggests foolish stubbornness."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0051' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To hobble means to walk with difficulty, awkwardly and slowly, often because of pain or weakness. The sentence says the old man moved slowly and leaned heavily on his stick. Hobbled fits this picture best.', wrong = '{"0": "Sprinted means ran very fast. It contradicts \"slowly\" and \"leaning heavily on his stick\".", "1": "Marched means walked with firm, regular steps, like a soldier. This does not fit a man struggling with a stick.", "3": "Skipped means moved with light, bouncy steps. It does not fit an old man leaning on a stick."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0052' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Advice, spelt with c, is the noun meaning a suggestion about what to do. Advise, spelt with s, is the verb meaning to give advice. After "the doctor''s" a noun is needed, so advice is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Advise is the verb, as in \"The doctor advised him to stop smoking\". A possessive like doctor''s must be followed by a noun.", "2": "Advize is not an English word. The verb is spelt advise.", "3": "Adviced is not a word. The past tense of advise is advised, and advice as a noun has no past tense."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0053' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Affect is usually a verb meaning to influence or change something. Effect is usually a noun meaning a result. The blank needs a verb after "can", and too much salt influences blood pressure, so affect is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Effect is normally a noun, as in \"the effect of salt\". As a verb it means to bring about, as in \"effect a change\", which does not fit here.", "2": "Afect is a misspelling. The correct verb is spelt affect, with two f''s.", "3": "Affection is a noun meaning fondness or love. It does not fit after \"can\" and has the wrong meaning."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0054' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Their is the possessive form of they, meaning belonging to them. The bags belong to the students, so their is correct. A quick check: replace it with "the students''" and the sentence still makes sense.', wrong = '{"0": "There refers to a place, as in \"put it there\", or introduces a sentence, as in \"there is\". It cannot show ownership.", "1": "They''re is a short form of \"they are\". \"The students left they are bags\" makes no sense.", "3": "Thier is a common misspelling. The correct spelling is their, with e before i."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0055' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Lose, with one o, is a verb meaning to misplace or fail to keep something. After "do not" a base form of a verb is needed, and the meaning is about misplacing the card. So lose is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Loose, with two o''s, is an adjective meaning not tight, as in a loose shirt. It is not a verb here.", "1": "Loss is a noun meaning the act of losing. \"Do not\" must be followed by a verb.", "2": "Lost is the past tense or past participle. \"Do not\" must be followed by the base form, lose."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0056' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A simile compares two different things using "like" or "as". Here a voice is compared to silk with "as smooth as", to show how soft and pleasant it sounds. So the line uses a simile.', wrong = '{"0": "A metaphor makes a comparison without \"like\" or \"as\", for example \"Her voice was silk\". This line uses \"as\".", "1": "Personification gives human qualities to something non-human. Here a human voice is compared to silk, so this is not personification.", "2": "Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration. Calling a voice smooth like silk is a comparison, not an exaggeration."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0057' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A metaphor says that one thing is another to suggest a likeness. Calling the classroom "a zoo" suggests it became noisy and wild like a zoo, without using "like" or "as". So the line uses a metaphor.', wrong = '{"1": "A simile uses \"like\" or \"as\", for example \"the classroom was like a zoo\". There is no such word here.", "2": "Onomatopoeia uses words that imitate sounds, such as buzz or bang. No such word appears.", "3": "Alliteration repeats the same first consonant sound in nearby words. The line has no such pattern."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0058' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Personification gives human actions or qualities to something that is not human. Whispering secrets is something people do, but here the wind does it. So the line uses personification.', wrong = '{"0": "A simile compares two things using \"like\" or \"as\". There is no such comparison in the line.", "2": "An oxymoron puts two contradictory words side by side, such as \"deafening silence\". The line has no such pair.", "3": "Irony is a contrast between what is said and what is meant, or what is expected and what happens. No such contrast appears."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0059' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration used for emphasis, not meant to be taken literally. Nobody has really said something a million times; the speaker is showing frustration at having to repeat it. So the line uses hyperbole.', wrong = '{"0": "A simile compares two things using \"like\" or \"as\". No comparison is made here.", "1": "Personification gives human qualities to non-human things. Nothing non-human acts like a person in this line.", "3": "Onomatopoeia uses words that imitate sounds, such as hiss or crash. No such word appears."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0060' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Onomatopoeia is the use of words whose sound imitates the noise they describe. Buzz sounds like a bee and hiss sounds like steam escaping from a kettle. Both key words in the line are sound words, so it mainly uses onomatopoeia.', wrong = '{"0": "A metaphor says one thing is another, such as \"the kettle was a snake\". Nothing is called something else here.", "1": "Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration. Bees buzzing and a kettle hissing is a plain description, not an exaggeration.", "2": "An oxymoron joins two words with opposite meanings, such as \"bitter sweet\". The line has no such pair."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0061' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An oxymoron puts together two words that seem to contradict each other. Silence is the absence of sound, so it cannot literally be deafening. The phrase suggests the silence was so heavy and noticeable that it felt overwhelming.', wrong = '{"1": "A simile uses \"like\" or \"as\" to compare two things. \"Deafening silence\" has no such comparison.", "2": "Alliteration repeats the same first consonant sound in nearby words. Deafening and silence begin with different sounds, d and s.", "3": "Onomatopoeia uses words that imitate sounds, such as bang or crash. Neither deafening nor silence imitates a sound."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0062' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Alliteration is the repetition of the same consonant sound at the start of words that are close together. Silent, snow, settled, softly, sleeping all begin with the s sound. This repeated opening sound is alliteration.', wrong = '{"0": "Assonance is the repetition of vowel sounds inside nearby words, as in \"the rain in Spain\". The question is about a repeated consonant at the start of words.", "2": "A metaphor says one thing is another. The line describes snow falling and does not call it something else.", "3": "Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration. Nothing in the line is exaggerated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0063' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Verbal irony is saying the opposite of what one really means, often to show disappointment or mockery. Losing 6-0 is a heavy defeat, so when the coach says it went "brilliantly", he means it went very badly. This is verbal irony, often called sarcasm when it is mocking.', wrong = '{"0": "A simile compares two things using \"like\" or \"as\". The coach makes no comparison.", "1": "Personification gives human qualities to something non-human. The coach is simply commenting on the match.", "3": "An oxymoron joins two contradictory words in one phrase, such as \"living dead\". The irony here comes from the whole remark clashing with the facts, not from a word pair."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0064' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Situational irony happens when the outcome of a situation is the opposite of what one would expect. A fire station exists to fight fires, so it is the last place one expects to burn down, especially because of its own faulty alarm. This unexpected twist is situational irony.', wrong = '{"0": "Verbal irony is about what someone says, meaning the opposite of their words. Here the irony lies in an event, not in a remark.", "1": "A metaphor calls one thing another to suggest a likeness. Nothing in the situation is described as something else.", "2": "Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration. The event is described plainly, without exaggeration."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0065' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Satire is writing that uses humour, exaggeration and ridicule to expose and criticise people''s foolishness or a society''s faults. Its aim is often to make readers see the problem and want to correct it. The description in the stem matches satire exactly.', wrong = '{"1": "An elegy is a sad, serious poem mourning someone who has died. It does not use ridicule.", "2": "A eulogy is a speech or piece of writing that praises someone, usually after their death. It praises rather than mocks.", "3": "An autobiography is the story of a person''s life written by that person. It is not defined by humour or ridicule."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0066' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A parody is a comic imitation of a well-known work or writer''s style. It copies the original''s features but exaggerates them to make fun of it. The stem describes exactly this, so the answer is a parody.', wrong = '{"0": "A paraphrase restates the meaning of a text in different words. It aims to explain, not to mock.", "2": "A prologue is an introductory section at the start of a play, poem or book.", "3": "An epic is a long, serious narrative poem about heroic deeds, such as the Iliad. It is not a comic imitation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0067' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage states that the Aedes mosquito "bites mostly during the day, especially in the early morning and late afternoon". It also contrasts this with the malaria mosquito. So the answer is during the day, especially early morning and late afternoon.', wrong = '{"0": "Late night is not mentioned as its biting time. The passage contrasts it with the malaria mosquito and says it bites during the day.", "1": "Noon is not mentioned. The passage names early morning and late afternoon as the peak times, and \"only\" is too narrow.", "3": "The passage says \"during the day\", so biting throughout the night contradicts it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0068' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage says the Aedes mosquito breeds in clean, still water, and that prevention "depends largely on removing breeding sites". Putting these together, removing stagnant clean water from flower pots, tyres and coolers around homes helps prevent dengue. This statement is directly supported.', wrong = '{"0": "The passage says there is \"no specific antiviral cure\", so dengue is not usually cured with antiviral drugs.", "1": "The passage says most patients recover with rest and fluids. Only \"a small number\" have a sharp fall in platelets.", "2": "The passage says the mosquito breeds in clean, still water, not in dirty water such as sewage."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0069' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a number "falls sharply", it drops suddenly and by a large amount, as on a steep graph. The passage contrasts these patients with most who recover easily, so the fall in platelets is a serious, steep drop. Suddenly and steeply is the closest meaning.', wrong = '{"1": "Sharp can describe pain, as in a sharp pain, but a platelet count is a number and cannot fall painfully.", "2": "Slightly means by a small amount, which is the opposite of sharply in this sense.", "3": "Slowly suggests a gradual drop, the opposite of a sudden, steep fall."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0070' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The main idea is the point that every sentence in the passage supports. The first sentence says sleep helps the brain store information, the study shows sleepers remembered more, and the conclusion says sleep actively strengthens memories. So the main idea is that sleep helps strengthen newly learned information.', wrong = '{"0": "The study favoured the group that learned in the evening and then slept, so the passage does not say morning is best.", "2": "Word lists were only the material used in the study. The passage is not about the best way to learn.", "3": "The passage says nothing about sleeping in lectures. It is about sleep after learning."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0071' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The last sentence says sleep "does not merely protect new memories from being disturbed; it actively strengthens them". The phrase "not merely" signals that the researchers went further than protection. Their extra conclusion is that sleep actively strengthens new memories.', wrong = '{"0": "Erasing unimportant memories is not mentioned anywhere in the passage.", "1": "The students who stayed awake recalled fewer words, so staying awake did not improve recall.", "3": "The evening group, which then slept, did better. The passage never says evening learning is harder."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0072' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The writer calls the parking system "a triumph" but then lists failures: drivers spend twice as long looking for a space, and the app crashes three times a day. Praising something that is clearly bad, in order to mock it, is sarcasm. So the tone is sarcastic.', wrong = '{"0": "The praise is contradicted by the facts given, so it is not sincere admiration.", "1": "Words like \"triumph\" and \"helpful\" are loaded with false praise. A neutral, factual report would not use them this way.", "2": "Sorrowful means sad and grieving. The writer mocks the system rather than mourning anything."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0073' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each is a singular indefinite pronoun, so a pronoun referring to it must also be singular, even when a plural noun follows in "of the boys". The group is boys, so the singular masculine form is used. The correct choice is his.', wrong = '{"1": "Their is plural. In formal grammar, singular each needs a singular pronoun, and here the gender is known.", "2": "Our refers to a group that includes the speaker. The antecedent is each of the boys, not \"we\".", "3": "Its is used for things and animals, not people."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0074' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When two singular nouns are joined by neither...nor, the pronoun that refers to them is singular. Sara and Hina are both female, so the singular feminine possessive her is correct. Her comes before the noun calculator.', wrong = '{"0": "Their is plural. Neither...nor joining two singular nouns takes a singular pronoun.", "2": "Hers is a possessive pronoun that stands alone, as in \"The calculator is hers\". It cannot come before a noun.", "3": "They is a plural subject pronoun. A possessive form is needed before the noun calculator."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0075' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With either...or and neither...nor, the pronoun agrees with the nearer of the two antecedents. Here the nearer one is "the students", which is plural. So the plural possessive their is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "His would agree with \"the teacher\", but the pronoun must agree with the nearer antecedent, \"the students\", which is plural.", "1": "Her would agree with a single female teacher, but the nearer antecedent, \"the students\", is plural.", "3": "Its is used for things, not people, and it is singular. The nearer antecedent is plural."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0076' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two antecedents joined by "and" form a plural antecedent. Ahmed and Bilal together are two people, so the plural possessive their is needed. The sentence means the project belonged to both of them.', wrong = '{"0": "His refers to one male person. The antecedent \"Ahmed and Bilal\" is two people, so it is plural.", "1": "Its is singular and used for things or animals, not people.", "2": "There is an adverb of place, as in \"over there\". It is not a possessive and cannot come before project."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0077' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a sentence begins with the impersonal pronoun one, the pronouns that refer back to it should also use one. The matching possessive is one''s. So "One should always keep one''s promises" keeps the person consistent.', wrong = '{"1": "Your switches from the third person (one) to the second person (you). This shift in person is a grammar error.", "2": "Its is used for things and animals. It cannot refer to one meaning a person.", "3": "Our switches from one to the first person plural (we). This is a shift in person."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0078' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Its, without an apostrophe, is the possessive form of it, meaning belonging to it. The tail belongs to the dog, so its is correct. A quick check: if "it is" cannot replace the word, use its with no apostrophe.', wrong = '{"0": "It''s is a short form of \"it is\" or \"it has\". \"The dog wagged it is tail\" makes no sense.", "2": "Its'' is not an English word. The possessive of it never takes an apostrophe.", "3": "Their is plural. The antecedent is one dog, and the sentence itself uses \"its owner\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0079' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each is singular, so a pronoun referring to it must be singular, and the verb "has" confirms this. The group is girls, so the singular feminine possessive her is correct before the noun assignment.', wrong = '{"0": "Their is plural, but each is singular. In formal grammar, with the gender known, a singular pronoun is needed.", "1": "Hers stands alone, as in \"The assignment is hers\". It cannot come before a noun.", "3": "His is masculine. The group is girls, so the pronoun should be feminine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0080' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The subject is "one", which is singular, even though a plural noun follows in "of the girls". One of the girls is a single female person. So the singular feminine possessive her is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Their would agree with \"girls\", but the real antecedent is \"one\", which is singular.", "1": "Its is used for things and animals, not people.", "2": "His is masculine. The person is one of the girls, so the pronoun should be feminine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0081' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A pronoun reference is unclear when the pronoun could point to more than one noun. In "Ayesha told Sana that she had won the prize", she could mean Ayesha or Sana. The reader cannot tell who won, so this sentence has an unclear reference.', wrong = '{"1": "The direct speech makes it clear: \"You have won the prize\" means Sana won.", "2": "She clearly refers to Ayesha, because the clause \"When Ayesha won the prize\" has already named the winner.", "3": "Ayesha is clearly the winner, and \"it\" plainly refers to winning the prize. There is no confusion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0082' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The blank needs a relative pronoun that stands for a person, the doctor, and acts as the subject of the verb treated. Who is the subject relative pronoun for people. So "The doctor who treated my father" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Which is used for things and animals, not for people such as a doctor.", "2": "Whom is the object form, as in \"the doctor whom we visited\". Here the pronoun is the subject of treated, so who is needed.", "3": "Whose shows possession and must be followed by a noun, as in \"whose clinic\". It cannot stand before treated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0083' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Whose is the possessive relative pronoun and means "of which" or "of whom". The cover belongs to the book, so "the book whose cover was torn" is correct. Whose can be used for things as well as people.', wrong = '{"0": "Who''s is a short form of \"who is\" or \"who has\". \"The book who is cover\" makes no sense.", "1": "Which cannot show possession directly before a noun. The correct form would be \"the book of which the cover was torn\".", "3": "Whom is the object form used for people. It cannot show possession and does not fit before cover."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0084' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a comparison with than, the pronoun takes the form it would have in the full clause. The verb am follows, so the pronoun is the subject of am: "than I am". A subject pronoun, I, is therefore needed.', wrong = '{"0": "Me is an object pronoun. \"Me am\" is ungrammatical because am needs a subject form.", "1": "Myself is a reflexive pronoun. It cannot act as the subject of a verb like am.", "2": "Mine is a possessive pronoun meaning \"belonging to me\". \"Mine am\" makes no sense."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0085' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A reflexive pronoun is used when the person doing the action is also the person receiving it. Hamza hurt Hamza, so the reflexive form for one male person, himself, is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Hisself is not standard English. The correct reflexive form is himself.", "2": "Him would suggest that Hamza hurt another male person, not himself.", "3": "Themself is used for a single person whose gender is unknown or not stated. Hamza is male, so the standard form is himself."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0086' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Both the cars" refers to two cars, so the antecedent is plural. The plural possessive pronoun their is needed before tyres. So "Both the cars had their tyres changed" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Its is singular. It would be correct for one car, but the antecedent here is two cars.", "2": "It''s is a short form of \"it is\" or \"it has\". It is not a possessive.", "3": "There is an adverb of place, not a possessive. It sounds like their but has a different meaning."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0087' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Between is a preposition, and a pronoun after a preposition must be in the object form. Me is the object form of I. So "between you and me" is correct; a quick check is that nobody would say "between I".', wrong = '{"0": "I is a subject pronoun. It cannot follow a preposition such as between.", "1": "Myself is a reflexive pronoun and needs an earlier I in the same clause, as in \"I did it myself\". There is none here.", "3": "Mine is a possessive pronoun meaning \"belonging to me\". It does not fit after between."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0088' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Since followed by a starting point (2015) shows an action that began in the past and is still going on. The present perfect, has lived, is used for this. So "She has lived in Lahore since 2015" means she moved there in 2015 and still lives there.', wrong = '{"0": "The simple present lives describes a present fact, but it cannot be used with \"since 2015\" to show duration up to now.", "1": "The present continuous is living describes what is happening now. It does not go with since and a starting point.", "2": "The simple past lived describes a finished action. Since links the past to the present, so it needs the present perfect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0089' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When two actions happened in the past, the one that happened first takes the past perfect (had + past participle). The train left before we reached the station, so "the train had left" is correct. "By the time" is a common signal for the past perfect.', wrong = '{"1": "The simple past left does not make clear that the train went before we arrived. With \"by the time\", the past perfect is needed.", "2": "The present perfect has left links the past to the present. It cannot be used inside a past time frame like \"reached\".", "3": "The simple present leaves describes habits or present facts. It does not fit a story set in the past."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0090' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'This is a real, possible future situation, so it uses the first conditional. The pattern is: if + present simple, will + base verb. "If it rains tomorrow, we will postpone the match" follows this pattern.', wrong = '{"0": "Would belongs to the second conditional, which describes unlikely or imaginary situations and needs a past tense in the if-clause (if it rained).", "2": "Postponed is the past tense, which does not fit an action planned for tomorrow.", "3": "Had postponed is the past perfect, used in third conditionals about the past. It does not fit a future plan."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0091' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'General truths and scientific facts are expressed in the simple present tense. Water boiling at 100 °C at sea level is always true, not just now. So "Water boils" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The present continuous is boiling describes something happening at this moment, such as water on a stove. It does not state a general fact.", "1": "The simple past boiled describes a finished event. A permanent scientific truth is stated in the present.", "3": "The present perfect has boiled describes a completed action with a link to now. It does not express a general truth."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0092' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The past continuous (was/were + -ing) shows an action that was in progress at a past moment. A shorter past event, in the simple past, often interrupts it. Doing homework was going on when the lights went out, so "was doing" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Am doing is the present continuous. It does not match the past tense of \"went out\".", "1": "Have done is the present perfect, which does not go with a specific past moment like when the lights went out.", "2": "Will do is the future tense. It cannot describe an action that was interrupted in the past."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0093' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Look!" draws attention to something happening at this very moment. Actions in progress now are expressed with the present continuous (is/am/are + -ing). So "The baby is sleeping" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "The simple present sleeps describes habits or general facts, such as \"The baby sleeps a lot\". It does not describe what is happening right now.", "2": "Slept is the past tense. \"Look!\" points to the present moment, not the past.", "3": "Has slept is the present perfect, describing a completed action. It does not show an action in progress."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0094' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The future perfect (will have + past participle) describes an action that will be finished before a stated time in the future. "By next June" sets that future deadline. So "she will have completed her house job" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The simple present completes does not show that the action will be finished before a future point.", "2": "Has completed is the present perfect, which looks back from now. It cannot point to a deadline in the future.", "3": "Had completed is the past perfect, used for an action completed before a past time. \"Next June\" is in the future."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0095' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The past perfect continuous (had been + -ing) describes an action that continued over a period up to a moment in the past. He had been waiting for three hours up to the time the doctor saw him. Since "saw" is past, "had been waiting" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Has been waiting is the present perfect continuous, which connects to the present. The story here is set in the past (saw).", "1": "Is waiting is the present continuous. It does not fit an event that happened in the past.", "3": "Will have been waiting is the future perfect continuous, which looks ahead to the future. It clashes with \"saw\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0096' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A finished action at a stated past time takes the simple past. "Last year" is a finished time period, so "I visited Murree last year" is correct. The present perfect is never used with exact past time words like yesterday, last year or in 2010.', wrong = '{"0": "The present perfect have visited cannot be used with a finished past time such as last year.", "1": "The past perfect had visited is used when one past event happened before another. No second past event is mentioned.", "2": "The present perfect continuous have been visiting describes an ongoing activity up to now. It cannot go with last year."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0097' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A daily habit is expressed with the simple present. With a third-person singular subject such as Ali, the verb takes -s or -es. So "Ali goes to college every day" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Go is the base form, used with I, you, we and they. It does not agree with the singular subject Ali.", "2": "Going is an -ing form and needs a helping verb such as is. \"Ali going\" is not a complete verb.", "3": "Gone is a past participle and needs has or have before it, as in \"Ali has gone\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0098' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two actions happened in the past: they ate, and then I arrived. The earlier action takes the past perfect (had + past participle). So "they had already eaten", which also explains why there was nothing left.', wrong = '{"0": "Have already eaten is the present perfect. It does not fit a story told in the past (arrived, was).", "2": "Are already eating is the present continuous. It does not fit the past tense of the rest of the sentence.", "3": "Will already eat is the future tense, which does not fit a past story."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0099' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Know is a stative verb: it describes a state of mind, not an action. Stative verbs are normally not used in continuous (-ing) forms. So even with "now", the simple present "I know the answer now" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Am knowing puts a stative verb into the present continuous. Verbs like know, believe and understand do not normally take this form.", "1": "Have been knowing is a continuous form, which stative verbs like know do not take. The present perfect would be \"have known\".", "3": "Was knowing is the past continuous. It does not fit \"now\", and know is not used in continuous forms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0100' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'English uses a fixed pattern: "This is the first/second/third time" + present perfect. The sentence already has the present perfect "you have been late", so the main verb must be "is". The lateness has happened up to now, in the present week.', wrong = '{"0": "Was belongs to the past pattern \"That was the third time + past perfect (you had been late)\". It does not match \"have been\".", "1": "Has been is not used in this fixed pattern. The pattern is \"This is the ... time\".", "2": "Will be points to the future, but the lateness has already happened this week."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0101' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Since followed by a past point (he was admitted last night) shows a period running from then up to now. The present perfect is used for this. So "The patient has not eaten anything since he was admitted" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Did not eat is the simple past, which describes a finished time. It cannot link a past point to now with since.", "2": "Does not eat is the simple present, which describes a habit, such as \"He does not eat meat\". It does not fit since.", "3": "Is not eating is the present continuous, describing what is happening now. It does not go with since and a starting point."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0102' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The future continuous (will be + -ing) shows an action that will be in progress at a particular moment in the future. "This time tomorrow" names that moment. So "I will be flying over the Arabian Sea" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Flew is the simple past, which does not fit a future time such as tomorrow.", "2": "Have flown is the present perfect, linking the past to now. It cannot describe a future moment.", "3": "Had been flying is the past perfect continuous, which refers to a time before a past moment."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0103' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In time clauses beginning with as soon as, when, after, before or until, English uses the simple present to talk about the future. The main clause keeps will. So "I will call you as soon as I reach home" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Will reach puts will inside a future time clause. English does not use will after as soon as.", "1": "Reached is the past tense. It does not fit a plan about the future.", "3": "Would reach suggests an imaginary or reported situation. It does not fit a real future plan."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0104' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"How long" asks about the duration of an activity that began in the past and is still going on. The present perfect continuous (have been + -ing) expresses this. "By now" confirms the person still works there, so "have you been working" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Are you working is the present continuous. It describes what is happening now and cannot show duration up to now.", "1": "Do you work is the simple present. It describes habits or facts, not how long something has continued.", "2": "Were you working is the past continuous, which suggests the job is over. \"By now\" shows it is still going on."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0105' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'This is the second conditional, used for an imaginary or unlikely present situation. The pattern is: if + past simple, would + base verb. The speaker does not know the answer, so "If I knew the answer, I would tell you" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Know (present simple) belongs to the first conditional, which pairs with will, not would.", "2": "Will is not used in the if-clause of a conditional sentence.", "3": "Had know is not a correct verb form. The past perfect would be \"had known\", used in the third conditional."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0106' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'This is the third conditional, used for an imaginary past situation that did not happen. The pattern is: if + had + past participle, would have + past participle. She did not take her medicine and became ill, so "she would not have become so ill" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Would not become is a second conditional main clause, about the present or future. The if-clause here is about the past.", "2": "Will not become belongs to the first conditional, about real future possibilities.", "3": "Did not become states a real past fact. The sentence imagines a different past result, which needs would have."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0107' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The zero conditional is used for general truths and scientific facts. It uses the present simple in both the if-clause and the main clause. Heating ice always makes it melt, so "If you heat ice, it melts" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Melted is the past simple. It does not state a general truth that is always true.", "1": "Would melt suggests an imaginary situation, as in the second conditional. A scientific fact uses the present simple.", "3": "Had melted is the past perfect. It does not fit a general truth."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0108' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some verbs must be followed by a gerund (-ing form), and enjoy is one of them. So the correct form is "She enjoys reading detective novels". Other such verbs include avoid, finish, mind and suggest.', wrong = '{"1": "Enjoy does not take a to-infinitive. \"She enjoys to read\" is a common error.", "2": "A bare verb cannot follow enjoy. \"She enjoys read\" is ungrammatical.", "3": "To reading is not used after enjoy. Enjoy takes the -ing form directly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0109' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Decide is one of the verbs followed by a to-infinitive. So the correct form is "He decided to study medicine". Other verbs like this include plan, hope, want and agree.', wrong = '{"0": "Decide does not take a gerund. \"He decided studying\" is ungrammatical.", "2": "Decide needs to before the next verb. A bare infinitive cannot follow it.", "3": "To studying wrongly mixes the infinitive marker to with an -ing form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0110' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In "look forward to", the word to is a preposition, not part of an infinitive. A verb after a preposition takes the gerund (-ing) form. So "I am looking forward to meeting you" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Meet is a bare verb. After the preposition to in \"look forward to\", a gerund is needed.", "1": "\"Look forward to to meet\" would double the to. The to in the phrase is a preposition and is followed by -ing.", "3": "Met is a past form. A verb after a preposition takes the -ing form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0111' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the active voice, "make + object" is followed by a bare infinitive, the base verb without to. So "My parents made me wash the dishes" is correct. In the passive, to returns: "I was made to wash the dishes".', wrong = '{"0": "\"Made me to wash\" is incorrect in the active voice. To is only used after make in the passive.", "1": "Make + object does not take a gerund. \"Made me washing\" is ungrammatical.", "2": "Washed is a past form. After \"made me\" the base verb is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0112' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Stop + gerund means to quit an activity, while stop + to-infinitive means to pause in order to do something. The doctor warned him about his lungs, so he quit the habit. "He stopped smoking" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "\"Stopped to smoke\" means he paused what he was doing in order to smoke, the opposite of what the doctor''s warning suggests.", "2": "A bare verb cannot follow stopped. \"He stopped smoke\" is ungrammatical.", "3": "Smoked is a past form. Stopped is followed by a gerund or a to-infinitive, not a past form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0113' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A gerund is a verb form ending in -ing that works as a noun. In the sentence, swimming is the subject: it is the thing that "is excellent exercise". So swimming is the gerund.', wrong = '{"0": "Is is the main verb of the sentence, a form of be. It is not an -ing form.", "2": "Excellent is an adjective describing exercise.", "3": "Exercise is an ordinary noun here, the complement after is. It is not an -ing verb form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0114' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"To learn English" explains why she came to Lahore. A word group that tells why an action happens modifies the verb, so it works as an adverb of purpose. A quick test: it answers the question "Why did she come?"', wrong = '{"0": "The subject is She, the person who did the action of coming.", "1": "The phrase does not describe Lahore. It gives the reason for coming.", "3": "The main verb is came. An infinitive phrase cannot be the main verb of a sentence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0115' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In "To err is human", the infinitive "To err" (to make mistakes) is what the sentence is about. It comes before the verb is and acts as its subject, so it works as a noun. A quick test: replace it with a noun, as in "Error is human".', wrong = '{"0": "An adverbial infinitive tells why or how an action happens. \"To err\" does not modify any verb here.", "1": "An adjectival infinitive describes a noun, as in \"work to do\". \"To err\" does not describe any noun.", "2": "An object comes after the verb and receives its action. \"To err\" comes before is and is the subject."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0116' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"To do" comes after the noun work and tells us which work, the work that needs doing. An infinitive that describes a noun works as an adjective. So "to do" is an adjective describing work.', wrong = '{"1": "An adverb of purpose explains why an action happens, as in \"She came to learn\". \"To do\" does not explain why anyone has the work.", "2": "The subject of the sentence is I, the person who has the work. \"To do\" is not the subject.", "3": "A gerund is an -ing form used as a noun. \"To do\" is an infinitive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0117' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Be used to" means to be accustomed to something, and its to is a preposition. A verb after a preposition takes the -ing form. So "She is used to getting up early" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Is used to get\" mixes up two patterns. \"Used to + base verb\" (She used to get up early) describes a past habit and has no be.", "2": "Got is a past form, which cannot follow the preposition to.", "3": "Gets is a present tense form with -s, which cannot follow to."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0118' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"It is no use" is a fixed expression that is followed by a gerund. It means there is no point in doing something. So "It is no use worrying about the result now" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The fixed expression is \"it is no use + -ing\". \"It is no use to worry\" is not standard.", "1": "A bare verb cannot follow \"no use\". \"It is no use worry\" is ungrammatical.", "3": "Worried is an adjective or past form. It does not fit after \"it is no use\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0119' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Let + object is always followed by a bare infinitive, the base verb without to. So "He let me use his car" is correct. The same pattern applies to make in the active voice.', wrong = '{"0": "Let never takes to before the next verb. \"He let me to use\" is a common error.", "1": "Let + object does not take a gerund. \"He let me using\" is ungrammatical.", "2": "Used is a past form. After \"let me\" the base verb use is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0120' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Remember + to-infinitive refers to a duty or task that still has to be done. Remember + gerund refers to recalling something that already happened. Locking the door is a task to do when leaving, so "Remember to lock the door" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "\"Remember locking\" means recalling an action already done, as in \"I remember locking the door last night\". It does not fit an instruction about the future.", "2": "A bare verb cannot follow remember. It needs to + verb or an -ing form.", "3": "Locked is a past form. It cannot follow remember in an instruction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0121' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A gerund phrase is an -ing verb with its object, working as a noun. In "She is good at solving puzzles", the phrase "solving puzzles" comes after the preposition at and acts as its object. Prepositions are always followed by a noun or a gerund.', wrong = '{"0": "\"To solve puzzles\" is an infinitive phrase, the object of wants. It is not a gerund and does not follow a preposition.", "2": "\"Is solving\" is a present continuous verb. The -ing form here is part of the verb, not a gerund used as a noun.", "3": "Solved is a past participle used as an adjective to describe puzzle. It is not a gerund."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0122' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Mind, including in the polite request "Would you mind...?", is followed by a gerund. So "Would you mind opening the window?" is correct. It politely asks someone to open it.', wrong = '{"0": "Mind does not take a to-infinitive. \"Would you mind to open\" is a common error.", "1": "A bare verb cannot follow mind. \"Would you mind open\" is ungrammatical.", "3": "Opened is a past form, which does not fit a polite request."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0123' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Avoid is one of the verbs that must be followed by a gerund (-ing form). So "She avoided answering the question" is correct. Other such verbs include enjoy, finish, mind and deny.', wrong = '{"0": "Avoid does not take a to-infinitive. \"She avoided to answer\" is a common error.", "1": "A bare verb cannot follow avoided. \"She avoided answer\" is ungrammatical, since answer would have to be read as a noun.", "2": "Answered is a past form. Avoid needs the -ing form after it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0124' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An adverb of manner tells how an action is done. Carefully answers the question "How did the surgeon stitch the wound?" So it is an adverb of manner. Many adverbs of manner end in -ly.', wrong = '{"1": "An adverb of place tells where something happens, such as here or outside. Carefully does not say where.", "2": "An adverb of time tells when something happens, such as now or yesterday. Carefully does not say when.", "3": "An adverb of frequency tells how often, such as always or rarely. Carefully does not say how often."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0125' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An adverb of time tells when an action happens. Tomorrow answers the question "When will we discuss the results?" So it is an adverb of time, like today, soon and later.', wrong = '{"0": "An adverb of manner tells how something is done, such as carefully. Tomorrow does not describe how.", "2": "An adverb of place tells where something happens, such as here. Tomorrow does not say where.", "3": "An adverb of frequency tells how often, such as always. Tomorrow refers to a single time, not repetition."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0126' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An adverb of frequency tells how often an action happens. Seldom means rarely, so it answers the question "How often does he eat fast food?" It is therefore an adverb of frequency, like always, often and never.', wrong = '{"0": "An adverb of manner tells how something is done, such as quickly. Seldom does not describe the way he eats.", "1": "An adverb of place tells where, such as there or nearby. Seldom says nothing about place.", "3": "An adverb of time tells when, such as today. Seldom tells how often, not when."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0127' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An adverb of degree tells how much or to what extent. Too modifies the adjective hot and shows the tea is hotter than is acceptable for drinking. So too is an adverb of degree, like very, quite and extremely.', wrong = '{"0": "An adverb of manner tells how an action is done. Too does not describe an action; it modifies the adjective hot.", "1": "An adverb of place tells where something happens. Too says nothing about place.", "2": "An adverb of time tells when something happens. Too says nothing about time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0128' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An adverb of place tells where an action happens. Outside answers the question "Where were the children playing?" So it is an adverb of place, like inside, here and nearby.', wrong = '{"1": "An adverb of manner tells how something is done, such as happily. Outside does not describe how they played.", "2": "An adverb of time tells when, such as yesterday. Outside does not say when.", "3": "An adverb of frequency tells how often, such as often. Outside does not say how often."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0129' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Adverbs of frequency such as never, always and often usually come after the verb be (am, is, are, was, were). So the correct order is "He is never late for class". With other main verbs they come before the verb.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Never he is late\" puts the adverb before the subject without inverting the verb. If never begins the sentence, inversion is needed (Never is he late), which sounds formal.", "2": "Never must come between is and late. Placing it after late breaks the normal order.", "3": "\"He never late is\" breaks the normal order of verb and complement. Is must come before late."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0130' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An adverb of frequency usually comes before the main verb, but after the verb be. Arrives is a main verb, so always goes before it. "She always arrives on time" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Always should come before a main verb like arrives, not after it.", "1": "\"She always on time arrives\" puts the verb at the end, which breaks normal English word order.", "3": "This order is broken: the adverb opens the sentence and the verb comes last."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0131' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When adverbs of manner, place and time come together after a verb, the usual order is manner, place, time. Beautifully (how), at the concert (where), last night (when). So "She sang beautifully at the concert last night" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "This puts time first. In the usual order, time comes last, after manner and place.", "1": "Place and time are in the right order, but manner (beautifully) should come first, not last.", "2": "This completely reverses the usual order, putting time first and manner last."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0132' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When enough modifies an adjective, it comes after the adjective. So the correct form is "old enough", followed by a to-infinitive: "She is old enough to drive". With a noun, enough comes before it, as in "enough money".', wrong = '{"1": "Enough comes after an adjective, not before it. \"Enough old\" is incorrect.", "2": "Enough must stay right after old. Moving it to the end breaks the phrase.", "3": "Enough cannot open this sentence. The word order is broken."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0133' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only limits the word or phrase it is placed next to. Placed before Ali, it means Ali and no one else solved the problem. So "Only Ali solved the problem" has the required meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "Here only sits next to the verb, so it limits the action: Ali merely solved it and did nothing more.", "2": "Here only limits \"the problem\": Ali solved the problem and nothing else.", "3": "Here only is an adjective before problem: there was just one problem, and Ali solved it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0134' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A word that describes how an action is done must be an adverb. Drives is a verb, so the adverb carefully is needed. "Very" then modifies carefully, giving "He drives very carefully in the rain".', wrong = '{"0": "Careful is an adjective. It describes nouns, as in a careful driver, but cannot describe the verb drives.", "1": "Care is a noun or verb. It cannot describe how someone drives.", "3": "Carefulness is a noun meaning the quality of being careful. It cannot modify a verb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0135' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hard can be an adjective or an adverb with the same form. As an adverb it means with great effort, and "she rarely takes a day off" shows she puts in great effort. So "She works hard" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Hardly means almost not, as in \"She hardly works\". This is the opposite of the meaning needed.", "1": "Hardy is an adjective meaning tough and able to survive difficult conditions. It cannot describe how she works.", "2": "Hardness is a noun. It cannot describe the verb works."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0136' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hardly means almost not, or only with great difficulty. The speaker asks the other person to speak louder, which shows they can barely hear. So "I can hardly hear you" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Hard as an adverb means with great effort. \"I can hard hear you\" is ungrammatical.", "2": "Hardest is a superlative form. It does not fit between can and hear.", "3": "Harder is a comparative form. \"I can harder hear you\" is ungrammatical."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0137' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An adverb of place tells where an action happens. Everywhere answers the question "where?", as in "I looked everywhere". So it is the adverb of place.', wrong = '{"0": "Yesterday tells when something happened, so it is an adverb of time.", "2": "Quickly tells how something is done, so it is an adverb of manner.", "3": "Often tells how many times something happens, so it is an adverb of frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0138' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The pattern "too + adjective + to-infinitive" means something is more than is acceptable, so the action cannot happen. "The tea is too hot to drink" means it is so hot that it cannot be drunk. This is the correct form.', wrong = '{"0": "Too much is used before uncountable nouns, as in \"too much sugar\". Before an adjective like hot, only too is used.", "1": "Very only makes hot stronger. It does not express that the heat prevents drinking, so \"very hot to drink\" is not standard.", "3": "So + adjective needs a that-clause: \"so hot that I cannot drink it\". \"So hot to drink\" is incorrect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0139' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Late can be an adjective or an adverb. In "He arrived late", it modifies the verb arrived and tells when he arrived in relation to the expected time. So here it is an adverb of time.', wrong = '{"0": "As an adjective, late describes a noun, as in \"a late train\". Here it describes the verb arrived.", "1": "A noun names a person, place or thing. Late does not name anything here.", "2": "A preposition links a noun to the rest of a sentence, such as for in \"for the viva\". Late does not do that."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0140' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At is used for a precise point in time, especially clock times. 9 a.m. is an exact clock time, so "starts at 9 a.m." is correct. A memory hook: at for clock times, on for days, in for months and years.', wrong = '{"0": "In is used for longer periods such as months, years and parts of the day (in the morning), not exact clock times.", "1": "On is used for days and dates, such as on Monday or on 5 May.", "2": "By means not later than. \"Starts by 9 a.m.\" would change the meaning to a deadline."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0141' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In is used with years, months, seasons and other long periods, as in in May or in winter. 2008 is a year, so "She was born in 2008" is correct. A memory hook: at for clock times, on for days and dates, in for months and years.', wrong = '{"1": "On is used for days and dates, as in \"born on 3 March 2008\". A year on its own takes in.", "2": "At is used for exact clock times, as in at 5 p.m. It does not go with years.", "3": "Since marks a starting point and is used with perfect tenses for continuing actions. Being born is a single past event."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0142' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On is used with days of the week and with dates, as in on Friday or on 14 August. Monday is a day, so "We will meet on Monday" is correct. Longer periods take in, and clock times take at.', wrong = '{"0": "In is used for longer periods such as months, years and seasons, not for a single day.", "2": "At is used for clock times, such as at noon or at 4 p.m.", "3": "For shows how long something lasts, as in \"for two hours\". It does not name a day."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0143' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Into shows movement from outside to inside a place. She walked from outside the room to inside it and then sat down. So "She walked into the room" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "At shows position at a point, as in \"at the door\". It does not show movement into a place.", "1": "On shows position on a surface, as in \"on the table\". It does not show entering a room.", "3": "Of shows belonging or connection, as in \"the door of the room\". It does not show movement."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0144' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Since is used with a starting point in time, together with a perfect tense. Monday is the point when the illness began, and "has been" is the present perfect. So "He has been ill since Monday" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "For is used with a length of time, as in \"for three days\". Monday is a point in time, not a period.", "1": "At is used for clock times, such as at 6 o''clock. It does not show a starting point.", "2": "By means not later than. It does not fit with the present perfect to show a continuing illness."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0145' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For is used with a length or period of time. Three hours is a duration, so "She has been revising for three hours" is correct. A quick check: for + how long, since + from when.', wrong = '{"1": "Since needs a starting point, such as \"since 6 o''clock\". Three hours is a duration, not a starting point.", "2": "From marks a start and normally needs a matching to or until, as in \"from 6 to 9\".", "3": "At is used for a point of time, such as at noon. It cannot express a duration."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0146' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Across means from one side of something to the other side. The swimmers went from one bank of the river to the other bank. So "We swam across the river" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Along means following the length of something, such as swimming down the river. It would not take them to the other bank.", "2": "Among is used for being surrounded by a group of people or things, as in \"among friends\".", "3": "Between is used with two named things, as in \"between the two banks\". Here only the river is named."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0147' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Among is used when something is shared out within a group of more than two. All the members of a team form such a group. So "divided among all the members" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Between is traditionally used for two people or things, or for clearly separate individuals. \"All the members\" is treated as a group, which takes among.", "1": "Within means inside certain limits, as in \"within the hospital\". It does not express sharing.", "3": "Along shows movement or position following a line, as in \"along the road\". It does not express sharing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0148' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Between is used to describe a position in the middle of two named things. The mosque is in the middle of the bank and the post office. So "stands between the bank and the post office" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Among is used for a group of more than two things. Here only two buildings are named.", "1": "Across means on the other side of something, as in \"across the road\". It does not mean in the middle of two things.", "2": "\"Beside of\" is not standard English. The correct form is beside, meaning next to."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0149' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With arrive, English uses in for large places such as cities and countries, and at for smaller places such as a station or airport. Karachi is a city, so "The train arrived in Karachi" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "At is used with smaller places, as in \"arrived at the station\". With a city name, in is normal.", "2": "Arrive is never followed by to. \"Arrived to Karachi\" is a common error.", "3": "On is used for surfaces or days, such as on the platform or on Friday. It does not go with a city."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0150' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By is used to name a means of transport when there is no article or possessive before the noun: by bus, by train, by car, by air. "We travelled to Murree by bus" tells how they travelled. So by is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "On is used for buses and trains only with an article, as in \"on the bus\". With bus alone, by is needed.", "2": "With is not used to name a means of transport. \"Travelled with bus\" is a common error.", "3": "In is used with cars and taxis and needs an article, as in \"in a car\". It does not fit \"bus\" alone."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0151' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By means not later than, and it is used for a deadline for a single action. Submitting a report is one action that must be done no later than Friday. So "I will submit the report by Friday" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Until is used for an action that continues up to a time, as in \"I will work until Friday\". Submitting is a single act, not a continuing one.", "1": "Since needs a starting point and a perfect tense, as in \"since Monday\". It does not express a future deadline.", "3": "During is used with a period, as in \"during the week\". It does not express a deadline."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0152' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A period with a clear start and end is expressed with "from ... to" (or "from ... until"). The OPD opens at 8 a.m. and closes at 2 p.m. So "open from 8 a.m. to 2 p.m." is correct. "Between ... and" would also work, but not "between ... to".', wrong = '{"0": "Since marks a starting point up to now and is used with perfect tenses. It cannot be paired with to.", "1": "Between must be paired with and, as in \"between 8 a.m. and 2 p.m.\". \"Between ... to\" is incorrect.", "2": "\"At 8 a.m. from 2 p.m.\" makes no sense. It does not show a time range with a start and an end."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0153' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Off shows movement away from a surface. The cat was on the table and jumped down away from it. So "The cat jumped off the table" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Of shows belonging or connection, as in \"the leg of the table\". It does not show movement.", "2": "In shows being inside something. A cat cannot jump inside a table.", "3": "At shows a point or direction, as in \"jumped at the mouse\". It does not show leaving a surface."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0154' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Above means at a higher level than something. If the passengers could see clear blue sky, nothing was blocking the view upward, so the plane must have been higher than the clouds. So "flew above the clouds" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Below the clouds, the clouds would hide the sky, so passengers would not see clear blue sky.", "2": "Among the clouds, the view would be grey and cloudy, not clear blue sky.", "3": "Beneath means below, which would hide the blue sky from view."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0155' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Along means following the length of something such as a road, river or wall. He walked beside the river, following its bank, for an hour. So "walked along the bank" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Across means from one side to the other. Walking across a river bank for an hour does not make sense.", "1": "Into means entering an enclosed space. One does not walk into a river bank.", "3": "Among is used for being in the middle of a group of things, such as trees or people."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0156' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"At the end of" is the fixed expression for the place where something such as a road finishes. So "The hospital is at the end of this road" is correct. At is used for a point or position.', wrong = '{"0": "\"In the end\" is a time expression meaning finally. It does not describe a location on a road.", "1": "\"On the end\" is not the standard expression for the far point of a road.", "2": "For shows purpose or duration, not position. It cannot describe where the hospital is."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0157' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When good means skilled, it is followed by at: good at maths, good at cricket. So "She is good at mathematics" is correct. The same pattern applies to bad at and better at.', wrong = '{"1": "\"Good in\" is not the standard pairing for being skilled at a subject.", "2": "\"Good on\" is not used to express skill in a subject.", "3": "\"Good with\" is used for people, animals or tools, as in \"good with children\". It is not used for school subjects."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0158' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The verb insist is followed by the preposition on, and a verb after on takes the -ing form. So "He insisted on paying the bill" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Insist for\" is incorrect. The fixed pattern is insist on.", "2": "\"Insist to\" is a common error. Insist takes on, not to.", "3": "\"Insist at\" is incorrect. Insist always takes on."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0159' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The verb prefer uses the pattern "prefer A to B". So "She prefers tea to coffee" is correct. With infinitives the pattern is "would rather ... than" or "prefer to do X rather than Y".', wrong = '{"0": "\"Prefer ... than\" is a common error. Than is used after comparatives such as better, not after prefer.", "1": "From is not used with prefer. \"Prefer tea from coffee\" is incorrect.", "3": "\"Over than\" is not English. \"Prefer tea over coffee\" is informal, but \"over than\" is always wrong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0160' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The fixed pattern is "accuse someone of something". In the passive this becomes "was accused of". So "The suspect was accused of theft" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Accused for\" is incorrect. Accuse always takes of before the crime.", "1": "\"Accused with\" is incorrect. With goes with charge, as in \"charged with theft\".", "2": "\"Accused about\" is incorrect. The fixed preposition is of."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0161' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The verb suffer is followed by from when naming an illness, pain or condition. So "The patient is suffering from a high fever" is correct. This is the standard form expected in tests.', wrong = '{"1": "\"Suffer with\" is heard in informal speech, but \"suffer from\" is the standard form for an illness.", "2": "\"Suffer of\" is incorrect. The verb suffer takes from when naming an illness.", "3": "By shows the doer in passive sentences. It is not used after suffer to name an illness."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0162' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The two ideas contrast: he studied hard, yet he failed. A contrast transition such as however is needed to show this unexpected result. So "He studied hard for months; however, he failed the test" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Therefore shows a result. Failing is not a logical result of studying hard.", "2": "Moreover adds a similar, supporting point. Failing is not an extra point in the same direction.", "3": "\"For example\" introduces an example. Failing the test is not an example of studying hard."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0163' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heavy rain caused the match to be cancelled. A result transition such as consequently (as a result) is needed to link a cause to its effect. So "It rained heavily all morning; consequently, the match was cancelled" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "However signals contrast. The cancellation is an expected result of rain, not a contrast.", "1": "Nevertheless means in spite of that, which signals contrast. There is no contrast here.", "3": "\"For instance\" introduces an example. The cancellation is a result, not an example of rain."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0164' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first sentence names one benefit of exercise and the second adds another benefit. An addition transition such as moreover (also, in addition) is needed. So "Moreover, it reduces stress" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "However signals contrast. Reducing stress agrees with strengthening the heart, since both are benefits.", "1": "\"On the contrary\" rejects the previous statement. The second sentence supports it.", "2": "Otherwise means if not. There is no condition between the two sentences."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0165' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first part makes a general statement, that many vegetables are rich in iron. Spinach is one particular case of this. A transition that introduces an example, "for instance", is needed.', wrong = '{"1": "However signals contrast. Spinach being rich in iron agrees with the first statement.", "2": "Therefore shows a result. Spinach is not a result of vegetables being rich in iron.", "3": "Otherwise means if not, which signals a condition. There is no condition here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0166' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Being exhausted would normally make a person stop, but he kept working, which is a contrast. Although introduces a clause (with a subject and verb) that contrasts with the main clause. So "Although he was exhausted, he kept working" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Because gives a reason. Being exhausted is not a reason to keep working.", "2": "Despite shows contrast but must be followed by a noun or -ing form, as in \"Despite being exhausted\". \"He was exhausted\" is a full clause.", "3": "Unless means if not. \"Unless he was exhausted, he kept working\" does not make sense."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0167' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Despite shows contrast and is followed by a noun or noun phrase, not a full clause. "The heavy traffic" is a noun phrase, and reaching on time contrasts with heavy traffic. So "Despite the heavy traffic" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Although must be followed by a clause with a subject and verb, as in \"Although the traffic was heavy\".", "1": "Even though also needs a full clause with a verb. \"Even though the heavy traffic\" is incomplete.", "3": "Because gives a reason. Heavy traffic would not be a reason for arriving on time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0168' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Otherwise means "if not" or "if you do not". The sentence warns that if you do not take an umbrella, you will get soaked. So otherwise is the correct transition.', wrong = '{"0": "Moreover adds another point in the same direction. Getting soaked is not an extra reason given in addition.", "1": "Similarly shows a likeness between two ideas. There is no comparison here.", "2": "Consequently shows a result. Getting soaked is not a result of taking an umbrella."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0169' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage gives steps in order, marked by First and Finally. The middle step needs a sequence word such as next or then. So "Next, apply an antiseptic" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "However signals contrast. The steps follow each other in order with no contrast.", "2": "\"In conclusion\" is used to sum up at the end. It cannot mark a middle step.", "3": "\"On the other hand\" introduces a contrasting point. There is no contrast between the steps."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0170' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The sentence sums up the main points about good health: diet, exercise and sleep. A concluding transition such as "in conclusion" introduces a summary like this. So "In conclusion" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"For example\" introduces a specific case of a general idea. This sentence is a summary, not an example.", "2": "Nevertheless signals contrast. The sentence does not go against anything earlier.", "3": "\"In contrast\" shows a difference between two things. There is no contrast in the sentence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0171' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ali talks a lot, while his brother hardly speaks, so the two brothers are being contrasted. "On the other hand" introduces the contrasting side. So "his brother, on the other hand, hardly says a word" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Similarly shows a likeness. The brothers behave in opposite ways.", "1": "Therefore shows a result. The brother''s silence is not caused by Ali''s talking.", "3": "\"As a result\" also shows cause and effect. The brother''s silence is not a result of Ali''s talkativeness."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0172' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sana finds chemistry fascinating, and her twin also loves it. The second sentence shows a similarity, so a comparison transition such as likewise (in the same way) is needed.', wrong = '{"0": "\"In contrast\" shows a difference. Both sisters like chemistry, so there is no contrast.", "1": "Otherwise means if not. There is no condition linking the two sentences.", "2": "Nevertheless means in spite of that, signalling contrast. The sentences agree."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0173' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Provided that" means on condition that, or only if. Passing depends on the condition of revising regularly. So "You will pass provided that you revise regularly" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Unless means if not. \"You will pass unless you revise regularly\" would mean revising causes failure.", "2": "Although shows contrast. Revising regularly does not contrast with passing.", "3": "Whereas compares two different facts, as in \"She likes tea, whereas he likes coffee\". It does not express a condition."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0174' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Unless means "if ... not". "Unless you leave now, you will miss the flight" means if you do not leave now, you will miss it. This is the only option that makes sense.', wrong = '{"0": "\"If you leave now, you will miss the flight\" makes no sense. Leaving now is what prevents missing it.", "2": "Because gives a reason. Leaving now would not cause someone to miss the flight.", "3": "Since here would mean because. Leaving now would not cause missing the flight."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0175' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Waking up late is the reason he missed the bus. Because introduces a reason or cause. So "He missed the bus because he woke up late" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "So introduces a result. \"He missed the bus so he woke up late\" reverses cause and effect.", "1": "Although shows contrast. Waking up late and missing the bus do not contrast; one causes the other.", "3": "Unless means if not. \"He missed the bus unless he woke up late\" makes no sense."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0176' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Not only" forms a fixed correlative pair with "but also". When a sentence begins with "Not only", the verb is inverted (did he finish), and the second part begins with "but ... also". So "Not only did he finish first, but he also broke the record" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Not only ... and also\" is incorrect. The fixed pair is \"not only ... but also\".", "1": "Or offers a choice between alternatives. The sentence adds a second achievement.", "2": "\"Not only ... yet also\" is incorrect. Yet signals contrast and is not part of this pair."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0177' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nevertheless means in spite of that, so it introduces an idea that contrasts with what came before. For example: "The test was hard; nevertheless, she passed." It is the contrast transition.', wrong = '{"1": "Furthermore adds more information in the same direction. It shows addition, not contrast.", "2": "Consequently shows a result or effect of what came before.", "3": "\"For instance\" introduces an example of a general idea."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0178' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'It''s is the contraction of "it is" or "it has". The apostrophe shows where the letter i has been left out. Since the sentence means "It is a beautiful day", "It''s a beautiful day" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Its without an apostrophe is the possessive form, as in \"the dog wagged its tail\". It cannot mean \"it is\".", "2": "Its'' is not an English word. Neither the contraction nor the possessive uses this form.", "3": "A comma cannot replace an apostrophe. \"It,s\" is a punctuation error."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0179' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To show possession with a plural noun that already ends in -s, add only an apostrophe after the s. There are two doctors, so the plural is doctors and the possessive is doctors''. "The two doctors'' reports were identical" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Doctor''s is the singular possessive, meaning one doctor. The sentence says there are two doctors.", "1": "The apostrophe belongs on the owner, doctors, not on the thing owned. \"Reports''\" would make reports the owner.", "3": "A plural noun ending in -s takes only an apostrophe. \"Doctors''s\" adds an extra s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0180' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Children is already plural but does not end in -s. Plurals that do not end in -s form the possessive by adding ''s, just like singular nouns. So the correct form is children''s: "The children''s ward is on the second floor."', wrong = '{"0": "Childrens is not a word, because children is already plural. The apostrophe is also in the wrong place.", "1": "The possessive apostrophe is missing. The ward belongs to the children, so it needs children''s.", "2": "A plural that does not end in -s needs ''s, not just an apostrophe. \"Children''\" is incorrect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0181' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A transition word such as however, moreover or therefore at the start of a sentence is followed by a comma. The comma separates it from the main clause. So "However, the results were not significant." is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "This leaves out the comma after However and puts one between the subject (the results) and its verb (were), which is not allowed.", "2": "This puts a comma between were and its complement (not significant), which breaks the clause, and still has no comma after However.", "3": "A semicolon joins two independent clauses. It is not used after an introductory word like However."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0182' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"She asked me where I lived" is an indirect, or reported, question. It reports a question but is itself a statement, so it ends with a full stop. It also keeps normal statement word order: subject then verb (I lived).', wrong = '{"0": "An indirect question is a statement, so it does not take a question mark.", "2": "No comma is needed before the reported question, and it should not end with a question mark.", "3": "Reported questions use statement word order. \"Where did I live\" is direct question word order."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0183' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A colon is used after a complete clause to introduce a list or an explanation. "You will need three things" is a complete clause, and the list follows it. So the colon goes after "three things".', wrong = '{"0": "A semicolon joins two independent clauses. It does not introduce a list.", "1": "\"You will need\" is not a complete idea, so a colon should not come straight after need. The colon belongs after \"three things\".", "3": "Semicolons are only used between list items that already contain commas. Here the simple items need commas, and a colon should introduce the list."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0184' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"I wanted to go" and "it was raining" are both independent clauses. When they are joined by a transition word like however, a semicolon goes before it and a comma after it. So "I wanted to go; however, it was raining." is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Using only commas to join two independent clauses creates a comma splice. A semicolon is needed before however.", "1": "The semicolon must come before however, not after it. Here however is wrongly attached to the first clause.", "2": "The comma after it splits the subject from its verb (was), and there is no comma after however."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0185' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a quoted statement comes before the reporting clause, it ends with a comma, not a full stop. The comma goes inside the closing quotation mark. The whole sentence then ends with a full stop: "Close the door," said the teacher.', wrong = '{"1": "A comma is needed at the end of the quoted words to separate them from \"said the teacher\".", "2": "A full stop ends a sentence, but the sentence continues with \"said the teacher\". A comma is used instead.", "3": "The comma should be inside the closing quotation mark, and the sentence must end with a full stop, not a comma."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0186' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A sentence that begins with "What a ..." or "How ..." and expresses strong feeling is an exclamation. Exclamatory sentences end with an exclamation mark. So "What a brilliant idea!" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "This sentence expresses admiration; it does not ask for information. It is an exclamation, not a question.", "2": "A full stop is used for ordinary statements. \"What a ...\" shows strong feeling and needs an exclamation mark.", "3": "A sentence can never end with a comma. It needs a closing mark, here an exclamation mark."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0187' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"When the bell rang" is an introductory adverb clause: it has its own subject and verb but cannot stand alone. When such a clause comes before the main clause, a comma separates them. So "When the bell rang, the students left the hall." is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The comma here comes between the subject (the students) and its verb (left), which is not allowed.", "1": "A comma cannot come straight after When, because it splits the clause it begins.", "3": "A semicolon joins two independent clauses. \"When the bell rang\" is dependent, so a comma is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0188' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A person has only one mother, so "who is a doctor" does not identify which mother is meant. It just adds extra information, so it is a non-defining clause. Non-defining clauses are set off by a pair of commas, one before and one after.', wrong = '{"0": "Without commas the clause looks defining, as if the speaker had several mothers and is saying which one.", "1": "The opening comma is there, but the closing comma after doctor is missing.", "2": "The closing comma is there, but the opening comma after mother is missing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0189' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Proper nouns take capital letters. These include the names of buildings (Badshahi Mosque), cities (Lahore) and days of the week (Friday). The first word of a sentence also takes a capital, so only the first sentence is fully correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Badshahi Mosque, Lahore and Friday are all proper nouns and need capital letters.", "2": "Mosque is part of the building''s name and Friday is a day of the week, so both need capitals.", "3": "The first word of a sentence must start with a capital letter, so \"we\" should be \"We\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0190' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a contraction, two words are joined and some letters are dropped. An apostrophe marks the place of the missing letters: do not becomes don''t, with the apostrophe standing for the o of not. So the mark is the apostrophe.', wrong = '{"0": "A comma separates parts of a sentence, such as items in a list. It does not show missing letters.", "2": "A hyphen joins words or parts of words, as in well-known. It does not show missing letters.", "3": "A colon introduces a list, an explanation or a quotation. It does not show missing letters."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0191' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When two words act together as one adjective before a noun, they are joined with a hyphen. Well and known together describe the author, so they become well-known. "She is a well-known author" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The hyphen belongs between well and known, the two parts of the adjective, not between known and author.", "1": "A comma cannot split the two parts of a compound adjective.", "3": "Author is the noun being described, not part of the compound adjective, so it should not be hyphenated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0192' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Titles such as Dr., personal names such as Ayesha, and city names such as Islamabad all start with capital letters. Abbreviations of organisations made from first letters, like WHO (World Health Organization), are written in full capitals. The first sentence follows all these rules.', wrong = '{"1": "The title Dr., the abbreviation WHO and the city name Islamabad all need capital letters.", "2": "Ayesha is a personal name and needs a capital, and WHO must be written in full capitals.", "3": "W.h.o is wrongly capitalised (it should be WHO), and islamabad needs a capital letter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0193' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"With a red cap" has no subject and finite verb, so it is a phrase, not a clause. It describes the noun boy, telling us which boy is meant. A phrase that describes a noun is an adjective phrase.', wrong = '{"0": "An adverb phrase modifies a verb, adjective or adverb. This phrase describes the noun boy.", "1": "A noun clause has a subject and finite verb and acts as a noun. \"With a red cap\" has no verb.", "2": "An adverb clause has a subject and finite verb. \"With a red cap\" has no verb, so it is not a clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0194' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Before sunrise" has no subject and finite verb, so it is a phrase. It tells when she left, so it modifies the verb left. A phrase that modifies a verb is an adverb phrase.', wrong = '{"1": "An adjective phrase describes a noun. \"Before sunrise\" describes when the action happened, not a noun.", "2": "A noun clause needs a subject and finite verb. \"Before sunrise\" has neither.", "3": "An adjective clause needs a subject and finite verb, usually after who, which or that. \"Before sunrise\" has no verb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0195' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"That he is honest" has its own subject (he) and finite verb (is), so it is a clause. It answers the question "What do I know?" and acts as the object of know. A clause that acts as a noun is a noun clause.', wrong = '{"0": "An adjective clause describes a noun, such as \"the man who helped us\". This clause is the object of know, not a description.", "2": "An adverb clause tells how, when, where or why. This clause tells what I know.", "3": "A phrase has no subject and finite verb. This group of words contains \"he is\", so it is a clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0196' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Who helped us" has a subject (who) and finite verb (helped), so it is a clause. It describes the noun man, telling us which man is meant. A relative clause like this is an adjective clause.', wrong = '{"0": "A noun clause works as a subject or object, such as \"I know who helped us\". Here the clause describes man.", "1": "An adverb clause modifies a verb, telling how, when or why. This clause describes a noun.", "3": "An adverb phrase has no finite verb. \"Who helped us\" has the finite verb helped, so it is a clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0197' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Because it was raining" has a subject (it) and finite verb (was raining), so it is a clause. It tells why we stayed indoors, modifying the verb stayed. A clause that gives a reason is an adverb clause of reason.', wrong = '{"0": "An adjective clause describes a noun. This clause explains why, not which one.", "1": "A noun clause acts as a subject or object. This clause gives a reason for the action.", "2": "A prepositional phrase has no finite verb, such as \"because of the rain\". This group has \"it was raining\", so it is a clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0198' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A clause is a group of words that contains its own subject and a finite verb, such as "when the bell rang". A phrase, by contrast, has no subject-verb pair, such as "before sunrise". So the first description is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "A group of words without a subject or finite verb is a phrase, not a clause.", "2": "A single word that names a person is a noun. A clause is a group of words.", "3": "A paragraph is made of several sentences on one idea. A clause is a part of a sentence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0199' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"I called him twice" and "he did not answer" can each stand alone as sentences, so they are independent clauses. They are joined by the coordinating conjunction but. Two or more independent clauses joined this way make a compound sentence.', wrong = '{"0": "A simple sentence has only one independent clause. This sentence has two.", "2": "A complex sentence has one independent clause and at least one dependent clause. Both clauses here are independent.", "3": "A compound-complex sentence needs at least two independent clauses and one dependent clause. There is no dependent clause here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0200' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"He attended the class" is an independent clause. "Although he was ill" has a subject and verb but cannot stand alone, so it is a dependent clause. One independent clause plus one dependent clause makes a complex sentence.', wrong = '{"0": "A simple sentence has only one clause. This sentence has two.", "1": "A compound sentence joins two independent clauses with words like and or but. Here one clause is dependent.", "3": "A compound-complex sentence needs two independent clauses plus a dependent one. There is only one independent clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0201' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A simple sentence has only one independent clause and no dependent clause. "The tired doctor went home after the night shift" has only one subject-verb pair (doctor went). "Tired" and "after the night shift" are just a word and a phrase, not clauses.', wrong = '{"0": "Two independent clauses, \"The doctor was tired\" and \"he went home\", are joined by so, making a compound sentence.", "1": "\"Because he was tired\" is a dependent clause, so the sentence is complex.", "2": "\"When his shift ended\" is a dependent clause, so the sentence is complex."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0202' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By function, sentences can make statements, ask questions, give commands or show strong feeling. "Switch off the lights before you leave" gives an instruction, with the subject "you" understood. A sentence that gives a command or request is imperative.', wrong = '{"1": "A declarative sentence makes a statement, such as \"The lights are off\". This sentence tells someone what to do.", "2": "An interrogative sentence asks a question and ends with a question mark.", "3": "An exclamatory sentence expresses strong feeling, such as \"What a bright light!\". This one gives an instruction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0203' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An -ing phrase at the start of a sentence must describe the subject that comes right after the comma. In "Walking to college, I saw an accident", the subject is I, and I was the one walking. So the modifier is placed correctly.', wrong = '{"0": "The subject after the comma is \"an accident\", which suggests the accident was walking to college. This is a dangling modifier.", "2": "The subject is \"the accident\", which again suggests the accident was walking to college.", "3": "The phrase is misplaced in the middle, and the word order breaks the sentence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0204' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a negative adverb such as never, seldom or rarely begins a sentence, the auxiliary verb comes before the subject, as in a question. This is called inversion. So "Never have I seen such a beautiful sunset" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"I have seen\" uses normal word order. After a fronted Never, the auxiliary have must come before I.", "1": "\"I saw\" uses normal word order. Inversion needs an auxiliary before the subject, as in \"Never did I see\".", "3": "\"Seen I have\" puts the participle first. The correct order is auxiliary, subject, participle: have I seen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0205' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When hardly opens a sentence, the following clause is inverted, usually with the past perfect: "Hardly had we left". The second event is introduced by when. So "Hardly had we left the hospital when it started to rain" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"We had left\" uses normal word order. After a fronted Hardly, had must come before we.", "1": "\"We left\" has normal word order and no auxiliary. Inversion requires an auxiliary before the subject.", "2": "Did must be followed by the base form (leave, not left). In any case, this pattern uses had with the past participle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0206' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"No sooner" forms a fixed pair with than. The pattern is "No sooner had + subject + past participle ... than ...". So "No sooner had the teacher entered than the students stood up" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "When pairs with Hardly or Scarcely, not with No sooner. \"No sooner ... when\" is a common error.", "2": "Then is an adverb of time. It is not part of the \"No sooner ... than\" pattern.", "3": "That does not complete this structure. The pair is always \"No sooner ... than\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0207' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When "Not only" begins a clause, the auxiliary comes before the subject. In the simple past, the auxiliary did is added: "did she pass". The second part then uses "but ... also", so "Not only did she pass the exam, but she also topped the province" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"She passed\" uses normal word order. Inversion is needed after a fronted \"Not only\".", "2": "\"She did pass\" puts the subject before the auxiliary. The auxiliary must come first: did she pass.", "3": "In modern English, main verbs like passed are not inverted with the subject. Did is used instead."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0208' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When rarely, a negative adverb, opens a sentence, the auxiliary comes before the subject. In the simple present the auxiliary is do or does. So "Rarely do we see such dedication" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"We see\" uses normal word order. Inversion is needed after a fronted Rarely.", "1": "\"We do see\" puts the subject before the auxiliary. The auxiliary must come first: do we see.", "3": "Main verbs like see are not inverted in modern English. The auxiliary do is used: do we see."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0209' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a sentence begins with "Only after ...", "Only when ..." or "Only then", the main clause that follows is inverted. In the simple past, the auxiliary did comes before the subject. So "did they realise" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"They realised\" uses normal word order. The main clause after \"Only after ...\" needs inversion.", "1": "\"They did realise\" puts the subject first. The auxiliary must come before the subject: did they realise.", "2": "Main verbs like realised are not inverted in modern English. Did is used instead."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0210' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In formal English, the if of a third conditional can be dropped by putting had before the subject. "Had I known about the test" means "If I had known about the test". The sentence talks about an imagined past: I did not know, so I did not prepare.', wrong = '{"1": "\"When I knew ... I prepared\" says I did know and did prepare. The original means the opposite: I did not know and did not prepare.", "2": "\"Because I knew\" states that I knew about the test. The original sentence implies that I did not know.", "3": "\"If I know ... I will prepare\" is a first conditional about a real future possibility. The original is about an unreal past situation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0211' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Starting a sentence with should before the subject is a formal way of saying if. "Should you need any help" means "If you need any help". So the sentence means: if you need any help, call the ward nurse.', wrong = '{"0": "Should here does not express obligation. It simply replaces if, so the sentence does not tell anyone that they ought to need help.", "2": "The instruction depends on a condition: call the nurse only if you need help, not whether or not you do.", "3": "The roles are reversed. The sentence tells you to call the nurse, not that the nurse will call you."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0212' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A sentence fragment is a group of words punctuated as a sentence but not expressing a complete thought. "Because the lab was closed on Sunday" begins with because, so it is a dependent clause. On its own it leaves the reader asking "What happened because of that?"', wrong = '{"0": "This has a subject (the lab) and verb (was closed) and expresses a complete thought, so it is a full sentence.", "1": "The dependent clause \"because the lab was closed\" is attached to the main clause \"We could not work\", so the sentence is complete.", "3": "This is an imperative sentence. Its subject, you, is understood, so it is complete."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0213' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"After the results were announced" is a dependent clause and cannot stand alone. The fix is to join it to the main clause "the students celebrated". When the dependent clause comes first, a comma separates the two parts.', wrong = '{"0": "A semicolon only joins two independent clauses. It cannot join a dependent clause to a main clause.", "1": "The first part is still cut off by a full stop, so it remains a fragment. Starting the next sentence with And does not fix it.", "2": "The comma after After breaks the dependent clause apart, and there is no comma between the two clauses."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0214' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The active sentence is in the simple past (cleaned). In the passive, the simple past becomes was/were + past participle. The object, the wound, becomes the subject: "The wound was cleaned by the nurse."', wrong = '{"0": "\"Is cleaned\" is the simple present passive. The original verb is in the past.", "2": "\"Has been cleaned\" is the present perfect passive. The original is simple past.", "3": "\"Was being cleaned\" is the past continuous passive, which would come from \"was cleaning\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0215' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The active sentence is in the present continuous (are building). In the passive, this becomes is/are + being + past participle. The object, a new hospital, becomes the subject: "A new hospital is being built." The doer "by them" is dropped because it is general.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Is built\" is the simple present passive. It loses the idea that building is in progress now.", "1": "\"Was being built\" is the past continuous passive. The original is in the present.", "3": "\"Has been built\" is the present perfect passive, meaning the building is finished. The original says it is still going on."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0216' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The active sentence is in the present perfect (has written). In the passive, this becomes has/have + been + past participle. The new subject "three books" is plural, so have is needed: "Three books have been written by her."', wrong = '{"0": "Has is singular, but the new subject \"three books\" is plural, so it needs have.", "1": "\"Were written\" is the simple past passive. The original is present perfect.", "2": "\"Had been written\" is the past perfect passive, which would come from \"had written\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0217' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the active question begins with who as the subject, the passive question begins with "By whom". The simple past becomes was/were + past participle, with the auxiliary before the subject. So "Who broke the window?" becomes "By whom was the window broken?"', wrong = '{"1": "\"Who was broken by the window?\" changes the meaning completely, making the window the doer.", "2": "A question needs the auxiliary was before the subject. \"The window was broken\" keeps statement order.", "3": "\"Whom did the window break?\" is not passive, and it turns the window into the doer of the action."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0218' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An imperative (a command) is made passive with the pattern "Let + object + be + past participle". The object of "Open the door" is the door. So the passive is "Let the door be opened."', wrong = '{"0": "\"The door is opened\" is a statement of fact, not a command.", "2": "\"The door was opened\" is a statement about the past, not a command.", "3": "\"The door has been opened\" is a present perfect statement, not a command."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0219' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The active sentence is in the simple future (will arrest). In the passive, will + verb becomes will + be + past participle. So "The thief will be arrested by the police."', wrong = '{"0": "Would changes the meaning to a conditional or a reported future. The original uses will.", "1": "\"Will arrest\" is still active and makes the thief the one doing the arresting. The passive needs be + past participle.", "3": "\"Will have been arrested\" is the future perfect passive, which would come from \"will have arrested\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0220' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The active sentence is in the past perfect (had examined). In the passive, this becomes had + been + past participle. The object, the patient, becomes the subject: "The patient had been examined by the doctor."', wrong = '{"0": "\"Has been examined\" is the present perfect passive. The original is past perfect.", "1": "\"Was examined\" is the simple past passive. It loses the had of the past perfect.", "2": "\"The patient had examined the doctor\" is still active and reverses who examined whom."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0221' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The active sentence is in the simple present (speak). In the passive, this becomes is/are + past participle. The doer "by people" is usually left out because it is general and adds nothing, giving "English is spoken all over the world."', wrong = '{"1": "\"Was spoken\" is the simple past passive. The original is in the present.", "2": "\"Is speaking\" is the active present continuous. It would mean English itself is doing the speaking.", "3": "\"Has spoken\" is the active present perfect, not passive. It also makes English the speaker."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0222' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The active question is in the simple past (Did he complete). In the passive, a simple past question becomes Was/Were + subject + past participle. The object, the task, becomes the subject: "Was the task completed by him?"', wrong = '{"0": "The passive does not use did. It needs was + past participle, and \"Did the task complete\" makes the task the doer.", "2": "\"Has the task been completed\" is the present perfect passive. The original is simple past.", "3": "\"Was the task being completed\" is the past continuous passive, which would come from \"Was he completing\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0223' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With a modal such as must, can or should, the passive is formed with modal + be + past participle. The object, the rules, becomes the subject. So "We must obey the rules" becomes "The rules must be obeyed."', wrong = '{"0": "\"The rules must obey\" makes the rules the doers, which changes the meaning, and it is not passive.", "1": "\"Must have obeyed\" is an active form about the past. The passive needs be + past participle.", "3": "After a modal the base form be is needed. \"Must been\" is ungrammatical."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0224' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The active sentence is in the past continuous (was chasing). In the passive, this becomes was/were + being + past participle. So "The mouse was being chased by the cat."', wrong = '{"0": "\"Was chased\" is the simple past passive. It drops the idea that the action was in progress.", "1": "\"Is being chased\" is the present continuous passive. The original is in the past.", "2": "\"Had been chased\" is the past perfect passive, which would come from \"had chased\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0225' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The active sentence is in the present perfect (has stolen). In the passive, this becomes has/have + been + past participle. The doer "by somebody" is dropped because it is unknown, giving "My bag has been stolen."', wrong = '{"1": "\"Had been stolen\" is the past perfect passive. The original is present perfect.", "2": "\"Was being stolen\" is the past continuous passive, which describes an action in progress.", "3": "\"My bag has stolen\" is still active and would mean the bag stole something."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0226' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A why question in the simple past becomes Why + was/were + subject + past participle in the passive. The object him becomes the subject he. So "Why did you scold him?" becomes "Why was he scolded by you?"', wrong = '{"0": "The passive does not use did. \"Why did he scold by you\" is ungrammatical and makes him the doer.", "2": "A question needs the auxiliary was before the subject. \"Why he was scolded\" uses statement word order.", "3": "Scolding is a present participle, not a past participle. The passive needs scolded."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0227' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passive "was won" is in the simple past, so the active form must also be in the simple past. The doer Pakistan, after "by", becomes the subject. So "The match was won by Pakistan" becomes "Pakistan won the match."', wrong = '{"0": "\"Has won\" is the present perfect. The passive was simple past (was won), so the tense would change.", "1": "\"Wins\" is the simple present, which changes the tense.", "3": "\"Was winning\" is the past continuous, which would come from the passive \"was being won\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0228' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A passive sentence uses be + past participle, and its subject receives the action. In "The results were announced on Monday", were announced is be + past participle, and the results are what was announced. So this sentence is passive.', wrong = '{"0": "The subject, the board, does the action of announcing. This is active voice.", "1": "The subject, the students, does the checking. This is active voice.", "2": "Arrived is an active verb with no object. The results did the arriving, so the sentence is active."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0229' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only transitive verbs, those that take an object, can be made passive, because the object becomes the new subject. Slept is intransitive: it has no object. So "The baby slept peacefully" cannot be put into the passive.', wrong = '{"1": "Drank has the object milk, so it can be passive: \"The milk was drunk by the baby.\"", "2": "Fed has the object baby, so it can be passive: \"The baby was fed by the mother.\"", "3": "Examined has the object baby, so it can be passive: \"The baby was examined by the doctor.\""}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0230' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passive voice puts the focus on the action or process and lets the writer leave out the doer. In a lab report, what was done to the solution matters more than who heated it. That is why "The solution was heated to 60 °C" is a good choice.', wrong = '{"0": "It is the other way round. The passive moves attention away from the doer and onto the action.", "2": "Passive sentences are often longer than active ones, because they add be and sometimes \"by ...\".", "3": "Scientists can and do use the active voice. Using the passive is a choice of focus, not a rule."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0231' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the reporting verb is in the past (said), the tense in the reported words moves one step back: am becomes was. The pronoun I changes to he, to match the speaker. So the indirect form is "He said that he was tired."', wrong = '{"0": "The pronoun has changed correctly, but am should shift back to was after the past reporting verb said.", "1": "The tense has changed, but the pronoun I should become he, because the speaker is he.", "3": "\"Had tired\" is a different verb meaning, as if he had made someone tired. \"Am tired\" simply becomes \"was tired\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0232' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In indirect speech after a past reporting verb, will becomes would. Time words also change: tomorrow becomes "the next day" or "the following day". With I changing to she, the indirect form is "She said that she would come the next day."', wrong = '{"0": "Will should change to would, and tomorrow should change to the next day.", "1": "Will has correctly become would, but tomorrow should change to the next day.", "2": "The tense and time word are right, but I should become she, to match the speaker."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0233' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The usual rule is to shift the tense back after a past reporting verb. But a universal truth or scientific fact, such as the earth revolving around the sun, can keep the simple present. So "The teacher said that the earth revolves around the sun" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "\"Had revolved\" suggests the earth used to revolve but no longer does, which is false.", "2": "\"Would revolve\" turns a permanent fact into a future or conditional idea.", "3": "\"Is revolving\" uses the continuous form, which makes a permanent truth sound like a temporary action."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0234' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A reported question uses asked instead of "said to". Question word order (do you live) changes to statement order (I lived), and the tense shifts back from live to lived. You becomes I, since the listener was me. So "He asked me where I lived."', wrong = '{"0": "Reported questions do not use did or question word order. \"Where did I live\" should be \"where I lived\".", "2": "A question is reported with asked, not \"said to\", and live should shift back to lived.", "3": "Reported questions use statement order and end with a full stop. \"Where do I live?\" keeps question form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0235' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A polite request is reported with a verb such as requested, followed by the object and a to-infinitive. The word please is dropped, since requested already carries that politeness. Me becomes her, so the indirect form is "She requested him to help her."', wrong = '{"0": "Commands and requests are not reported with \"that\" + please. Please is dropped and a to-infinitive is used.", "1": "The pattern is \"requested him to help\". \"That he helps me\" keeps the wrong tense and pronoun.", "3": "\"Said him\" is ungrammatical (it should be told him), and me should become her."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0236' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A negative command is reported with a verb such as advised, told or ordered, followed by the object and "not to" + verb. The doctor was giving advice, so advised fits. The indirect form is "The doctor advised the patient not to eat oily food."', wrong = '{"0": "That cannot be followed by an infinitive in this pattern. The correct form is \"advised the patient not to eat\".", "1": "A command is not reported with that + an imperative such as \"do not eat\". It needs \"not to eat\".", "2": "After the object, an infinitive with to is needed: \"not to eat\", not \"do not eat\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0237' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After a past reporting verb, the present perfect shifts back to the past perfect: have finished becomes had finished. The pronouns change to match the speaker: I becomes he and my becomes his. So "He said that he had finished his work."', wrong = '{"1": "The pronouns have changed, but the tense should shift back from \"has finished\" to \"had finished\".", "2": "My should become his. As written, it suggests he finished the reporter''s work, and the tense should be had finished.", "3": "The tense has changed, but I and my should become he and his to match the speaker."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0238' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A yes/no question is reported with if or whether, and the question word order changes to statement order. The tense shifts back (are coming becomes was coming), and you becomes I because the listener was me. So "She asked me if I was coming."', wrong = '{"0": "\"Was I coming\" uses question word order. Reported questions use statement order after if or whether.", "2": "You in the original refers to the listener, me, so it should become I, not you.", "3": "\"Said to\" should become asked, and am should shift back to was."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0239' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After a past reporting verb, the past continuous (were playing) shifts back to the past perfect continuous (had been playing). The pronoun we changes to they, to match the speakers. So "They said that they had been playing cricket."', wrong = '{"0": "\"Were been\" is not a correct verb form in English. The backshifted form is \"had been playing\".", "1": "The tense is right, but we should become they, to match the speakers.", "3": "\"Have been playing\" is the present perfect continuous, not a backshift of \"were playing\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0240' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An exclamation is reported with a verb such as exclaimed, and the exclamation is turned into an ordinary statement. "What a beautiful view!" becomes "that it was a very beautiful view", with very carrying the strong feeling. The exclamation mark is dropped.', wrong = '{"0": "The exclamation form \"what a ...\" is not kept after that. It must become a statement such as \"it was a very beautiful view\".", "1": "Reported speech does not keep the exclamation form or the exclamation mark.", "2": "Asked is used for questions. The original is an exclamation, not a question."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0241' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To change indirect speech back to direct speech, undo each change. She becomes I, her becomes my, and the past perfect "had lost" returns to the present perfect "have lost". "Told me that" becomes "said to me" with quotation marks: She said to me, "I have lost my pen."', wrong = '{"1": "Inside the quotation marks these are her own words, so she would say I, not she.", "2": "She lost her own pen. \"You have lost your pen\" would mean the listener lost a pen.", "3": "\"Had lost\" and her are the reported forms. In her actual words she would say \"I have lost my pen\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0242' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After a past reporting verb, can shifts back to could. A modal is always followed by the base form of the verb, so swim stays as swim. With I becoming he, the indirect form is "He said that he could swim."', wrong = '{"0": "Can should shift to could, and a modal cannot be followed by a past form like swam.", "2": "Could is correct, but a modal must be followed by the base form swim, not swam.", "3": "The tense is right, but I should become he, to match the speaker."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0243' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Let''s" is used to make a suggestion that includes the speaker. A suggestion is reported with suggested, either as "suggested that they should go" or "suggested going". So "She suggested that they should go for a walk" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Let''s\" is a friendly suggestion, not an order, so ordered changes the meaning.", "1": "\"Let us\" is not kept in reported speech after that. It must be turned into a reporting verb like suggested.", "3": "The original is a suggestion, not a question, and reported speech does not end with a question mark."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0244' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After a past reporting verb, the simple past (met) usually shifts back to the past perfect (had met). Time words also change: yesterday becomes "the day before" or "the previous day". So "He said that he had met her the day before."', wrong = '{"0": "Yesterday should change to \"the day before\", since the report is made at a later time. The past perfect is also expected.", "1": "\"Has met\" is the present perfect, which does not fit a past time like the day before. It should be had met.", "2": "The tense and time word are right, but I should become he, to match the speaker."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0245' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In indirect speech, words pointing to something near the speaker change: this becomes that. The tense shifts back (is becomes was), and my becomes her to match the speaker. So "She said that that was her book."', wrong = '{"1": "Nothing has changed: this, is and my should become that, was and her.", "2": "Is has shifted to was, but my should become her and this should become that.", "3": "This and my have changed correctly, but is should shift back to was."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0246' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With neither...nor, the verb agrees with the subject nearer to it. Here the nearer subject is "the teacher", which is singular. So the singular verb was is correct: "Neither the students nor the teacher was present."', wrong = '{"0": "Were is plural and would agree with students, but the verb must agree with the nearer subject, teacher.", "2": "Are is plural. The nearer subject, teacher, is singular.", "3": "\"Have been\" is plural. The nearer subject is singular, so it would need \"has been\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0247' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The verb agrees with the main subject, not with a noun in a phrase that comes between them. The subject is quality, which is singular; "of these medicines" only describes it. So the singular verb is is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Are agrees with medicines, but medicines is inside the phrase \"of these medicines\". The subject is quality.", "1": "Were is plural and agrees with medicines. The real subject, quality, is singular.", "3": "\"Have been\" is plural. The singular subject quality would need \"has been\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0248' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each is a singular pronoun, even when followed by "of the players". So the verb must be singular, and in the present simple the singular form takes -s. "Each of the players receives a medal" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Receive without -s is the plural form. Each needs a singular verb.", "1": "Are is plural. Each would need is, as in \"is receiving\".", "2": "Have is plural. Each would need has, as in \"has received\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0249' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Normally two nouns joined by and take a plural verb. But when the two nouns together name one thing or one dish, the verb is singular. "Bread and butter" is one breakfast item, so "is" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Are treats bread and butter as two separate things. Here they form one dish, so a singular verb is needed.", "2": "Were is plural and past. \"Bread and butter\" as one dish takes a singular verb.", "3": "\"Have been\" is plural. One dish would take \"has been\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0250' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some nouns end in -s but are singular. Names of subjects such as mathematics, physics and economics take a singular verb. So "Mathematics is my favourite subject" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Are is plural. Mathematics is a singular subject name, despite ending in -s.", "2": "Were is plural and past. Mathematics takes a singular verb.", "3": "\"Have been\" is plural. Mathematics, being singular, would take \"has been\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0251' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"A number of" means many or several, so it refers to more than one and takes a plural verb. "A number of students are absent today" means several students are absent. A memory hook: a number of = plural, the number of = singular.', wrong = '{"0": "Is is singular. \"A number of students\" means many students, which needs a plural verb.", "1": "Was is singular. \"A number of\" takes a plural verb.", "3": "\"Has been\" is singular. \"A number of\" would take \"have been\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0252' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"The number of" refers to a single figure or count, so it takes a singular verb. Here what is increasing is the count of dengue patients. So "The number of dengue patients is increasing" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Are is plural. The subject is \"the number\", which is one count.", "1": "Were is plural and past. \"The number\" is one count, so it takes a singular verb.", "2": "\"Have been\" is plural. \"The number\" would take \"has been\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0253' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A distance, sum of money or period of time is usually thought of as one amount, so it takes a singular verb. "Ten kilometres" is one distance, not ten separate kilometres. So "Ten kilometres is a long distance to walk" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Are treats the kilometres as separate items. A distance as one amount takes a singular verb.", "2": "Were is plural and past. \"Ten kilometres\" as one distance takes a singular verb.", "3": "\"Have been\" is plural. One distance would take \"has been\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0254' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With either...or, the verb agrees with the subject nearer to it. The nearer subject is "my father", which is singular. So the singular verb is is correct: "Either my brothers or my father is going to the meeting."', wrong = '{"0": "Are agrees with brothers, but the verb must agree with the nearer subject, father, which is singular.", "2": "Were is plural. The nearer subject, father, is singular.", "3": "\"Have been\" is plural. The nearer subject is singular."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0255' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Phrases such as "along with", "as well as" and "together with" add information but do not change the subject. The subject is still "the teacher", which is singular. So the singular verb is is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Are treats \"her students\" as part of the subject. Words after \"along with\" do not change the subject, teacher.", "1": "Were is plural. The subject, teacher, is singular.", "3": "\"Have been\" is plural. The singular subject teacher would take \"has been\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0256' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In "One of my friends", the subject is one, which is singular; "of my friends" only says which group it comes from. So the verb must be singular. "One of my friends is a doctor" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Are agrees with friends, but the subject is one, which is singular.", "1": "Were is plural and past. The subject, one, is singular.", "2": "\"Have been\" is plural. The singular subject one would take \"has been\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0257' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In sentences beginning with there, the verb agrees with the real subject that follows it. Here the real subject is "many reasons", which is plural. So "There are many reasons for his failure" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Is is singular. The real subject, many reasons, is plural.", "2": "Was is singular. The real subject, many reasons, is plural, so it needs are.", "3": "\"Has been\" is singular. A plural subject would need \"have been\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0258' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nouns for things made of two matching parts, such as scissors, trousers, glasses and pliers, are treated as plural. They take a plural verb. So "The scissors are on the table" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Is is singular. Scissors always takes a plural verb.", "2": "Was is singular. Scissors takes a plural verb.", "3": "\"Has been\" is singular. Scissors would take \"have been\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0259' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Everyone is a singular indefinite pronoun, even though it refers to many people. So the verb must be singular, and in the present simple it takes -s. "Everyone in the class wants to pass the test" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Want without -s is the plural form. Everyone needs a singular verb.", "1": "Are is plural, and want is a stative verb that is not normally used in the continuous form.", "3": "Have is the plural form. Everyone is singular, so it would need has, as in \"has wanted\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0260' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'News ends in -s but is an uncountable noun and is always singular. It takes a singular verb. So "The news from the hospital is good" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Are is plural. News is singular despite ending in -s.", "1": "Were is the plural past form. News is singular, so it takes a singular verb.", "2": "\"Have been\" is plural. News would take \"has been\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0261' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With "not only ... but also", the verb agrees with the subject nearer to it. The nearer subject is "the principal", which is singular. So the singular verb was is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Were is plural and agrees with students. The verb must agree with the nearer subject, principal.", "2": "\"Have been\" is plural. The nearer subject is singular.", "3": "Are is plural. The nearer subject, principal, is singular."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0262' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Many a" means many, but grammatically it is followed by a singular noun and a singular verb. So "Many a student has failed this paper" is correct. It is a slightly formal way of saying many students have failed.', wrong = '{"0": "Have is plural. \"Many a\" always takes a singular verb.", "2": "Are is plural, and \"are failed\" is not the right form here. The active present perfect is needed: has failed.", "3": "Were is plural. \"Many a\" takes a singular verb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0263' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Physics and chemistry are two separate subjects joined by and, so together they form a plural subject. A plural subject takes a plural verb. So "Physics and chemistry are both compulsory" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Is is singular. Two separate subjects joined by and need a plural verb.", "1": "Was is singular. The compound subject is plural.", "3": "\"Has been\" is singular. A plural subject would need \"have been\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0264' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is accommodation, with a double c and a double m. A memory hook: the word is large enough to accommodate two c''s and two m''s. It means a place to live or stay.', wrong = '{"0": "Accomodation has only one m. The correct spelling has a double m: accommodation.", "1": "Acommodation has only one c. The correct spelling has a double c: accommodation.", "2": "Accommodasion ends in -sion. The correct ending is -tion: accommodation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0265' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is necessary, with one c and a double s. A memory hook: a shirt has one collar (c) and two sleeves (ss). It means needed or essential.', wrong = '{"1": "Neccessary has a double c. The word has only one c: necessary.", "2": "Necessery has e before the final ry. The correct ending is -ary: necessary.", "3": "Nessesary uses s where c should be. The correct spelling is necessary, with one c and double s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0266' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is occurrence, with a double c, a double r and the ending -ence. The verb occur doubles its r before adding a suffix, giving occurred and occurrence. It means something that happens.', wrong = '{"0": "Occurence has only one r. The r doubles before the suffix: occurrence.", "2": "Ocurrence has only one c. The word starts with occ-: occurrence.", "3": "Occurrance ends in -ance. The correct ending is -ence: occurrence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0267' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is embarrass, with a double r and a double s. A memory hook: someone embarrassed goes really red and smiles shyly (rr and ss). It means to make someone feel awkward or ashamed.', wrong = '{"0": "Embarass has only one r. The correct spelling has a double r: embarrass.", "1": "Embarras has only one s at the end. The correct spelling ends in double s: embarrass.", "3": "Emmbarrass has a double m. The word has only one m: embarrass."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0268' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is separate, with an a after the p. A memory hook: there is "a rat" in separate. It means to divide or keep apart.', wrong = '{"0": "Seperate uses e after the p. The correct spelling has a: separate.", "1": "Separete has e before the t. The correct spelling has a: separate.", "2": "Saperate has a after the s. The correct spelling has e there: separate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0269' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is receive, following the rule "i before e, except after c". Because the letters come after c, they are written ei. It means to get or be given something.', wrong = '{"1": "Recieve puts i before e after c, breaking the rule \"except after c\". The correct spelling is receive.", "2": "Receeve uses ee. The correct spelling uses ei: receive.", "3": "Reiceve puts the letters in the wrong order. The correct spelling is receive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0270' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is privilege, with i in the first two syllables and the ending -lege. There is no d in the word, even though many people add one. It means a special right or advantage.', wrong = '{"0": "Priviledge adds a d before the final ge. The correct spelling has no d: privilege.", "2": "Privilage uses a in the last syllable. The correct ending is -lege: privilege.", "3": "Previlege uses e in the first syllable. The correct spelling begins pri-: privilege."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0271' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is committee, with a double m, a double t and a double e. A memory hook: double letters three times in a row (mm, tt, ee). It means a group of people chosen to do a job.', wrong = '{"0": "Commitee has only one t. The correct spelling has a double t: committee.", "1": "Comittee has only one m. The correct spelling has a double m: committee.", "3": "Committe ends with a single e. The correct spelling ends in double e: committee."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0272' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct British spelling is diarrhoea, with a double r and the ending -hoea. (American English writes diarrhea.) It means frequent, watery stools.', wrong = '{"0": "Diarhoea has only one r. The correct spelling has a double r: diarrhoea.", "1": "Diarrhoae ends in -ae. The correct ending is -oea: diarrhoea.", "2": "Diarrohea puts the o before the h. The correct order is -rrhoea: diarrhoea."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0273' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The pattern "one of the + superlative + plural noun" is used to pick one member from a group. A superlative such as best needs the before it, and the noun after it must be plural, surgeons. So "He is one of the best surgeons in the city" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "After \"one of the best\", the noun must be plural, because he is one of many surgeons. Surgeon should be surgeons.", "2": "Better is a comparative, used for comparing two. Comparing him with a whole group needs the superlative best.", "3": "A superlative such as best needs the before it: \"one of the best\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0274' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A length of time, such as two hours, takes for, while a starting point takes since. An action that started in the past and is still going on takes the present perfect continuous. So "I have been waiting for two hours" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The tense is right, but since is used with a starting point, such as since 9 o''clock, not with a duration like two hours.", "2": "Both parts are wrong: the present continuous cannot show duration up to now, and since cannot be used with two hours.", "3": "The preposition is right, but the present continuous \"am waiting\" cannot show how long an action has been going on."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0275' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Items in a list should all have the same grammatical form; this is called parallel structure. Swimming, running and cycling are all gerunds (-ing forms), so they match. "She likes swimming, running and cycling" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"To cycle\" is an infinitive, which breaks the pattern of -ing forms.", "1": "\"To swim\" is an infinitive, while running and cycling are -ing forms. The list is not parallel.", "3": "\"To run\" is an infinitive between two -ing forms, which breaks the pattern."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0276' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When something is compared with the rest of its own group, "any other" must be used. Lahore is itself a city in Punjab, so it can only be bigger than the other cities. "Lahore is bigger than any other city in Punjab" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Lahore is a city in Punjab, so \"bigger than any city in Punjab\" would include Lahore itself. It cannot be bigger than itself.", "1": "Biggest is a superlative and cannot be followed by than. The comparative bigger is needed.", "2": "\"More bigger\" is a double comparative. Bigger already includes the idea of more."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0277' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Standard English uses only one negative word in a clause. "I don''t know anything" has one negative (don''t) followed by anything. So "I don''t know anything about it" is correct, and it means the same as "I know nothing about it".', wrong = '{"1": "\"Don''t ... nothing\" is a double negative. In standard English it should be \"don''t ... anything\" or just \"know nothing\".", "2": "This uses three negatives (don''t, nothing, nothing), which is not standard English.", "3": "A negative statement with a main verb needs do not. \"I not know\" should be \"I do not know\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0278' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Short, one-syllable adjectives like tall form the comparative by adding -er. Comparatives are followed by than. So "She is taller than her sister" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"More taller\" is a double comparative. Taller already means more tall.", "2": "Tallest is a superlative and cannot be followed by than.", "3": "Most is used for superlatives of longer adjectives. \"Most tall than\" mixes a superlative with than and is incorrect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0279' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The choice between a and an depends on the first sound, not the first letter. In honest the h is silent, so the word begins with a vowel sound ("on-ist"). So it takes an: "He is an honest man."', wrong = '{"0": "A is used before consonant sounds. Honest begins with a vowel sound because the h is silent.", "1": "The article must come before the adjective and noun: an honest man, not \"honest a man\".", "3": "The would point to one particular man already known to the listener, which changes the meaning."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0280' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A and an are chosen by sound, not spelling. MBBS is read letter by letter, and M is pronounced "em", which begins with a vowel sound. So it takes an: "She is an MBBS student."', wrong = '{"0": "M is pronounced \"em\", which starts with a vowel sound, so an is needed instead of a.", "1": "Student is a singular countable noun, so it needs an article before it.", "2": "This sentence is incomplete. It ends with \"of\" and does not finish the idea."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0281' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Discuss is a transitive verb, which means it takes a direct object without a preposition. You discuss something, not discuss about it. So "Please discuss the problem with your supervisor" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "\"Discuss about\" is a very common error. Discuss takes its object directly.", "2": "\"Discuss on\" is incorrect. No preposition follows discuss.", "3": "\"Discuss over\" is incorrect. No preposition follows discuss."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0282' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some comparatives from Latin, such as senior, junior, superior, inferior and prior, are followed by to, not than. So "He is senior to me" is correct. A memory hook: Latin -ior words take to.', wrong = '{"0": "Senior is followed by to, not than. \"Senior than\" is a common error.", "2": "Senior already expresses a comparison, so in the to-pattern it does not need more. \"More senior than\" is not the standard form tested here.", "3": "\"Senior from\" is incorrect. The fixed preposition is to."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0283' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Prefer uses the pattern "prefer A to B", and when the items are activities both are -ing forms. So "I prefer reading to watching television" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Prefer ... than\" is a common error. Prefer is followed by to when comparing two things.", "1": "Rather needs than and a different pattern, as in \"I prefer to read rather than watch television\".", "3": "From is not used with prefer. The correct preposition is to."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0284' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Despite and "in spite of" mean the same thing, but they are used differently. Despite is never followed by of, while "in spite" always needs of. So "Despite the rain, the match continued" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Despite of\" is incorrect. Despite is used on its own, without of.", "1": "\"In spite\" must be followed by of: \"In spite of the rain\".", "2": "\"Despite of\" is incorrect, and \"the\" before rain is also missing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0285' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each is singular, even when followed by "of the boys", so it needs a singular verb. "Have brought" is plural and should be "has brought". The corrected sentence is "Each of the boys has brought his own lunch box."', wrong = '{"1": "\"Each of the boys\" is a correct subject. Each is followed by of and a plural noun.", "2": "\"His own\" is correct, because it is singular and matches each.", "3": "\"Lunch box\" is a correct noun phrase and has no error."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0286' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Since is used with a starting point, and for with a length of time. Five years is a duration, so it should be "for five years". The corrected sentence is "She has been working here for five years and enjoys it."', wrong = '{"0": "\"Has been\" is correct, because the present perfect continuous shows an action continuing up to now.", "2": "\"Working here\" is correct as part of \"has been working\".", "3": "\"And enjoys it\" is correct. Enjoys agrees with the singular subject she."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0287' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an unreal or imaginary condition, formal English uses were for all persons, including I. The speaker is not the listener, so the situation is imaginary. "If I was you" should be "If I were you".', wrong = '{"0": "\"I would accept\" is correct. Would follows an unreal if-clause in the second conditional.", "1": "\"The offer\" is a correct noun phrase. It is the object of accept and has no error.", "3": "\"Without delay\" is a correct phrase meaning at once."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0288' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"The reason" already expresses cause, so adding because repeats the idea. The standard form is "The reason ... is that ...". So "is because" is the error: "The reason he failed is that he did not revise."', wrong = '{"0": "\"The reason he failed\" is a correct subject phrase.", "1": "\"He did not\" is correct. It forms the negative of the simple past.", "2": "Revise is correct, because did not is followed by the base form of the verb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0289' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Neither, used as a pronoun, means "not either one" and is singular. It therefore takes a singular verb, even when followed by "of the two answers". So are should be is: "Neither of the two answers is correct."', wrong = '{"1": "\"Neither of the\" is a correct way to begin the subject.", "2": "\"Two answers\" is correct after \"neither of the\", which is followed by a plural noun.", "3": "Correct is a proper adjective after the verb. The error is in the verb, not here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0290' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Avoid is one of the verbs followed by a gerund (-ing form), not a to-infinitive. So "to eat sweets" should be "eating sweets". The corrected sentence is "The doctor advised him to avoid eating sweets."', wrong = '{"0": "\"The doctor\" is a correct subject. It is singular and fits the verb advised.", "2": "\"Advised him\" is correct. Advise is followed by an object and then a to-infinitive.", "3": "\"To avoid\" correctly follows \"advised him\", since advise + object + to-infinitive is the right pattern."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0291' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Orthopaedic begins with a vowel sound, so the indefinite article an is used before it. The surgeon is not a particular, known one, but any surgeon of that kind, so the is not needed. "She wants to become an orthopaedic surgeon" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "A is used before consonant sounds. Orthopaedic begins with a vowel sound.", "1": "The would point to one particular surgeon already known. She wants to become a surgeon of that type, not a specific one.", "3": "Surgeon is a singular countable noun, so it needs an article here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0292' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Names of rivers, seas and oceans take the definite article the, as in the Indus, the Ravi and the Arabian Sea. So "The Indus is the longest river in Pakistan" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "A is used for any one of many things. The Indus is a single, named river.", "1": "An is also an indefinite article and cannot be used before a unique named river.", "2": "River names always take the, so the article cannot be left out."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0293' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A and an are chosen by sound. University begins with a "yoo" sound, which is a consonant sound (like y in yes). So it takes a: "There is a university near our house."', wrong = '{"1": "Although university begins with the letter u, it is pronounced \"yoo\", a consonant sound, so an is wrong.", "2": "\"There is\" introduces something new to the listener, so the indefinite article a is used, not the.", "3": "University is a singular countable noun, so it needs an article here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0294' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a question is placed inside another sentence (an embedded or indirect question), it uses statement word order: subject before verb. So "where is the pharmacy" becomes "where the pharmacy is". "Can you tell me where the pharmacy is?" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Where is the pharmacy\" keeps direct question order. Inside an embedded question the subject comes before the verb.", "2": "\"The pharmacy where is\" puts the words in the wrong order.", "3": "The main question needs \"Can you\" at the start. \"Where you can tell me\" makes no sense."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0295' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In medicine, a benign growth is one that is not cancerous and not harmful. The sentence says the patient needed no further treatment after the biopsy. This fits a lump that was found to be harmless.', wrong = '{"0": "Cancerous means malignant, which is the opposite of benign. A cancerous lump would need further treatment.", "1": "Benign does not describe pain. A benign lump may or may not be painful.", "3": "A lump growing quickly would be a warning sign and would need treatment."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0296' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In medicine, acute means coming on suddenly and being severe, often for a short time. The pain began only an hour earlier and he was rushed to the emergency room. Sudden and severe fits this exactly.', wrong = '{"0": "Long-lasting and mild describes chronic conditions, the opposite of acute.", "1": "Nothing in the sentence suggests the pain was not real. He was rushed to hospital.", "2": "Being rushed to the emergency room shows the pain was serious, not slowly improving."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0297' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Chronic means lasting a long time or coming back again and again. The cough troubled her every winter for more than ten years. So chronic means lasting a long time; its opposite in medicine is acute.', wrong = '{"1": "Sudden is the meaning of acute, the opposite of chronic.", "2": "Infectious means able to spread to others. The sentence says nothing about the cough spreading.", "3": "The cough troubled her for years, so mild is not the meaning. Chronic is about duration, not strength."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0298' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Dormant means alive but not active for a time, with the ability to become active again. The virus stays quiet in the body for years and then causes symptoms "again". So the meaning is inactive but able to become active.', wrong = '{"0": "A dead virus could not cause symptoms again later.", "2": "Spreading rapidly is the opposite of staying quiet in the body for years.", "3": "The virus does cause symptoms again, so it is not harmless forever."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0299' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Lethal means able to cause death, that is, deadly. The sentence warns that even a small dose can be lethal if not treated within an hour. This urgency shows the poison can kill.', wrong = '{"0": "Mild contradicts the warning that a small dose needs treatment within an hour.", "1": "Taste is not mentioned in the sentence. Lethal is about the poison''s effect, not its taste.", "3": "The one-hour limit suggests the poison acts fast, not slowly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0300' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Contagious means easily passed from one person to another, usually by contact. The sentence shows this: one infected child can spread measles to most of an unvaccinated class. So the closest meaning is easily passed from person to person.', wrong = '{"0": "Being easily cured is about treatment, not about how a disease spreads.", "1": "The sentence shows measles spreads widely through a class, which is the opposite of rare.", "2": "Diet is not mentioned. The disease is passed between children, not caused by what they eat."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0301' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ample means plentiful, more than enough. The sentence says nobody had to rush the last questions, which shows there was spare time. So ample means more than enough.', wrong = '{"1": "Too little time would have forced students to rush, which the sentence says did not happen.", "2": "Ample suggests extra or plenty, not an exact amount.", "3": "Nothing in the sentence says the time was wasted. It says there was plenty of it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0302' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Vigilant means watchful and alert, especially for possible danger or problems. Nurses in intensive care must keep watching every monitor through the night. This careful watching is what vigilant describes.', wrong = '{"0": "Sleepy is the opposite of what the sentence requires. Nurses watching every monitor must stay awake and alert.", "2": "Careless is the opposite of vigilant. Watching every monitor shows great care.", "3": "Cheerful describes mood. The sentence is about staying watchful, not about being happy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0303' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Superfluous means more than is needed, extra and unnecessary. After the editor removed these words, the report was half as long but just as clear. This shows the words were not needed.', wrong = '{"0": "Removing difficult words would not leave the report just as clear unless they were also unneeded. The sentence stresses that they were extra.", "1": "Removing important words would make the report less clear, but it stayed just as clear.", "3": "The words were cut because they were extra, not because of spelling mistakes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0304' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To augment means to make something larger by adding to it. The hospital hired extra nurses, which made its staff bigger during the busy dengue season. So augment means increase.', wrong = '{"0": "The existing staff stayed and new nurses were added, so nobody was replaced.", "1": "Hiring extra nurses makes the staff larger, not smaller.", "2": "Training means teaching skills. Hiring extra nurses adds people rather than training them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0305' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Tedious means tiring and dull because it goes on too long or is repetitive. Copying the same table fifty times by hand is repetitive, and the sentence says it left everyone bored. So tedious means long and boring.', wrong = '{"1": "Exciting contradicts the sentence, which says the task left everyone bored.", "2": "Dangerous means likely to cause harm. Copying a table by hand carries no danger.", "3": "Copying a table fifty times by hand takes a long time, so it is not quick."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0306' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Resilient means able to recover quickly from illness, shock or difficulty. The sentence explains this directly: children recover quickly from illnesses that would weaken an adult for weeks. So resilient means able to recover quickly.', wrong = '{"0": "Fragile means easily broken or harmed. It is the opposite of resilient.", "2": "Careless means not paying attention. It has nothing to do with recovering from illness.", "3": "Infectious means able to spread disease. The sentence is about recovering, not spreading illness."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0307' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ambivalent means having mixed or opposing feelings about something at the same time. She was excited about the opportunity but sad to leave her family. Holding both feelings at once is what ambivalent means.', wrong = '{"0": "Completely certain is the opposite. She is torn between two feelings.", "1": "Anger is not one of the feelings described. She feels excitement and sadness.", "3": "Uninterested means not caring. She cares a great deal, both about the move and about her family."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0308' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To exacerbate means to make a problem, pain or illness worse. Scratching a rash increases the itching and may even cause an infection. So exacerbate means make worse.', wrong = '{"0": "Relieve means to make less severe, which is the opposite of exacerbate.", "1": "Scratching does not hide the itching. It makes it stronger.", "2": "Explain means to make clear. Nothing is being explained in the sentence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0309' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Feasible means possible to do or practical. Finishing the whole syllabus twice in only two weeks is too much to do in that time. So "not feasible" means not possible, and feasible means possible to do.', wrong = '{"1": "Necessary means needed. The sentence is about whether the task can be done, not whether it must be.", "2": "Enjoyable means pleasant. The problem is the lack of time, not enjoyment.", "3": "Expensive means costing a lot. Money is not mentioned in the sentence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0310' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Adequate means enough for a particular purpose, good enough but not more. Sufficient has the same meaning. For example, "adequate rest" and "sufficient rest" both mean enough rest.', wrong = '{"0": "Excellent means very good, which is well above adequate. Adequate only means good enough.", "2": "Lacking means not having enough, the opposite of adequate.", "3": "Excessive means too much. Adequate means just enough, not more."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0311' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To deter means to discourage someone from doing something, often by making them afraid of the result. For example, heavy fines deter people from breaking traffic rules. So discourage is the closest synonym.', wrong = '{"0": "Encourage means to give someone support or confidence to do something, the opposite of deter.", "1": "Delay means to make something happen later. Deter means to stop or discourage it.", "3": "Decide means to make a choice. It does not mean to discourage."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0312' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Vivid means very bright, strong and clear. It is used for colours (vivid red), images and memories (a vivid memory). So bright and clear is the closest meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "Dull means lacking brightness or interest. It is the opposite of vivid.", "1": "Faint means weak and hard to see or hear. It is the opposite of vivid.", "2": "Secret means kept hidden. It has nothing to do with brightness or clarity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0313' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Reluctant means unwilling or hesitant to do something. For example, "He was reluctant to admit his mistake." So unwilling is the closest synonym.', wrong = '{"1": "Eager means keen and wanting to do something, the opposite of reluctant.", "2": "Angry describes a different feeling. A reluctant person may be calm but simply not want to act.", "3": "Unable describes a lack of ability. Reluctant describes a lack of willingness."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0314' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fatigue means extreme tiredness, usually from hard work, illness or lack of sleep. Doctors often list fatigue as a symptom. So tiredness is the closest synonym.', wrong = '{"0": "Fever is a raised body temperature. It can cause fatigue but is not the same thing.", "2": "Hunger is the need or desire for food. It is a different feeling from tiredness.", "3": "Anxiety is a feeling of worry or nervousness, not tiredness."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0315' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Trivial means of little value or importance. For example, "Don''t waste time on trivial details." So unimportant is the closest synonym.', wrong = '{"0": "Serious means important and needing attention, which is close to the opposite of trivial.", "1": "Difficult describes how hard something is, not how important it is.", "3": "Ancient means very old. It has nothing to do with importance."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0316' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Artificial means made by people rather than occurring in nature, as in artificial flowers. Natural means existing in nature, not made by people. Natural is therefore the opposite of artificial.', wrong = '{"0": "False means not real or not true. It is close in meaning to artificial, not opposite.", "1": "Man-made means made by people, which is a synonym of artificial, not an opposite.", "2": "Expensive is about price. Artificial things can be cheap or costly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0317' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To expand means to become larger in size or amount. To contract means to become smaller, for example metals expand when heated and contract when cooled. Contract is therefore the opposite of expand.', wrong = '{"1": "Enlarge means to make bigger, a synonym of expand.", "2": "Explain means to make something clear. It only looks a little like expand.", "3": "Extend means to make longer or larger, which is close in meaning to expand."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0318' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Humble means modest, not thinking oneself better than others. Arrogant means having too high an opinion of oneself and looking down on others. Arrogant is therefore the opposite of humble.', wrong = '{"0": "Modest means not boasting about oneself. It is a synonym of humble, not an opposite.", "2": "Humble can describe poor or simple circumstances, as in \"humble home\", but the opposite of a humble character is arrogance, not poverty.", "3": "Gentle means kind and mild. It is close in meaning to humble, not opposite."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0319' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Temporary means lasting for only a limited time. Permanent means lasting for ever or for a very long time without change. Permanent is therefore the opposite of temporary.', wrong = '{"0": "Brief means short in time, which is a synonym of temporary.", "1": "Short-term means lasting a short time, which is a synonym of temporary.", "3": "Recent describes when something happened, not how long it lasts."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0320' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Benevolent means kind, generous and wishing to do good to others. Cruel means wanting to cause pain or suffering. Cruel is therefore the opposite of benevolent.', wrong = '{"0": "Kind means caring and helpful to others. It is a synonym of benevolent, not an opposite.", "1": "Wealthy means rich. A wealthy person can be benevolent or not.", "2": "Famous means well known. It has nothing to do with kindness."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0321' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Vague means not clearly expressed or not exact, as in vague instructions. Precise means exact, accurate and clearly stated. Precise is therefore the opposite of vague.', wrong = '{"1": "Unclear means hard to understand. It is a synonym of vague, not an opposite.", "2": "Distant means far away. It describes distance, not clarity.", "3": "Quiet describes sound, not how clearly something is expressed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0322' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Did not just damage" calls for a stronger word than damage. "Leaving nothing but ashes" shows total ruin. Destroyed, meaning damaged so badly that nothing is left, is the strongest and best fit.', wrong = '{"0": "Harmed means damaged to some degree. It is too weak for a building reduced to ashes.", "2": "Affected means had some influence on. It is far too mild for total destruction.", "3": "Scratched means only slight surface damage, the opposite of being reduced to ashes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0323' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Not merely pleased" calls for a feeling stronger than pleased. Overjoyed means extremely happy. So "they were overjoyed" fits the sentence best.', wrong = '{"0": "Satisfied means about as happy as pleased. It is not stronger.", "1": "Content means quietly happy, which is milder than pleased.", "3": "Calm means peaceful and not excited. It does not show greater happiness."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0324' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Effect is usually a noun meaning a result or consequence. The phrase "had a bad ___" needs a noun after the adjective bad. The rain caused a bad result for the crop, so effect is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Affect is usually a verb meaning to influence, as in \"The rain affected the crop\". A noun is needed after \"a bad\".", "1": "Effort means hard work or an attempt. Rain does not make an effort.", "2": "Afect is not an English word. The verb is spelt affect, and the noun needed here is effect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0325' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Rest means to relax, sleep or recover. Patients in a ward need quiet in order to rest. After "trying to" the base form of a verb is needed, so rest is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Wrest means to pull something away by force. It sounds like rest but has a very different meaning.", "2": "Rust is the reddish coating that forms on iron. It does not fit patients in a ward.", "3": "\"Trying to\" must be followed by the base form of a verb, so rested, a past form, is wrong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0326' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sew means to stitch with a needle and thread. A surgeon stitches a wound closed, which needs a steady hand. So sew is correct, even though sow and so sound the same.', wrong = '{"0": "Sow means to plant seeds. It sounds like sew but has a different meaning.", "2": "So is a conjunction or adverb, as in \"so tired\". It cannot be a verb here.", "3": "Saw is the past tense of see or a cutting tool. It does not mean to stitch."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0327' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage states that antibiotics kill bacteria but "have no effect on viruses, which cause most colds and flu". So antibiotics are not useful for most colds because colds are mostly caused by viruses.', wrong = '{"0": "The two days in the passage refers to patients feeling better during a course of antibiotics, not to how long colds last.", "1": "The cost of antibiotics is never mentioned in the passage.", "3": "The passage does not say cold bacteria are always resistant. It says most colds are viral."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0328' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage explains how taking antibiotics when not needed, or stopping early, lets the toughest bacteria survive and spread resistance. Every sentence leads to this point and to the doctors'' advice. So the main idea is that misusing antibiotics helps bacteria become resistant.', wrong = '{"0": "The passage does not compare how dangerous viruses and bacteria are.", "1": "The passage focuses on patients misusing antibiotics, not on doctors prescribing too many.", "2": "Treating colds at home is not discussed in the passage."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0329' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage says that when people stop a course early, "the bacteria that survive are the toughest ones", and these multiply and pass on resistance. Finishing the course kills these tough bacteria too. That is why doctors ask patients to finish it even if they feel better.', wrong = '{"1": "Feeling better is a sign the infection is improving, not a sign of resistance.", "2": "The passage does not say the medicine stops working after two days. Two days is when patients may start feeling better.", "3": "Throwing away unused tablets is not mentioned in the passage."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0330' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To urge someone means to strongly advise or encourage them to do something. Doctors urge patients to take antibiotics only when prescribed and to finish the course, because misuse leads to resistance. So urge means strongly advise.', wrong = '{"0": "Forbid means to not allow something. Doctors want patients to follow the advice, which is the opposite of forbidding.", "2": "Force means to make someone do something against their will. Doctors can advise strongly, but they cannot force patients.", "3": "Forget makes no sense in the sentence. Doctors are actively giving advice."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0331' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage says Snow marked each death on a map and "noticed that the deaths clustered around a single water pump on Broad Street". So he saw that the deaths were grouped around one water pump.', wrong = '{"0": "The passage says the deaths were clustered in one place, not spread evenly across London.", "1": "Hospitals are not mentioned anywhere in the passage.", "3": "Wind direction would fit the bad-air theory, but the map showed deaths grouped around a pump, not following the wind."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0332' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Most doctors believed cholera spread through bad air, but Snow''s map showed deaths grouped around one pump, and cases fell after the handle was removed. His careful evidence helped show that cholera spreads through water. This supports the idea that observation can overturn a widely held belief.', wrong = '{"0": "The passage says most doctors believed in the bad-air theory, so they did not agree with Snow.", "1": "This is the old belief that the passage shows to be wrong. The evidence pointed to contaminated water.", "2": "The passage says the number of new cases \"soon fell\" after the handle was removed, so it did have an effect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0333' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Clustered means gathered or grouped closely together. The deaths were clustered around a single water pump, meaning many of them were close to that one spot. So the closest meaning is gathered closely together.', wrong = '{"1": "The deaths did not disappear. They were marked on a map and found to be grouped together.", "2": "Spread out means scattered over a wide area. It is the opposite of clustered.", "3": "Clustered describes where things are, not how fast they increase."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0334' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The writer describes Snow as a doctor who solved the problem, and praises "his careful use of evidence". The passage presents his work as successful and important. This shows the writer admires him.', wrong = '{"0": "Nothing in the passage criticises Snow. It praises his methods and results.", "2": "The tone is serious, as it describes deaths and a scientific discovery. Nothing is treated as funny.", "3": "The writer clearly values Snow''s work, calling it careful and showing its success, so the attitude is not indifferent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0335' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A metaphor says one thing is another to show a likeness, without using "like" or "as". Time is directly called "a thief" that steals our youth, meaning it slowly takes away our young years. So the line uses a metaphor.', wrong = '{"0": "A simile would use \"like\" or \"as\", as in \"Time is like a thief\". There is no such word here.", "1": "Onomatopoeia uses words that imitate sounds, such as buzz. No such word appears.", "3": "Alliteration repeats the same first sound in nearby words. The line is not built on such a pattern."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0336' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Personification gives human actions or qualities to something that is not human. Coughing and groaning are things people do, but here the old car does them. So the line mainly uses personification.', wrong = '{"0": "Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration. The line describes the car''s noises, not an exaggeration.", "1": "A simile uses \"like\" or \"as\" to compare. There is no such comparison here.", "2": "An oxymoron puts two opposite words together, such as \"bitter sweet\". The line has no such pair."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0337' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration for emphasis. A bag cannot really weigh a ton (about 1000 kg); the speaker just means it is very heavy. So the line uses hyperbole.', wrong = '{"1": "A metaphor says one thing is another. The bag is not being called something else.", "2": "Personification gives human qualities to non-human things. The bag is not described as acting like a person.", "3": "Irony means saying the opposite of what one means. The speaker really means the bag is heavy, only exaggerating it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0338' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A simile compares two different things using "like" or "as". Here the doctor''s calmness is compared to a cucumber using "as cool as". So the line uses a simile; "cool as a cucumber" is a common English expression for very calm.', wrong = '{"0": "A metaphor makes a comparison without \"like\" or \"as\". This line uses \"as ... as\".", "2": "An oxymoron joins two contradictory words. No opposite words are paired here.", "3": "Onomatopoeia uses words that imitate sounds. No word in the line imitates a sound."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0339' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each is singular, so the pronoun that refers to it must also be singular. The group is mothers, so the singular feminine possessive her is correct. "Each of the mothers brought her baby" means every mother brought her own baby.', wrong = '{"0": "Its is used for things and animals, not for mothers.", "1": "Hers is a possessive pronoun that stands alone, as in \"The baby is hers\". It cannot come before the noun baby.", "3": "Our would include the speaker in the group. The antecedent is each of the mothers."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0340' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A collective noun such as committee takes a singular pronoun when the group acts as one unit. Here the committee made one decision together. So the singular possessive its is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "It''s is a short form of \"it is\" or \"it has\". It is not a possessive.", "1": "His refers to one male person. A committee is a group, not a man.", "2": "Her refers to one female person. A committee is a group, not a woman."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0341' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A collective noun such as jury can take a plural pronoun when its members act as separate individuals. Here the jury members disagreed and argued among themselves. So the plural pronoun they is correct, and it matches "themselves".', wrong = '{"1": "It treats the jury as one single unit, but the members are acting separately and arguing with each other. It also clashes with \"themselves\".", "2": "He refers to one male person. A jury is a group of people.", "3": "Its is a possessive and cannot act as the subject of the verb argued."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0342' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Neither, used as a pronoun, is singular, and the verb has confirms this. A pronoun referring to it must also be singular. The group is girls, so her is correct: "Neither of the girls has finished her homework."', wrong = '{"0": "Their is plural, but neither is singular. In formal grammar, with the gender known, her is needed.", "2": "Hers stands alone and cannot come before a noun such as homework.", "3": "Them is an object pronoun, not a possessive. It cannot come before a noun."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0343' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The relative pronoun refers to people (the patients) and acts as the subject of the verb "were admitted". Who is the subject relative pronoun for people. So "The patients who were admitted yesterday have been discharged" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Which is used for things and animals, not people.", "1": "Whose shows possession and must be followed by a noun, as in \"whose files\".", "3": "Whom is the object form. Here the pronoun is the subject of \"were admitted\", so who is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0344' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The relative pronoun refers to a person (the doctor) and is the object of the preposition about: "I told you about him". In formal English the object form for people is whom. So "This is the doctor whom I told you about" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Which is used for things and animals, not people.", "1": "Whose shows possession and must be followed by a noun, as in \"whose clinic\".", "2": "What cannot introduce a relative clause after a noun. \"The doctor what I told you about\" is non-standard."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0345' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The pronoun is part of the subject "Sana and ___", which does the action went. So the subject form I is needed. A quick test: remove Sana and say "I went to the library", which is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Me is an object form. Removing Sana gives \"me went\", which is clearly wrong.", "2": "Myself is a reflexive pronoun and needs an earlier I in the same clause. It cannot act as a subject.", "3": "Mine is a possessive pronoun meaning \"belonging to me\". It cannot be the subject of went."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0346' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The pronoun comes after the preposition to, so it must be in the object form. Me is the object form of I. A quick test: remove Ali and say "gave the prize to me", which is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "I is a subject form. Removing Ali gives \"to I\", which is wrong after a preposition.", "2": "Myself is used only when the subject of the clause is also I, as in \"I gave myself a prize\". The subject here is the teacher.", "3": "Mine is a possessive pronoun. \"Gave the prize to mine\" does not make sense."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0347' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A pronoun must agree with its antecedent in number and stay in the same person. "A student" is singular and in the third person, so "he or she" matches it. So "If a student wants to pass, he or she must work hard" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Switching from \"a student\" (third person) to you (second person) is a shift in person.", "1": "Switching from \"a student\" to we (first person) is a shift in person.", "3": "\"Students wants\" breaks subject-verb agreement, and the singular he does not match the plural students."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0348' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A pronoun reference is clear when the reader can tell exactly which noun is meant. Repeating "the bottle" instead of using it removes any doubt about what broke. So this sentence has a clear reference.', wrong = '{"0": "It could mean the bottle or the glass, so the reader cannot tell which one broke.", "1": "She could mean Hina or her mother, so it is unclear who needed rest.", "2": "He could mean the doctor or the patient, so it is unclear who had eaten."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0349' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A fixed timetable or a regular, repeated event is expressed with the simple present. "Every evening" shows the train leaves at 6 p.m. as a routine. With a singular subject, the verb takes -s: leaves.', wrong = '{"1": "The present continuous is leaving describes an action happening now or a single arrangement. It does not fit a daily timetable.", "2": "Left is the simple past, which describes one past event. \"Every evening\" shows a regular habit.", "3": "Has left is the present perfect, which describes a completed action. It does not describe a daily schedule."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0350' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The past continuous (was/were + -ing) shows a longer action in progress in the past. A shorter action, in the simple past, interrupts it. She was in the middle of cooking when the phone rang, so "was cooking" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Cooked is the simple past. It makes cooking a completed action and loses the idea that it was in progress.", "2": "Is cooking is the present continuous. The time is past, as shown by rang.", "3": "Had cooked is the past perfect, which would mean dinner was already finished before the phone rang."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0351' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The present perfect (have/has + past participle) is used for experiences up to now, especially with already and a number of times. The speaker has seen the film three times so far. So "I have seen this film three times already" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Saw is the simple past, used for a finished time. It does not go naturally with already for experience up to now in British English.", "1": "See is the simple present. It does not show completed viewings.", "3": "Had seen is the past perfect, which needs a later past point to compare with. None is given."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0352' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The present perfect continuous (has/have been + -ing) describes an action that started in the past and is still going on, often with since or for. She started studying in the morning and is still at it, which explains why she is tired now. So "has been studying" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Studies is the simple present, describing a habit. It cannot be used with since to show duration.", "1": "Studied is the simple past, which describes a finished action. It does not connect to her tiredness now.", "2": "Is studying is the present continuous. It does not go with since to show how long."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0353' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When one past action was finished before another past action, the earlier one takes the past perfect. The patient lost consciousness before the ambulance arrived. So "the patient had lost consciousness" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Lost is the simple past. With \"by the time\", it does not make clear that this happened first.", "2": "Has lost is the present perfect, which links to now. It does not fit a past time frame like \"arrived\".", "3": "Loses is the simple present, which is wrong for an event in the past."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0354' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The future continuous (will be + -ing) shows an action that will be in progress at a particular future time. At 9 tonight, the speaker will be in the middle of watching the programme. So "I will be watching" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Will watch is the simple future. It does not show that the action will already be in progress at 9.", "2": "Watch is the simple present. It does not describe an action in progress at a future time.", "3": "Watched is the simple past, which is wrong for an event tonight."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0355' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'This is a third conditional, about an imaginary past situation. The pattern is: if + had + past participle, would have + past participle. The speaker did not know about the test, so "I would have studied harder" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Would study\" belongs to the second conditional, about the present or future. The if-clause here is about the past.", "1": "\"Will study\" belongs to the first conditional, about real future possibilities.", "3": "\"Had studied\" is the form used in the if-clause. The main clause needs would have + past participle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0356' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'This is a first conditional, about a real possibility. The if-clause takes the simple present and the main clause takes will. So "If you press the red button, the alarm will ring" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Pressed belongs to the second conditional, which pairs with would, not will.", "1": "Will is not used in the if-clause of a conditional sentence.", "2": "Had pressed belongs to the third conditional, about an imaginary past."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0357' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Last month" is a finished time in the past. A completed action at a stated past time takes the simple past. So "She went to Karachi last month" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "The present perfect has gone cannot be used with a finished past time like last month.", "2": "Goes is the simple present, which does not fit an event that happened last month.", "3": "Had been going is the past perfect continuous, used for an ongoing action before another past event. It is not needed for one completed trip."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0358' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When taste describes a quality of something (how it seems to the tongue), it is a stative verb. Stative verbs are used in simple forms, not continuous ones. So "This soup tastes delicious" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Is tasting\" is a continuous form. Taste meaning \"has a flavour\" is stative and not normally used this way.", "2": "\"Has been tasting\" is a perfect continuous form, which is not used to describe a quality.", "3": "Tasting alone is not a finite verb, so the sentence would have no main verb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0359' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing) describes an action continuing up to a point in the future, with its length stated. By the end of this year, their living in the city will have lasted ten years. So "will have been living" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Will live is the simple future. It does not show duration up to a future point.", "1": "Are living is the present continuous. It does not fit \"by the end of this year ... for ten years\".", "3": "Lived is the simple past, which is wrong for a point in the future."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0360' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Admit, meaning to confess, is one of the verbs followed by a gerund (-ing form). So "He admitted taking the money from the drawer" is correct. "Admitted to taking" is also possible, but there to is a preposition and is still followed by -ing.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Admitted to take\" is wrong. Admit takes a gerund, and if to follows it, to is a preposition followed by -ing.", "1": "A bare verb cannot follow admitted. \"He admitted take\" is ungrammatical.", "2": "\"Admit for\" is not an English pattern. The verb takes a gerund directly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0361' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The pattern "advise + object + to-infinitive" is used to report advice given to someone. So "The doctor advised him to drink more water" is correct. Without an object, advise takes a gerund, as in "advised drinking more water".', wrong = '{"1": "After \"advise + object\" (advised him), a gerund is not used. It should be \"advised him to drink\".", "2": "Advise does not take a bare infinitive. To is needed before drink.", "3": "Drank is a past tense form, which cannot follow \"advised him\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0362' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hope is one of the verbs followed by a to-infinitive. So "She hopes to become a cardiologist one day" is correct. Other such verbs include want, plan, decide and expect.', wrong = '{"0": "Hope does not take a gerund. \"She hopes becoming\" is ungrammatical.", "2": "Hope needs to before the next verb. A bare infinitive cannot follow it.", "3": "\"To becoming\" wrongly mixes the infinitive marker to with an -ing form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0363' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Can''t help" means cannot stop oneself from doing something, and it is followed by a gerund. So "I can''t help laughing when I watch that comedy" means the speaker cannot stop laughing.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Can''t help to laugh\" is not standard with the meaning \"cannot stop oneself\". This expression takes -ing.", "1": "A bare verb cannot follow \"can''t help\" in this meaning. \"I can''t help laugh\" is ungrammatical.", "3": "Laughed is a past form, which cannot follow help."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0364' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Had better" is used to give strong advice, and it is followed by a bare infinitive, the base verb without to. So "You had better see a doctor about that cough" is correct. Like modal verbs, it never takes to after it.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Had better to see\" is wrong. \"Had better\" is never followed by to.", "1": "A gerund cannot follow \"had better\". It takes the base form.", "2": "Saw is a past form. \"Had better\" refers to the present or future and takes the base verb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0365' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A verb that follows a preposition must be in the gerund (-ing) form. In "interested in", in is a preposition. So "He is interested in studying surgery" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "A to-infinitive cannot follow a preposition. \"Interested in to study\" is ungrammatical.", "2": "A bare verb cannot follow a preposition. \"Interested in study\" would have to treat study as a noun, which does not fit before surgery.", "3": "Studied is a past form, which cannot follow the preposition in."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0366' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Forget + gerund refers to the memory of something that already happened. Forget + to-infinitive means failing to do something one should do. The speaker treasures the memory of seeing the Himalayas, so "I will never forget seeing" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Forget to see\" means failing to do something one was supposed to do. It does not fit a treasured memory.", "2": "A bare verb cannot follow forget. \"Never forget see\" is ungrammatical.", "3": "\"To seeing\" is not correct after forget. Forget is followed directly by -ing or by to + base verb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0367' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Stop + to-infinitive means to stop one activity in order to do something else. He was running, and he stopped so that he could drink a glass of water. So "He stopped to drink a glass of water" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Stopped drinking\" would mean he gave up or ended the activity of drinking, which does not fit a tired runner wanting water.", "1": "A bare verb cannot follow stopped. \"He stopped drink\" is ungrammatical.", "3": "Drank is a past form, which cannot follow stopped."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0368' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A gerund is an -ing verb form used as a noun. "Painting landscapes" names what her hobby is and completes the verb is, just as a noun would (Her hobby is art). So painting is used as a gerund.', wrong = '{"0": "A present participle used as an adjective describes a noun, as in \"a painting brush\". Here painting names the hobby, not describing a noun.", "1": "The main verb of the sentence is is. Painting is not the main verb.", "2": "An infinitive is formed with to + base verb, as in \"to paint\". Painting is an -ing form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0369' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"To become a doctor" answers the question "What does she want?" A word group that receives the action of a verb is its object. So the infinitive phrase works as the object of wants, acting like a noun.', wrong = '{"1": "The subject of the sentence is She, the person who wants.", "2": "An adverb of purpose explains why someone does something, as in \"She studies to become a doctor\". Here the phrase says what she wants.", "3": "An adjectival infinitive describes a noun, as in \"a book to read\". This phrase does not describe any noun."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0370' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Already usually goes in the middle of the verb, between the auxiliary have and the past participle. So "I have already finished my assignment" is correct. It can also go at the end in informal speech, but never between the verb and its object.', wrong = '{"0": "Already wrongly separates the verb finished from its object, my assignment.", "2": "\"Already I have my assignment finished\" is not natural English word order and changes the structure of the sentence.", "3": "This changes the meaning: finished now describes the assignment as an adjective, and \"already have\" means already possess."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0371' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An adverb of manner usually comes after the verb and its object, not between them. The verb is speaks and the object is English, so fluently goes at the end. "He speaks English fluently" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Fluently separates the verb speaks from its object English, which is not allowed in English.", "1": "\"He fluently English speaks\" puts the verb at the end, which is not English word order.", "3": "\"Fluently he English speaks\" breaks normal English order of subject, verb, object."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0372' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only limits the word or phrase it is placed next to. Placed before the verb read, it limits the action: reading was all Sara did with the report, and she did not discuss or sign it. So "Sara only read the report" has the required meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Only Sara\" limits the person: nobody except Sara read the report.", "1": "\"Only the report\" limits the thing: she read nothing except the report.", "2": "\"Only yesterday\" limits the time: she read it as recently as yesterday."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0373' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To make a comparative such as better stronger, English uses an adverb of degree like much, far or a lot. Very cannot be used before a comparative. So "The patient is much better today" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Many is used before plural countable nouns, as in \"many patients\". It cannot modify better.", "2": "Very is used with ordinary adjectives (very good) but not with comparatives. \"Very better\" is wrong.", "3": "\"More better\" is a double comparative, because better already means more good."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0374' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '"Perhaps once a year" shows she goes to the gym very seldom. The adverb of frequency that means almost never is rarely. So "She rarely goes to the gym" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Usually means most of the time, which contradicts \"once a year\".", "2": "Always means every time, which contradicts \"once a year\".", "3": "Often means many times, which contradicts \"once a year\"."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0375' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The blank describes how he answered, so it modifies the verb answered. A word that modifies a verb must be an adverb. So the adverb correctly is needed: "He answered the question correctly."', wrong = '{"0": "Correct is an adjective. It describes nouns, as in \"a correct answer\", but not verbs.", "1": "Corrected is a verb form meaning fixed. It does not describe how he answered.", "3": "Correctness is a noun. It cannot modify a verb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0376' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some verbs, such as smell, taste, look, feel and sound, can act as linking verbs that describe the subject. Here smell describes how the flowers are, not an action they perform. A linking verb is followed by an adjective, so "The flowers smell sweet" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Sweetly is an adverb. It would describe how the flowers carry out the action of smelling, which they cannot do.", "1": "Sweetness is a noun. A linking verb like smell is completed by an adjective here.", "2": "Sweeten is a verb meaning to make sweet. It cannot follow another verb like this."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0377' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When adverbs of manner, place and time come together after a verb, the usual order is manner, place, time. Hard (how), in the lab (where), yesterday (when). So "She worked hard in the lab yesterday" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "This puts time first and manner last, which reverses the usual order.", "2": "Place comes before manner here. Manner (hard) should come first.", "3": "Time (yesterday) comes first here, but time should come last."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0378' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An interrogative adverb is a question word that asks about time, place, manner or reason and modifies the verb. Why asks about the reason for leaving early and modifies the verb leave. So why is an interrogative adverb, like when, where and how.', wrong = '{"0": "A relative pronoun, such as who or which, refers back to a noun and begins a relative clause. Why here begins a direct question.", "2": "An adverb of manner tells how something is done, such as quickly. Why asks for a reason, not a way.", "3": "A conjunction joins two clauses. In this question, why does not join any clauses."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0379' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The adjective absent is followed by the preposition from. So "She has been absent from college for three days" is correct. The same preposition is used in "away from".', wrong = '{"0": "\"Absent of\" is not standard English. The fixed preposition is from.", "1": "\"Absent at\" is not standard. Absent takes from.", "3": "\"Absent in\" is not standard. Absent takes from."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0380' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The adjective suitable is followed by for when saying who or what something is right for. So "This medicine is not suitable for children under five" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "\"Suitable to\" is occasionally used for situations, but for people the standard form is \"suitable for\".", "1": "\"Suitable with\" is incorrect. The fixed preposition is for.", "2": "\"Suitable at\" is incorrect. The fixed preposition is for."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0381' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Superior is one of the Latin comparatives, along with inferior, senior and junior, that take to instead of than. So "The new medicine is superior to the old one" is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "\"Superior than\" is a common error. Superior is followed by to.", "2": "\"Superior from\" is incorrect. The fixed preposition is to.", "3": "\"Superior over\" is not standard. Superior takes to."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0382' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Between is used when sharing or dividing something among two people or things. The sweets are shared by two children. So "Divide the sweets between the two children" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Among is used for groups of more than two. Here there are only two children.", "2": "In shows position inside something. It does not express sharing.", "3": "With shows being together or using a tool. It does not express dividing something between people."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0383' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Among is used when something is shared out within a group of more than two. There are five winners, so the money was distributed among them. "Distributed among the five winners" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Between is traditionally used for two people or things. Here there are five winners.", "1": "With shows being together. It does not express handing out shares to a group.", "3": "On shows position on a surface. It does not express distribution."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0384' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Under shows a position below something. A ball that rolls under a bed ends up beneath it, where it is hard to reach. So "The ball rolled under the bed and could not be reached" is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Over means above or across something. A ball rolling over the bed would not be out of reach.", "1": "On means resting on top of a surface. A ball on the bed would be easy to reach.", "2": "Among is used for being in the middle of a group of things. A bed is a single object."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0385' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Since'' is used with a point in time, the moment when an action started, and it pairs naturally with the present perfect continuous. ''Two o''clock'' is a point in time, so the sentence means the waiting began at two and is still going on: ''I have been waiting since two o''clock.''', wrong = '{"1": "''For'' is used with a length of time, such as ''for two hours'' or ''for a week''. ''Two o''clock'' is a point in time, not a period.", "2": "''From'' marks a starting point but normally needs ''to'' or ''until'' to complete the range, as in ''from two to five''. It does not fit with the present perfect continuous here.", "3": "''At'' names an exact clock time for a single event, as in ''The class starts at two''. It cannot show an action continuing up to now."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0386' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Congratulate'' takes the preposition ''on'': we congratulate someone on an achievement or a happy event. So the correct sentence is ''She congratulated me on my success.'' A similar pattern: ''congratulations on your result''.', wrong = '{"0": "''Congratulate for'' is a very common error, probably copied from ''thank someone for''. Standard English uses ''on'' after congratulate.", "2": "''Congratulate at'' is not a standard combination. ''At'' is used for places and times, not for the reason for praise.", "3": "''Congratulate about'' is incorrect. ''About'' follows verbs like talk, think or worry, not congratulate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0387' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''On'' is the preposition used with days and dates, as in ''on Monday'' or ''on 5 May''. ''Sundays'' is a day of the week (here meaning every Sunday), so the sentence is ''The clinic is closed on Sundays.''', wrong = '{"0": "''In'' is used with longer periods such as months, years, seasons and parts of the day (''in June'', ''in the morning''), not with named days.", "1": "''At'' is used with clock times and short points like ''at noon'' or ''at night''. It is not used with days of the week.", "3": "''By'' means ''not later than'', as in ''Submit it by Sunday''. It does not describe when the clinic is regularly closed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0388' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Accuse'' always takes the preposition ''of'' before the wrongdoing: someone is accused of a crime or fault. So the correct sentence is ''He was accused of cheating in the exam.'' Compare ''charged with'', which uses a different preposition.', wrong = '{"0": "''Accused for'' is a common mistake, mixing it up with ''blamed for'' or ''punished for''. Accuse takes ''of''.", "1": "''With'' goes with ''charged'' (charged with theft), not with ''accused''. ''Accused with'' is incorrect.", "2": "''Accused on'' is not standard English. ''On'' does not follow accuse when naming the wrongdoing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0389' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first clause praises the medicine and the second gives a drawback, so the two ideas contrast. ''However'' is a contrast linker that joins two independent clauses after a semicolon: ''The medicine is effective; however, it has several side effects.''', wrong = '{"1": "''Therefore'' shows a result. Side effects are not a consequence of the medicine being effective, so a result link makes no sense.", "2": "''For example'' introduces an example of the idea before it. Side effects are not an example of effectiveness.", "3": "''Similarly'' shows that two ideas are alike. Here the second idea is a drawback, so it contrasts rather than matches."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0390' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first sentence gives a cause (he did not prepare) and the second gives its effect (he could not answer). A result linker is needed, and ''As a result'' shows exactly this cause-and-effect link between the two sentences.', wrong = '{"0": "''In contrast'' introduces an opposing idea. Failing to answer is what we expect after no preparation, so there is no contrast.", "2": "''Moreover'' adds a further point of the same kind. Here the second sentence is a consequence of the first, not an extra point.", "3": "''For instance'' introduces an example. Failing the questions is a result of not preparing, not an example of it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0391' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Vitamins A, D, E and K'' are examples of the vitamins that dissolve in fat. ''Such as'' introduces examples of the noun just mentioned, so the sentence reads ''Some vitamins dissolve in fat, such as vitamins A, D, E and K.''', wrong = '{"0": "''However'' shows contrast between two ideas. A list of vitamins does not contrast with the first part of the sentence.", "1": "''Therefore'' introduces a result or conclusion. The vitamin names are not a result of anything, they are examples.", "3": "''Although'' introduces a contrasting clause with its own subject and verb. A simple list of names cannot follow it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0392' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The family bought the medicine despite its high cost, which is an unexpected result, so the clause shows concession (contrast). ''Even though'' introduces such a clause: ''Even though the medicine was expensive, his family bought it.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Because'' gives a reason. A high price is not a reason for buying something, so the logic would be wrong.", "1": "''Therefore'' is a linking adverb that shows result. It cannot begin a dependent clause that is joined to a main clause by a comma.", "2": "''In addition'' adds a further point. It cannot introduce a dependent clause, and the idea here is contrast, not addition."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0393' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The lab being closed is the cause, and moving the practical to Friday is the consequence. ''Therefore'' is a result linker and correctly follows the semicolon: ''The lab was closed for repairs; therefore, the practical was moved to Friday.''', wrong = '{"1": "''Nevertheless'' means ''in spite of that'' and shows contrast. Moving the practical is the expected result, not something done despite the closure.", "2": "''For instance'' introduces an example. Moving the practical is not an example of the lab closing.", "3": "''In contrast'' introduces an opposing idea. The two clauses do not oppose each other; one causes the other."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0394' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both sentences list harmful effects of smoking, so the second adds information to the first. ''Furthermore'' is an addition linker meaning ''also'' or ''in addition'', so it fits: ''Furthermore, it raises the risk of heart disease.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Nevertheless'' shows contrast, meaning ''despite this''. Heart disease risk does not contrast with lung damage; both are harms.", "2": "''Instead'' introduces a replacement or alternative. Heart disease does not replace lung damage; it adds to it.", "3": "''Otherwise'' means ''if not'' and introduces what happens when a condition is not met. There is no condition here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0395' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The second clause gives the purpose of the action in the first: the vaccine is kept cold in order to keep its strength. ''So that'' introduces a clause of purpose, giving ''Keep the vaccine refrigerated so that it does not lose its strength.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Even though'' shows contrast or concession. There is no conflict between refrigerating the vaccine and keeping its strength.", "1": "''Unless'' means ''if not''. ''Keep it refrigerated unless it does not lose its strength'' turns the logic upside down.", "3": "''Whereas'' compares two contrasting facts, as in ''A is hot, whereas B is cold''. The second clause here is a purpose, not a contrast."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0396' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The sentence sets Ali''s preference beside his sister''s different preference, so the two clauses contrast. ''Whereas'' links two contrasting facts in one sentence: ''Ali prefers biology, whereas his sister prefers physics.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Because'' would make the sister''s preference the reason for Ali''s preference, which the sentence does not suggest.", "1": "''So'' shows result. His sister''s liking for physics is not a result of Ali liking biology.", "2": "''Therefore'' shows a logical result and normally needs a semicolon or new sentence. There is no cause and effect here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0397' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The sentences give instructions in order: first read each question, then, as the last step, check the answers. ''Finally'' marks the last step in a sequence, so it fits: ''Finally, check your answers before handing in the paper.''', wrong = '{"1": "''However'' introduces a contrast. Checking answers does not oppose reading carefully; it is the next step.", "2": "''Consequently'' shows a result. Checking answers is a separate instruction, not a consequence of reading carefully.", "3": "''For example'' introduces an example. Checking answers is not an example of reading the questions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0398' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correlative pair ''not only ... but also'' adds a second quality to the first, with emphasis. Once ''not only'' appears, it must be completed by ''but also'': ''She was not only intelligent but also hardworking.''', wrong = '{"0": "''And also not'' would deny the second quality and contradict the positive meaning. It is also not a standard partner of ''not only''.", "2": "''Or'' pairs with ''either'' (either ... or). It does not complete ''not only''.", "3": "''Nor'' pairs with ''neither'' (neither ... nor) and is negative. It does not complete ''not only''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0399' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a plural noun that already ends in -s, the possessive is formed by adding only an apostrophe after the s. The results belong to many students, so the correct form is ''The students'' results were announced on Friday.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Student''s'' is singular possessive, which does not suit results of many students, and the comma before ''by the board'' is unnecessary.", "1": "The apostrophe must go on the owner, ''students'', not on the thing owned. ''Results'''' is wrong and ''students'' lacks the possessive mark.", "3": "''Students''s'' adds an extra s. A plural ending in -s takes only an apostrophe, so it should be ''students''''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0400' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Who''s'' is the contraction of ''who is'', which is the meaning needed: ''Who is coming to the seminar?'' Because the sentence is a direct question, it must end with a question mark. Only this option has both the right word and the right end mark.', wrong = '{"0": "''Whose'' is a possessive word, as in ''Whose book is this?''. The sentence needs ''who is'', so ''who''s'' is required.", "1": "The contraction is correct, but a direct question must end with a question mark, not a full stop.", "2": "''Whos'' is missing the apostrophe that marks the dropped letter in ''who is''. The correct contraction is ''who''s''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0401' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Items in a list are separated by commas, and the last two items can be joined by ''and''. No comma is placed between a verb and its object. So ''He bought eggs, milk, bread and butter.'' is correctly punctuated.', wrong = '{"1": "With no commas at all, the list items run together and the reader cannot see where one item ends and the next begins.", "2": "A comma after ''bought'' wrongly separates the verb from its object, the list of things he bought.", "3": "A comma after ''and'' is never needed in a list. ''And'' joins the last two items directly to each other."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0402' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Our family doctor'' renames Dr. Imran; such a phrase is called an appositive. When a non-essential appositive sits in the middle of a sentence, it needs a comma on both sides: ''Dr. Imran, our family doctor, has retired.''', wrong = '{"0": "The comma before the appositive is missing, so ''Dr. Imran our family doctor'' runs together and is not set off properly.", "2": "The closing comma after ''doctor'' is missing. Without it, the extra phrase is not closed off from the verb ''has retired''.", "3": "Both commas around the appositive are missing, so the extra information is not separated from the rest of the sentence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0403' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A colon is used after a complete clause to introduce a list or explanation. ''The patient had three symptoms'' is a complete clause, so the colon goes after it, followed by the list: ''three symptoms: fever, cough and fatigue.''', wrong = '{"0": "A semicolon joins two complete clauses. It is not used to introduce a list, so the colon is needed here.", "1": "A colon should not split a verb from its object. ''The patient had:'' is not a complete clause, and the list still lacks punctuation.", "3": "Semicolons are used between list items only when the items already contain commas. Simple items like these just need commas."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0404' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''The surgery was successful'' and ''the patient was discharged a week later'' are two complete, closely related sentences. A semicolon can join two such clauses without a conjunction, so this option is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Joining two complete sentences with only a comma is a comma splice. A semicolon, a full stop, or a comma plus ''and'' is needed.", "1": "A semicolon already joins the clauses, so adding ''and'' after it is wrong. Use either a comma plus ''and'' or a semicolon alone.", "2": "The semicolon splits the subject ''The surgery'' from its verb, and the two clauses are left without any join."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0405' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the quoted words are a question, the question mark goes inside the closing quotation mark, and it replaces the comma that would normally end the quotation. So the correct form is ''"Where are you going?" asked the nurse.''', wrong = '{"1": "The quoted words are a question, so they must end with a question mark, not a comma.", "2": "The question mark belongs to the quoted words, so it goes inside the closing quotation mark, not after it.", "3": "A question mark already ends the quotation, so adding a comma after it is double punctuation and is wrong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0406' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Brother-in-law'' is a compound noun made of three words that act as one unit. Such compounds are hyphenated throughout, with a hyphen between every part: brother-in-law, mother-in-law, son-in-law.', wrong = '{"0": "Only the second hyphen is present. The compound also needs a hyphen between ''brother'' and ''in''.", "2": "Only the first hyphen is present. The compound also needs a hyphen between ''in'' and ''law''.", "3": "The compound needs both hyphens, and a comma must not separate the subject from its verb ''is''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0407' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A sentence begins with a capital letter, and proper nouns such as days of the week, months and place names always take capitals. So ''Monday'', ''March'' and ''Islamabad'' must all be capitalised, as in this option.', wrong = '{"0": "Everything is in lower case. The first word ''We'' and the proper nouns Monday, March and Islamabad all need capitals.", "1": "''Monday'' is a day of the week, which is a proper noun, so it needs a capital M.", "3": "''March'' is a month and ''Islamabad'' is a city. Both are proper nouns and must start with capitals."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0408' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a dependent clause such as ''Although it was late'' comes before the main clause, a comma separates the two. The comma goes at the end of the dependent clause, just before ''the doctor agreed...''.', wrong = '{"0": "The comma is in the wrong place. It splits the subject ''the doctor'' from its verb ''agreed'' and leaves the clauses unseparated.", "1": "''Although'' is a subordinating conjunction that introduces its clause; it should not be followed by a comma.", "2": "A semicolon joins two independent clauses. ''Although it was late'' is dependent, so a comma is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0409' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''What the doctor said'' has its own subject and verb (''the doctor said''), so it is a clause. It is the subject of the verb ''surprised'', a job done by nouns, so it is a noun clause. A test: replace it with ''it'', and ''It surprised everyone'' still makes sense.', wrong = '{"1": "An adjective clause describes a noun, as in ''the book that I read''. This clause does not describe any noun; it is the subject.", "2": "An adverb clause tells how, when, where or why something happened. This clause names what surprised people, so it acts as a noun.", "3": "A prepositional phrase begins with a preposition and has no subject-verb pair. This group has a subject and verb, so it is a clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0410' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Where my grandparents live'' has a subject and verb, so it is a clause. It tells us which village is meant, so it describes the noun ''village''. A clause that describes a noun is an adjective (relative) clause.', wrong = '{"0": "A noun clause acts as a subject or object. Here the subject is ''the village'', and the clause only describes it.", "2": "An adverb clause modifies a verb, telling when, where or why. This clause follows and describes a noun, not a verb.", "3": "A prepositional phrase has no subject and verb. This group contains ''grandparents live'', so it is a clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0411' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''When you reach the hostel'' has a subject and verb, so it is a clause. It tells us when to call, so it modifies the verb ''call''. A clause that answers ''when?'' about a verb is an adverb clause of time.', wrong = '{"0": "A noun clause works as a subject or object. Here the clause only tells when, so it does not act as a noun.", "1": "An adjective clause describes a noun. This clause does not describe ''me'' or any other noun; it gives the time of calling.", "3": "A noun phrase has no subject and finite verb. This group contains ''you reach'', so it is a clause, not a phrase."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0412' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Walking along the beach'' begins with the present participle ''walking'' and has no subject and no finite verb, so it is a phrase, not a clause. It describes ''she'', the subject of the main clause, which makes it a participial phrase.', wrong = '{"0": "A noun clause needs a subject and a finite verb. ''Walking along the beach'' has neither, so it is not a clause.", "1": "An adverb clause also needs a subject and a finite verb, such as ''While she was walking''. This group has neither.", "2": "An independent clause can stand alone as a sentence. ''Walking along the beach'' cannot stand alone."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0413' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A complex sentence has one main clause and at least one dependent clause. ''She stayed at home'' is the main clause and ''because she was ill'' is a dependent clause introduced by the subordinating conjunction ''because'', so the sentence is complex.', wrong = '{"1": "''She was ill'' and ''she stayed at home'' are both main clauses joined by ''so''. Two joined main clauses form a compound sentence.", "2": "This has one subject, ''She'', with two verbs joined by ''and''. One clause with a compound verb is a simple sentence.", "3": "These are two separate simple sentences, each with its own full stop. There is no dependent clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0414' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A complete sentence needs a subject and a finite verb. ''Running down the corridor with a tray of medicines'' has only a participle, with no subject and no finite verb, so it does not express a complete thought. It is a fragment.', wrong = '{"0": "This has a subject, ''The nurse'', and a finite verb, ''ran'', so it is a complete sentence.", "2": "Here ''Running'' is a gerund acting as the subject, and ''is'' is the finite verb, so the sentence is complete.", "3": "This has a subject, ''She'', and a finite verb phrase, ''was running'', so it is a complete sentence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0415' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An opening participial phrase must describe the subject that comes right after the comma. Bilal is the one who finished the paper early, so ''Bilal'' must be the subject: ''Having finished the paper early, Bilal checked his answers.''', wrong = '{"0": "The subject after the comma is ''the answers'', so the sentence suggests the answers finished the paper. This is a dangling modifier.", "1": "The subject after the comma is ''the invigilator'', so the sentence says the invigilator finished the paper, which is not intended.", "3": "The phrase is attached to ''Bilal''s answers'', so it still suggests the answers finished the paper. The modifier is misplaced."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0416' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a negative or restrictive adverb like ''seldom'' begins a sentence, the auxiliary verb comes before the subject, as in a question. The auxiliary here is ''has'', so the correct order is ''Seldom has the clinic treated so many patients in one day.''', wrong = '{"0": "This keeps normal statement order (subject before auxiliary). After an opening ''seldom'', the auxiliary must come first.", "1": "This uses normal word order with a simple past verb and no inversion, which is wrong after an opening ''seldom''.", "2": "The main verb ''treated'' is placed first and the auxiliary after the subject, which is jumbled and not English word order."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0417' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''No sooner'' at the start of a sentence needs inversion, and the pattern is ''No sooner had + subject + past participle ... than ...''. It shows that one past action happened just before another: ''No sooner had we entered the hall than the fire alarm rang.''', wrong = '{"1": "This keeps normal word order. After an opening ''no sooner'', the auxiliary ''had'' must come before the subject.", "2": "This has no auxiliary and no inversion. The ''no sooner'' pattern needs ''had'' placed before the subject.", "3": "After ''did'' the main verb must be in the base form, so ''did we entered'' is wrong, and this pattern uses ''had'' anyway."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0418' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Rarely'' is a negative adverb, and when it opens a sentence the auxiliary verb moves before the subject. With the present perfect, the auxiliary is ''have'', so the sentence becomes ''Rarely have I met such a skilled surgeon.''', wrong = '{"0": "This keeps normal statement order (subject then auxiliary). An opening ''rarely'' requires the auxiliary to come first.", "2": "This uses normal word order with no auxiliary, so there is no inversion. It would need ''did I meet'' or ''have I met''.", "3": "In modern English only the auxiliary moves before the subject, not the main verb. ''Met I'' is incorrect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0419' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a third conditional, ''If I had known'' can be written with ''if'' dropped and ''had'' moved before the subject: ''Had I known''. The main clause ''I would have left earlier'' confirms a third conditional, so ''Had'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''If I known'' is ungrammatical. With ''if'', the clause needs ''had'': ''If I had known about the strike''.", "1": "''Have I known'' is present perfect in question order. It cannot form the if-clause of a past (third) conditional.", "3": "''Did'' must be followed by the base form (''know''), so ''Did I known'' is wrong, and ''did'' is not used to invert conditionals."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0420' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A negative phrase such as ''under no circumstances'' at the start of a sentence requires inversion: the modal comes before the subject. So the correct form is ''Under no circumstances should you leave the patient alone.''', wrong = '{"0": "This keeps normal word order (subject before modal). An opening negative phrase requires the modal to come first.", "1": "This has normal word order and uses ''will'', which turns a firm instruction into a plain prediction.", "2": "''Under no circumstances'' is already negative, so adding ''not'' creates a double negative and reverses the meaning."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0421' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Is checking'' is present continuous. Its passive is formed with am/is/are + being + past participle. The object ''the papers'' is plural, so it takes ''are'': ''The papers are being checked by the teacher.''', wrong = '{"1": "''Are checked'' is the simple present passive. It loses the idea of an action happening right now.", "2": "''Were being checked'' is the past continuous passive, which changes the time from present to past.", "3": "''Have been checked'' is the present perfect passive. It means the checking is finished, not in progress."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0422' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Will announce'' is simple future. Its passive is will + be + past participle, so it becomes ''will be announced''. The doer ''by them'' is vague and is usually left out: ''The results will be announced tomorrow.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Would be announced'' changes ''will'' to ''would'', which alters the tense and makes the statement less certain.", "2": "''The results will announce'' is active in form, so it wrongly says the results will do the announcing.", "3": "''Are announced'' is simple present passive. It does not keep the future tense of ''will announce''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0423' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With a modal verb, the passive is formed as modal + be + past participle. The modal ''can'' stays the same, and ''solve'' becomes ''be solved'': ''This problem can be solved easily.'' The doer ''by you'' is usually dropped.', wrong = '{"0": "''Could'' changes the modal from ''can'', which changes the meaning slightly. The modal should stay the same in the passive.", "1": "''Can solve'' is active. Without ''be'' + past participle, it suggests the problem solves something.", "3": "After ''be'' the passive needs the past participle ''solved'', not the base form ''solve''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0424' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A command in the active becomes a passive with ''let''. For a negative command, the pattern is ''Let + object + not be + past participle''. So ''Do not touch the wires'' becomes ''Let the wires not be touched.''', wrong = '{"0": "''The wires are not touched'' is a statement of fact, not a command, so it loses the imperative meaning.", "1": "''Should not touch'' is active in form and suggests the wires themselves must not touch something.", "2": "''Let not touch the wires'' has no object after ''let'' and no passive ''be touched'', so it is ungrammatical."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0425' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Has anyone seen'' is a present perfect question. In the passive it becomes has/have + subject + been + past participle, keeping question order. ''My stethoscope'' is singular, so it takes ''has'': ''Has my stethoscope been seen by anyone?''', wrong = '{"1": "''Was ... seen'' is the simple past passive. It does not keep the present perfect tense of ''has seen''.", "2": "''Is ... seen'' is the simple present passive, which changes the tense and suggests a habit.", "3": "''Had ... been seen'' is the past perfect passive. The original uses ''has'', so the tense must stay present perfect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0426' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Destroyed'' is simple past. Its passive is was/were + past participle, and the verb must agree with the new subject. ''Many houses'' is plural, so it takes ''were'': ''Many houses were destroyed by the flood.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Are destroyed'' is the simple present passive. The original action happened in the past, so the tense is wrong.", "2": "''Had been destroyed'' is the past perfect passive, which suggests an earlier past. The original is simple past.", "3": "The tense is right, but ''houses'' is plural and needs ''were''. ''Many houses was'' breaks subject-verb agreement."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0427' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Was written'' is the simple past passive. To make it active, the doer ''Ali'' becomes the subject and the verb returns to the simple past, ''wrote''. So the active sentence is ''Ali wrote the letter.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Writes'' is the simple present tense. The passive ''was written'' is past, so the active must be past too.", "1": "''Has written'' is the present perfect. Its passive would be ''has been written'', not ''was written''.", "3": "''Had written'' is the past perfect. Its passive would be ''had been written'', not ''was written''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0428' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Is being chased'' is the present continuous passive. In the active, the doer ''the police'' becomes the subject and the verb is present continuous. ''Police'' is a plural noun, so it takes ''are'': ''The police are chasing the thief.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Chased'' is the simple past. Its passive would be ''was chased'', not ''is being chased''.", "1": "''Were chasing'' is the past continuous. Its passive would be ''was being chased'', so the tense is wrong.", "2": "''Have chased'' is the present perfect. Its passive would be ''has been chased'', so the tense is wrong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0429' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Will have been examined'' is the future perfect passive, describing an action finished before a future time. The active future perfect is will have + past participle, so the sentence becomes ''The doctor will have examined the patients by noon.''', wrong = '{"1": "''Will examine'' is the simple future. It drops the ''completed before noon'' meaning of the future perfect.", "2": "''Would have examined'' is a conditional form, suggesting something that might have happened. It changes the meaning.", "3": "''Had examined'' is the past perfect, which moves the action into the past instead of the future."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0430' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the active voice the subject performs the action. In ''The nurse gave the injection'', the nurse (subject) does the giving and the injection receives it, so the sentence is active. Passive sentences use be + past participle.', wrong = '{"0": "''Was given'' is be + past participle, and the subject ''the injection'' receives the action. This is simple past passive.", "2": "''Has been given'' is the present perfect passive. The subject ''the injection'' receives the action.", "3": "''Is being given'' is the present continuous passive. The subject ''the injection'' receives the action."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0431' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A yes/no question is reported with ''asked'' + if/whether, and the words go back to statement order (subject before verb). ''Did you see'' is simple past, which shifts back to past perfect: ''He asked me whether I had seen the match.''', wrong = '{"0": "The structure is right, but the simple past ''saw'' should shift back to the past perfect ''had seen'' after a past reporting verb.", "1": "''Did I see'' keeps question word order and the auxiliary ''did''. Reported questions use statement order and need if/whether.", "3": "''Told me that'' reports a statement, not a question. It changes the meaning to him claiming I had seen the match."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0432' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A wh-question is reported with ''asked'' + the same question word, followed by statement order (subject before verb). After the past verb ''asked'', ''are'' shifts back to ''was'', and ''you'' becomes ''I'': ''She asked why I was late.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Why was I late'' keeps question word order. In reported speech the subject comes before the verb: ''why I was late''.", "1": "After the past reporting verb ''asked'', the present ''am'' should shift back to the past ''was''.", "2": "A question is reported with ''asked'', not ''said''. ''She said why I was late'' sounds like an explanation, not a question."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0433' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A command is reported with a verb such as told, ordered or asked, followed by the object and a to-infinitive. For a negative command, ''not'' goes before ''to'': ''The teacher told the students not to make a noise.''', wrong = '{"1": "After ''to'' the verb must be in its base form. ''To not making'' mixes an infinitive with an -ing form and is ungrammatical.", "2": "A command is not reported as a plain statement with ''said''. This also keeps the original wording without any change.", "3": "''That'' introduces a reported statement and cannot be followed by an imperative such as ''do not make a noise''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0434' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Alas'' is an interjection of sorrow, so it is reported as ''exclaimed with sorrow''. The interjection itself is dropped, ''I'' becomes ''he'', and ''have failed'' shifts back to ''had failed'': ''He exclaimed with sorrow that he had failed.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Alas'' expresses sorrow or regret, not joy. ''Hurrah'' would be reported as ''with joy''.", "2": "The interjection ''alas'' should be dropped and replaced by a phrase like ''with sorrow'', and ''has'' should shift to ''had''.", "3": "The speaker is talking about himself, so ''I'' must become ''he'', not stay as ''I''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0435' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In indirect speech after a past reporting verb, the present continuous ''am going'' shifts back to ''was going''. Words of time also change: ''now'' becomes ''then''. The speaker refers to herself, so ''I'' becomes ''she''.', wrong = '{"0": "This keeps the present ''is going'' and the word ''now''. Both should shift: ''was going'' and ''then''.", "1": "The tense has shifted correctly, but ''now'' should change to ''then'' in indirect speech.", "3": "The speaker is talking about herself, so ''I'' must become ''she''. ''I was going'' would wrongly refer to the reporter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0436' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After a past reporting verb, ''will'' becomes ''would'', ''here'' becomes ''there'' and ''tomorrow'' becomes ''the next day''. The speaker''s ''I'' becomes ''he'', and ''you'' (the listener) becomes ''me'' when the listener reports it: ''He said that he would meet me there the next day.''', wrong = '{"0": "Nothing has been changed: ''will'', ''here'' and ''tomorrow'' should become ''would'', ''there'' and ''the next day''.", "1": "''Will'' has changed to ''would'', but ''here'' and ''tomorrow'' are still unchanged. They should be ''there'' and ''the next day''.", "2": "The other changes are right, but ''you'' should become ''me'' because the listener is the one reporting."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0437' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Please'' marks a polite request, so the reporting verb becomes ''requested''. The request is reported as object + to-infinitive, and pronouns change: ''me'' (the speaker) becomes ''her'' and ''your'' (the listener''s) becomes ''my''.', wrong = '{"1": "''Ordered'' reports a command. ''Please'' shows politeness, so the right verb is ''requested'' or ''asked''.", "2": "The reporting verb is right but the pronouns are unchanged. ''Me'' should become ''her'' and ''your'' should become ''my''.", "3": "''Said'' cannot take an object followed by a to-infinitive. ''Said me to'' is ungrammatical."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0438' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After a past reporting verb, ''must'' meaning obligation usually becomes ''had to'', followed by the base form. The listener reports the sentence, so ''you'' becomes ''I'': ''The doctor said that I had to get some rest.''', wrong = '{"0": "A modal like ''must'' is followed by the base form ''get'', never ''got''. ''Must got'' is ungrammatical.", "2": "Nothing has been shifted: ''have to'' should become ''had to'', and ''you'' should become ''I'' when the listener reports it.", "3": "''Had got some rest'' means the rest had already happened. It loses the idea of obligation in ''must''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0439' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Changing back to direct speech reverses the indirect changes. ''Was going'' returns to the present ''are going'', ''I'' (the listener) becomes ''you'', and the question word order and question mark return: ''Where are you going?''', wrong = '{"0": "In direct speech the past ''was going'' should go back to the present ''are going''.", "1": "The listener is being asked about himself, so ''I'' in the report becomes ''you'' in the direct question.", "3": "A direct question needs inverted order: ''are you''. ''Where you are going?'' keeps statement order."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0440' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The subject of the sentence is ''the list'', which is singular. The phrase ''of items'' only describes the list and does not change its number, so the verb must be singular: ''The list of items is on the desk.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Are'' agrees with ''items'', but ''items'' is inside the phrase ''of items''. The real subject ''list'' is singular.", "1": "''Were'' is plural, agreeing wrongly with ''items''. The subject ''list'' needs a singular verb.", "2": "''Have been'' is plural, agreeing wrongly with ''items''. The singular subject ''list'' would need ''has been''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0441' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When ''neither'' is the subject, it means ''not one and not the other'' and is treated as singular in standard written English, even when followed by ''of'' + plural noun. So the verb is singular: ''Neither of the answers is correct.''', wrong = '{"1": "''Are'' is common in speech, but in formal English and exams ''neither'' as a subject takes a singular verb.", "2": "''Were'' is plural and past tense. The subject ''neither'' is singular and the sentence is about the present.", "3": "''Have been'' is plural. A singular subject like ''neither'' would need ''has been'', and the simple present is more natural."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0442' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some nouns end in -s but are singular in meaning. Names of diseases such as measles, mumps and rabies are treated as single conditions and take a singular verb, so ''Measles is a highly infectious disease.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Are'' treats measles as plural. Although it ends in -s, measles is one disease and takes a singular verb.", "2": "''Were'' is plural and past. The sentence states a general fact in the present about one disease.", "3": "''Have been'' is plural. Measles is singular, and a general fact needs the simple present."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0443' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a sum of money, a distance or a period of time is thought of as one total amount, it takes a singular verb. ''Five hundred rupees'' here is one price, so the sentence is ''Five hundred rupees is too much for this book.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Are'' treats the rupees as separate coins or notes. Here they form one price, which takes a singular verb.", "1": "''Were'' is plural and past. The amount is a single sum and the sentence is in the present.", "3": "''Have too much'' makes the rupees possess something, which makes no sense. The sentence needs the linking verb ''is''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0444' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When two different people or things are joined by ''and'', they form a plural subject. ''The doctor'' and ''the nurse'' each have their own ''the'', so they are two persons, and the verb must be plural: ''are on duty tonight.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Is'' is singular. Two separate people joined by ''and'' make a plural subject.", "1": "''Was'' is singular and past. The subject is plural, and ''tonight'' refers to the present or near future.", "2": "''Has been'' is singular. Two people joined by ''and'' need ''have been'', and the sense here is simply ''are''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0445' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When two nouns joined by ''and'' share a single article, they describe one person with two roles. ''The doctor and surgeon'' means one person who is both, so the verb is singular. The present perfect is has + past participle: ''has arrived''.', wrong = '{"1": "''Have'' is plural, which would suit two people (''the doctor and the surgeon''). One article shows one person.", "2": "''Arrive'' forms its perfect with ''have'', not ''be'', so ''are arrived'' is not standard, and ''are'' is plural.", "3": "''Were arrived'' is not standard, since ''arrive'' takes ''have'', and ''were'' is plural, which does not fit one person."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0446' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With ''either ... or'' and ''neither ... nor'', the verb agrees with the subject nearer to it. Here the nearer subject is ''his sisters'', which is plural, so the plural verb ''wash'' is needed. ''Every night'' shows a habit, so the simple present is right.', wrong = '{"0": "''Washes'' is singular and would agree with ''Ali''. The verb should agree with the nearer subject, ''sisters''.", "2": "''Is washing'' is singular and continuous. The nearer subject is plural, and ''every night'' needs the simple present.", "3": "''Has washed'' is singular, agreeing wrongly with ''Ali''. A habit is also shown by the simple present, not the present perfect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0447' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In sentences beginning with ''here'' or ''there'', the verb comes before the subject. The real subject here is ''the reports'', which is plural, so the verb must be plural: ''Here are the reports you asked for.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Is'' is singular. The subject ''the reports'' comes after the verb and is plural.", "1": "''Was'' is singular and past. The subject is plural, and the reports are being handed over now.", "3": "''Has been'' is singular. The subject ''reports'' is plural, and ''Here ... has been'' is not natural English."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0448' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When ''each'' or ''every'' comes before nouns joined by ''and'', each person is considered separately, so the verb is singular. ''Each boy and each girl'' therefore takes ''was'': ''Each boy and each girl was given a certificate.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Were'' is plural. ''Each ... and each'' makes every person a separate single subject, so the verb is singular.", "1": "''Are'' is plural. With ''each'' before the nouns, the verb must be singular.", "2": "''Have been'' is plural. With ''each'' before the nouns, a singular verb is required."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0449' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''The team'' acts as one unit here, so it takes a singular verb. The present perfect is formed with has/have + past participle, and the singular form is ''has'': ''The team has won every match this season.''', wrong = '{"1": "''Are won'' is a passive form, which would mean the team was won by someone. It also changes the meaning completely.", "2": "''Were won'' is a past passive form, which makes the team the thing that was won. The meaning is wrong.", "3": "''Having'' is a participle and cannot be the main verb of a sentence on its own. ''The team having won'' is a fragment."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0450' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is ''definitely''. It comes from ''definite'', which contains the word ''finite'' (f-i-n-i-t-e), and then adds -ly. Remembering ''finite'' inside it helps avoid the common misspelling with ''a''.', wrong = '{"0": "''Definately'' puts an ''a'' where ''i'' belongs. The root word ''finite'' has an ''i'', so it is ''definitely''.", "2": "''Defintely'' drops the ''i'' after ''n''. The full root ''definite'' must be kept: defin-i-tely.", "3": "''Definitly'' drops the ''e'' of ''definite'' before -ly. The correct spelling keeps it: definitely."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0451' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is ''pneumonia'', an infection of the lungs. It begins with a silent ''p'', from the Greek word for lung (pneumon), and the vowels go ''eu'' then ''o-n-i-a''. Compare ''pneumothorax'', which also starts with ''pneu''.', wrong = '{"0": "''Neumonia'' leaves out the silent ''p'' at the start. The word begins with ''pneu-''.", "1": "''Pnuemonia'' swaps the vowels ''e'' and ''u''. The correct order is ''pneu-'', as in ''pneumatic''.", "3": "''Pneumonea'' ends in ''-ea'', but the correct ending is ''-ia'', as in many disease names like anaemia."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0452' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is ''haemorrhage'', meaning heavy bleeding. It has a double ''r'' followed by ''h'' (-rrh-), as in other medical words like ''diarrhoea''. British spelling also keeps the ''ae'' at the start.', wrong = '{"0": "''Haemorrage'' is missing the ''h'' after the double ''r''. The correct form has ''-rrh-''.", "1": "''Haemorhhage'' has a single ''r'' and a double ''h''. The correct form is double ''r'' then one ''h''.", "2": "''Haemorhage'' has only one ''r''. The correct spelling needs a double ''r'' before the ''h''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0453' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is ''questionnaire'', a set of written questions used to collect information. It has a double ''n'' and ends in ''-aire'', from French. Think ''question'' + ''n'' + ''aire''.', wrong = '{"1": "''Questionaire'' has only one ''n''. The correct spelling has a double ''n'': questionnaire.", "2": "''Questionnair'' drops the final ''e''. The ending is ''-aire''.", "3": "''Questionnare'' drops the ''i'' in the ending. The correct ending is ''-aire'', not ''-are''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0454' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is ''conscientious'', meaning careful and hard-working. It is built from ''science'' with ''con-'' in front, then the ending ''-tious'': con-sci-en-tious. Keeping ''sci'' and ''tious'' in mind gives the right spelling.', wrong = '{"0": "''Conscientous'' drops the ''i'' before ''-ous''. The ending must be ''-tious''.", "2": "''Consciencious'' uses ''c'' where ''t'' belongs in the ending. The correct ending is ''-tious''.", "3": "''Concientious'' leaves out the ''s'' after ''con''. The word contains ''sci'', as in ''science''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0455' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is ''maintenance''. Although it comes from the verb ''maintain'', the noun drops the ''ai'' and is spelled main-ten-ance, with ''e'' after ''t'' and ''-ance'' at the end.', wrong = '{"0": "''Maintainance'' keeps the verb spelling ''maintain''. The noun changes to ''mainten-'', giving ''maintenance''.", "1": "''Maintenence'' ends in ''-ence''. The correct ending is ''-ance''.", "3": "''Maintanance'' uses ''a'' after ''t''. The correct middle is ''-ten-'', giving ''maintenance''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0456' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rule ''i before e except after c'' applies here. In ''receive'' the letters come straight after ''c'', so the order is ''ei''. ''Recieve'' breaks this rule, so it is the incorrectly spelled word.', wrong = '{"0": "''Believe'' is correct: there is no ''c'' before the vowels, so ''i'' comes before ''e''.", "1": "''Achieve'' is correct: the letters follow ''h'', not ''c'', so ''i'' comes before ''e''.", "2": "''Piece'' is correct: there is no ''c'' before the vowels, so ''i'' comes before ''e''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0457' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An action that started in the past and is still going on takes the present perfect continuous. A length of time such as ''ten years'' is introduced by ''for'', so ''He has been working here for ten years.'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "The present continuous cannot show an action running from the past up to now, and ''since'' cannot go with a period like ''ten years''.", "2": "The tense is right, but ''since'' needs a starting point such as ''since 2015''. A period of time needs ''for''.", "3": "The simple present does not show duration up to now, and ''from ten years'' is not standard. It needs the perfect tense with ''for''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0458' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ''look forward to'', the word ''to'' is a preposition, not part of an infinitive. A preposition is followed by a noun or a gerund (-ing form), so the correct sentence is ''I look forward to hearing from you.''', wrong = '{"0": "''To hear'' treats ''to'' as an infinitive marker. In this phrase ''to'' is a preposition and needs the gerund ''hearing''.", "2": "The fixed phrase is ''look forward to''. ''Look forward for'' uses the wrong preposition.", "3": "The continuous ''am looking forward'' is fine, but ''to'' still needs the gerund: ''to hearing'', not ''to hear''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0459' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Three rules apply. ''Neither'' pairs with ''nor''. The verb agrees with the nearer subject, ''the students'', which is plural, so it is ''were''. The adjective ''aware'' takes the preposition ''of''. Only this sentence gets all three right.', wrong = '{"0": "''Neither'' must pair with ''nor'', not ''or''. ''Either'' is the word that pairs with ''or''.", "1": "The verb should agree with the nearer subject, ''students'', which is plural, so ''were'' is needed, not ''was''.", "3": "The correct phrase is ''aware of''. ''Aware about'' uses the wrong preposition."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0460' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The verb ''prefer'' uses the pattern ''prefer A to B'', where both A and B have the same form. Here walking and driving are both gerunds, so the correct sentence is ''She prefers walking to driving.'' ''Rather than'' is the other accepted option.', wrong = '{"0": "''Prefer ... than'' is a common error. ''Than'' follows comparatives like ''better'', not ''prefer''.", "1": "''Prefer ... from'' uses the wrong preposition. The standard pattern is ''prefer A to B''.", "2": "''Over to'' is not part of this pattern. One can say ''prefer A over B'', but ''over to'' is wrong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0461' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A comparison must compare like with like. The climate of Murree must be compared with the climate of Lahore, so ''that of Lahore'' is needed, where ''that'' stands for ''the climate''. ''Cooler'' is already comparative, so no ''more'' is added.', wrong = '{"1": "This compares Murree''s climate with the city of Lahore itself, which is an illogical comparison.", "2": "''More cooler'' is a double comparative. ''Cooler'' already means ''more cool'', so ''more'' must not be added.", "3": "''Coolest'' is a superlative and cannot be followed by ''than''. Comparing two things needs the comparative ''cooler''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0462' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Items joined in a list should have the same grammatical form; this is called parallel structure. After ''is'', the list should be three adjectives: kind, patient and skilled. Only this option keeps all three items as adjectives.', wrong = '{"0": "''Has skill'' is a verb phrase, while ''kind'' and ''patient'' are adjectives. The list is not parallel.", "2": "''Has patience'' is a verb phrase between two adjectives, which breaks the parallel pattern.", "3": "''Patiently'' is an adverb, so it cannot describe ''doctor'' after ''is''. The list needs the adjective ''patient''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0463' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Hardly'' already has a negative meaning (almost not). Adding ''not'' or ''-n''t'' creates a double negative, which is incorrect in standard English. So the correct sentence is ''I could hardly hear him.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Couldn''t hardly'' puts two negatives together, the contraction ''-n''t'' and ''hardly''. It is a double negative.", "1": "''Could not hardly'' has both ''not'' and ''hardly'', which is a double negative.", "3": "''Hardly couldn''t'' is a double negative, and ''hardly'' is also in an unusual position before the modal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0464' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The choice between ''a'' and ''an'' depends on the sound, not the letter. In ''hour'' the ''h'' is silent, so the word begins with a vowel sound and takes ''an'': ''She waited for an hour at the clinic.''', wrong = '{"0": "''A'' is used before consonant sounds. ''Hour'' starts with a vowel sound because the ''h'' is silent.", "1": "''Hour'' is a singular countable noun, so it needs an article. ''For hour'' is ungrammatical.", "2": "Two articles cannot be used together. ''The an hour'' is wrong; only ''an'' is needed here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0465' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The choice between ''a'' and ''an'' depends on the first sound, not the first letter. ''European'' starts with a ''yoo'' sound, which is a consonant sound (like ''you''), so it takes ''a'': ''She is a European doctor.''', wrong = '{"1": "''An'' goes before vowel sounds. ''European'' begins with the consonant sound ''y'' (yoo), so ''a'' is needed.", "2": "The article must come before the adjective and noun it introduces: ''a European doctor'', not ''European a doctor''.", "3": "''Doctor'' is a singular countable noun, so it needs an article. ''She is European doctor'' is ungrammatical."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0466' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a question is placed inside another sentence (an embedded or indirect question), it uses statement word order: subject before verb. The whole sentence is a request, not a direct question, so it ends with a full stop: ''Please tell me where the pharmacy is.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Where is the pharmacy'' keeps direct question order. Inside a statement or request, the order becomes ''where the pharmacy is''.", "2": "The word order is right, but the sentence is a polite request, not a direct question, so it should end with a full stop.", "3": "''Does'' and ''is'' are both used as verbs here, which is ungrammatical. An embedded question needs no auxiliary ''does''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0467' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a relative clause, the verb agrees with the word the relative pronoun refers to. ''Who'' refers to ''students'', which is plural, so it must be ''who have passed''. The main verb ''is'' is correct because ''the number of'' takes a singular verb.', wrong = '{"0": "''The number of'' is correct. It refers to a single count, so it takes a singular main verb like ''is''.", "1": "''Students who'' is correct. ''Who'' is the right relative pronoun for people and refers back to ''students''.", "3": "''Is very small'' is correct. Its subject is ''the number'', which is singular, so ''is'' agrees with it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0468' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When ''each'' is the subject, it is singular even if followed by ''of the'' + plural noun. The verb must agree with ''each'', not with ''doctors'', so it should be ''Each of the doctors has a separate consulting room.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Each of the'' is correct. It is the normal way to begin a phrase that refers to every member of a group one by one.", "2": "''A separate'' is correct. The singular article fits because each doctor has one room.", "3": "''Consulting room'' is correct. It is a singular noun phrase matching ''a separate''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0469' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After the auxiliary ''did'' (and ''did not''), the main verb is always in the base form, because ''did'' already carries the past tense. So ''did not went'' is wrong and should be ''She did not go to college yesterday because she was ill.''', wrong = '{"0": "''She did not'' is correct. It is the normal way to make a simple past negative.", "2": "''Yesterday because'' is correct. ''Yesterday'' fits the past tense and ''because'' introduces the reason.", "3": "''She was ill'' is correct. It is a simple past clause giving the reason."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0470' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Cleverest'' is already a superlative (-est), so adding ''most'' makes a double superlative. The correct phrase is either ''the cleverest'' or ''the most clever''. So ''most cleverest'' is the error.', wrong = '{"0": "''He is'' is correct. The subject and verb agree and fit the sentence.", "1": "''One of the'' is correct. It is properly followed by a superlative and a plural noun (''one of the cleverest students'').", "3": "''Students in the class'' is correct. After ''one of the'', the noun must be plural, and it is."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0471' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Since'' is used with a starting point in time (since 2020, since Monday), while ''for'' is used with a length of time. ''Five years'' is a length of time, so the sentence should read ''She has lived in Quetta for five years.''', wrong = '{"0": "''She has'' is correct. The present perfect suits an action that began in the past and continues now.", "1": "''Lived in Quetta'' is correct. It completes the present perfect ''has lived''.", "3": "''Five years'' is correct as a period of time. Only the preposition before it needs changing to ''for''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0472' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Furniture'' is an uncountable noun, so it has no plural form and is never written ''furnitures''. To show quantity, we say ''pieces of furniture''. So the sentence should begin ''The furniture for the new ward arrived last week.''', wrong = '{"1": "''For the new ward'' is correct. It is a prepositional phrase showing who the furniture is for.", "2": "''Arrived'' is correct simple past, matching the time phrase ''last week''.", "3": "''Last week'' is correct. It is a past time expression that fits the simple past verb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0473' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The prefix ''hypo-'' means under or below normal. ''Glyc'' refers to sugar (glucose) and ''-aemia'' to blood, so hypoglycaemia means a blood sugar level that is lower than normal. Compare hypothermia, a body temperature below normal.', wrong = '{"0": "''Above or more than normal'' is the meaning of ''hyper-'', as in hyperglycaemia (high blood sugar).", "1": "''Without'' is the meaning of ''a-'' or ''an-'', as in anaemia or anaesthesia.", "3": "''Around'' is the meaning of ''peri-'', as in pericardium, the sac around the heart."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0474' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Medical words can be decoded from their parts. ''Brady-'' means slow and ''cardia'' refers to the heart, so bradycardia means an unusually slow heart rate, usually under about 60 beats per minute in an adult at rest.', wrong = '{"1": "An unusually fast heart rate is tachycardia, from ''tachy-'' meaning fast. It is the opposite of bradycardia.", "2": "An irregular heartbeat is called an arrhythmia, meaning a loss of normal rhythm, not slowness.", "3": "An enlarged heart is cardiomegaly, from ''-megaly'' meaning enlargement."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0475' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The suffix ''-itis'' means inflammation of the part named before it. So tonsillitis is inflammation of the tonsils and appendicitis is inflammation of the appendix. Other examples are arthritis (joints) and hepatitis (liver).', wrong = '{"0": "Surgical removal is shown by ''-ectomy'', as in tonsillectomy or appendectomy.", "1": "''The study of'' is shown by ''-logy'', as in cardiology or biology.", "2": "A tumour or swelling is shown by ''-oma'', as in carcinoma or lymphoma."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0476' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ''illegible'', the prefix ''il-'' is a form of ''in-'' meaning not, used before words starting with ''l''. Legible means readable, so illegible means not readable. In the other words, il-/im-/in- means into or on, or adds force.', wrong = '{"0": "In ''illuminate'', ''il-'' means upon or into: to shine light on something. It does not mean ''not''.", "2": "In ''immerse'', ''im-'' means into: to dip something into a liquid. It does not mean ''not''.", "3": "In ''inflammable'', ''in-'' adds force or means into; the word means easily set on fire, the same as flammable, not ''not flammable''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0477' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Bi-'' means two and ''lateral'' means relating to a side. So bilateral means on both sides. Bilateral weakness therefore affects both the left and the right sides of the body.', wrong = '{"0": "Weakness on one side only is ''unilateral'', from ''uni-'' meaning one.", "2": "''Many'' is shown by the prefix ''multi-'', as in multiple. ''Bi-'' means two.", "3": "Nothing in ''bilateral'' suggests ''neither''. The prefix ''bi-'' means two, so both sides are affected."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0478' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The root ''chron'' comes from the Greek word for time. A chronic illness lasts a long time, and chronology is the arrangement of events in the order in which they happened. A chronometer is an instrument for measuring time accurately.', wrong = '{"0": "Colour is the root ''chrom'', as in chromosome and monochrome. It looks similar but is a different root.", "1": "Light is the root ''photo'', as in photosynthesis and photograph.", "2": "Heat is the root ''therm'', as in thermometer and thermal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0479' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The prefix ''anti-'' means against or opposing. ''Septic'' relates to infection (sepsis), so an antiseptic is a substance that works against infection by killing or stopping germs. Compare antibiotic and antibody.', wrong = '{"1": "''Before'' is ''ante-'' (with an e), as in antenatal. It is easy to confuse with ''anti-''.", "2": "''After'' is shown by ''post-'', as in postnatal or postoperative.", "3": "''Within'' is shown by ''intra-'' or ''endo-'', as in intravenous or endoscope."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0480' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The suffix ''-able'' means ''capable of being'' and turns a verb into an adjective. Added to ''treat'', it gives ''treatable'', meaning able to be treated. Other examples are ''curable'' and ''readable''.', wrong = '{"0": "''-ment'' makes a noun, not an adjective. ''Treatment'' means the act or method of treating.", "1": "''-er'' makes a noun for a person or thing that does the action, so it cannot mean ''able to be treated''.", "3": "''-ise'' is used to form verbs. ''Treatise'' is actually a noun meaning a long written study of a subject."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0481' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Insomnia'' is formed from ''in-'' meaning not and ''somn'' meaning sleep. Together they mean the condition of not being able to sleep, or not sleeping enough. A drug that causes sleep is similarly called a soporific or hypnotic.', wrong = '{"0": "Sleeping too much is called hypersomnia, from ''hyper-'' meaning above normal.", "1": "Walking while asleep is somnambulism, from ''somn'' (sleep) and ''ambul'' (walk).", "3": "Fear of the dark is nyctophobia. Nothing in ''insomnia'' refers to fear."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0482' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ''bias'', the letters ''b-i'' are simply the start of the word, not a prefix. Bias means an unfair preference for or against something, and has nothing to do with the number two. In the other words, ''bi-'' does mean two.', wrong = '{"1": "In ''bisect'', ''bi-'' means two: to bisect is to cut into two equal parts.", "2": "In ''binocular'', ''bi-'' means two: binocular vision uses both (two) eyes.", "3": "In ''biennial'', ''bi-'' means two: it describes something that happens every two years."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0483' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Insidious'' describes something harmful that develops slowly and quietly, so it is not noticed until damage is done. The sentence says the disease went unnoticed for years while damaging the kidneys, which fits this meaning exactly.', wrong = '{"0": "A sudden and violent illness would be noticed at once, not hidden for years.", "1": "Nothing in the sentence suggests a cure. The disease kept damaging the kidneys.", "3": "The disease went unnoticed, which is the opposite of being openly visible."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0484' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A platitude is a remark that has been said so often that it sounds empty and dull. The clue is that the speech offered ''nothing the audience had not heard a hundred times'', so it was full of tired, overused statements.', wrong = '{"1": "Bold new proposals would be things the audience had not heard before, which contradicts the clue.", "2": "Precise statistics are specific facts. The sentence talks about repeated, familiar remarks, not numbers.", "3": "Nothing in the sentence suggests the speech attacked anyone. The complaint is that it was stale."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0485' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To corroborate a statement is to confirm or support it with more evidence. Two independent witnesses agreed with her account, and that is why the police accepted it. So ''corroborated'' most nearly means ''confirmed''.', wrong = '{"0": "If the witnesses had contradicted her, the police would have doubted her story, not accepted it.", "1": "Witnesses do not exaggerate someone else''s account, and exaggeration would make it less believable.", "2": "Simply recording an account does not give a reason to accept it. The clue is the agreement of the witnesses."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0486' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Equanimity means calmness and an even temper, especially in a difficult situation. During a crisis, only a calm leader would steady the whole team, so the word most nearly means ''calmness under pressure''.', wrong = '{"0": "Technical skill is about ability, not mood. The word ''steadied'' points to an emotional effect from calmness.", "2": "Anger during a crisis would unsettle a team rather than steady it.", "3": "Haste means hurry. Rushing in a crisis would add to the tension, not calm it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0487' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moribund means close to death or near its end, and is used for a place or organisation in final decline. Shut factories and young people leaving describe a town that is dying, so ''close to dying out'' is the meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "The young people have left, so the town is emptying, not overcrowded.", "1": "Nothing in the sentence mentions rebuilding. The factories are shut and the town is declining.", "2": "A dying town may be quiet, but ''moribund'' means near death, not peaceful."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0488' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To mitigate is to make something less harmful, serious or painful. Apologising at once is a way to reduce the harm caused by a mistake, so ''mitigate the damage'' means ''make the damage less severe''.', wrong = '{"1": "He was trying to reduce the damage, not cause it. An apology is meant to repair harm.", "2": "An apology does not measure anything. It is used to lessen harm.", "3": "An apology admits the damage openly, so it is not a way of hiding it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0489' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cursory means quick and not thorough, done without attention to detail. The glance missed a fine fracture that a specialist found later on careful examination, so the first look was hasty and superficial.', wrong = '{"0": "A careful look would have found the fracture. Missing it shows the glance was not careful.", "1": "The expert was the specialist who later found the fracture. The cursory glance was the one that missed it.", "3": "Nothing suggests the X-ray was checked repeatedly. It was a single quick glance."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0490' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To vindicate someone is to prove them right after they have been doubted or blamed. The scientist''s theory had been ridiculed for decades, and new evidence showed it was correct, so ''vindicated'' means ''proved right''.', wrong = '{"0": "Evidence that vindicates someone clears their name, so it reduces embarrassment rather than adding to it.", "1": "Nothing in the sentence suggests another scientist took her place.", "3": "The evidence supported the scientist. It did not punish her."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0491' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Laconic describes speech that uses very few words, sometimes to the point of seeming abrupt. A one-word reply, ''No'', that ends the discussion is a clear example, so the word means ''using very few words''.', wrong = '{"1": "A one-word reply is short, but the sentence gives no sign it was rude or insulting.", "2": "Nothing in the sentence shows anger. The reply is notable for its shortness.", "3": "Long-winded means using too many words, which is the opposite of a single ''No''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0492' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Efficacy means the ability of something, such as a drug, to produce the effect it is meant to have. Large trials are run to show that a drug works, and once it is proven effective, it becomes widely prescribed.', wrong = '{"0": "Proven harmful side effects would make doctors prescribe the drug less, not more.", "1": "Clinical trials test whether a drug works and is safe. They do not show its price.", "2": "Popularity may follow wide prescribing, but trials prove effectiveness, not popularity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0493' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Used as an adjective, ''precipitate'' means done too suddenly and without careful thought. The clue is that the decision was made ''within minutes of the meeting''. Its chemistry meaning, a solid formed in a solution, is a noun and does not fit here.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the chemistry noun meaning a solid that separates from a solution. Here the word is an adjective describing a decision.", "2": "A decision made within minutes was not carefully considered. That is the opposite meaning.", "3": "The decision was made very quickly, not after a long delay."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0494' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Abstruse means hard to understand because it is complex or obscure. Even the senior students, who should know the subject well, struggled to follow the lecture, so ''difficult to understand'' is the meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "A brief lecture is not, for that reason, hard to follow. The problem described is complexity, not length.", "1": "Loudness does not make a lecture hard to understand. The clue is about difficulty of the content.", "2": "An entertaining lecture is usually easier to follow, which does not fit students struggling."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0495' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An equivocal answer is unclear and open to more than one meaning, often on purpose to avoid committing. She would neither confirm nor deny seeing the accused, so her answer was deliberately unclear.', wrong = '{"0": "She refused to confirm or deny anything, so her answer was not clear or firm.", "1": "Nothing shows her answer was a lie. She avoided giving a definite answer at all.", "3": "The sentence says nothing about her emotions. The point is that she avoided a clear reply."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0496' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Apathy means a lack of interest, concern or feeling. The clue is that ''hardly anyone even bothered to read'' the new rules, which shows people did not care about them at all.', wrong = '{"0": "Angry people would at least read the rules in order to object to them.", "1": "Enthusiasm is eager interest, which is the opposite of not bothering to read.", "3": "To be confused by the rules, people would first have to read them. Most did not."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0497' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The relationship is worker : place of work. A doctor works in a hospital, and in the same way a teacher works in a school. So ''School'' completes the analogy, keeping the same link between a person and where that person does the job.', wrong = '{"0": "A student is the person a teacher teaches, not the place where the teacher works.", "1": "A lesson is what a teacher gives. It is a product of the work, not the workplace.", "2": "A book is a tool a teacher uses. It is not the teacher''s place of work."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0498' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The relationship is tool : person who uses it. A scalpel is the cutting tool a surgeon uses during an operation, and a brush is the tool a painter uses to apply paint. So ''Painter'' completes the analogy.', wrong = '{"0": "A canvas is the surface that is painted on. It is not the person who uses the brush.", "2": "Colour (paint) is the material applied with a brush. It is not the user of the tool.", "3": "A gallery is a place where paintings are displayed, not the person who uses a brush."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0499' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The relationship is adjective : organ it describes. ''Pulmonary'' means relating to the lungs, and ''renal'' means relating to the kidneys, as in renal failure. So ''Kidneys'' completes the analogy.', wrong = '{"0": "The adjective for the heart is ''cardiac'', as in cardiac arrest, not ''renal''.", "2": "The adjective for the liver is ''hepatic'', as in hepatic cells, not ''renal''.", "3": "The adjective for the brain is ''cerebral'', as in cerebral haemorrhage, not ''renal''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0500' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The relationship is a severe shortage : the thing that is lacking. A drought is a long period without enough rain, and a famine is a severe shortage of food. So ''Food'' completes the analogy.', wrong = '{"0": "Hunger is a result of famine. The pair needs the thing that is missing, which is food.", "1": "Crop failure may cause a famine, but a famine is defined as a lack of food itself.", "2": "A farmer is a person who grows food. He is not the resource that is lacking."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0501' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The relationship is antonyms. Verbose means using too many words, and concise means using few words, so they are opposites. The opposite of timid (shy and easily frightened) is bold, so ''Bold'' completes the analogy.', wrong = '{"1": "Shy is a synonym of timid. The first pair are opposites, so an antonym is needed.", "2": "Fearful is close in meaning to timid, not opposite to it.", "3": "Quiet is related to timid in meaning. It is not the opposite."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0502' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The relationship is part : whole. A chapter is one section of a book, and a stanza is one section (group of lines) of a poem. So ''Stanza : Poem'' shows the same relationship.', wrong = '{"0": "An author writes a novel. This is maker : product, not part : whole.", "1": "Ink is used to write on a page. A page is not a whole made up of ink in the way a book is made of chapters.", "3": "A title is printed on a cover. This shows location, not a section of a larger whole."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0503' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sardonic describes grim, mocking humour that shows contempt. Smiling when a rival trips on stage shows pleasure at his embarrassment, so the smile is mocking and scornful.', wrong = '{"1": "A warm, kind smile would not fit pleasure at a rival''s fall. It would show sympathy, not scorn.", "2": "Nothing in the sentence suggests he was nervous. He was reacting to his rival''s mistake.", "3": "He clearly understood what had happened, so his smile was not a puzzled one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0504' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Satire is writing that uses humour, irony or exaggeration to criticise the faults of people or institutions, usually to encourage change. Mocking corrupt officials in the hope of reform is a classic purpose of satire.', wrong = '{"0": "An elegy is a sad, serious poem that mourns someone who has died. It does not use humour to expose corruption.", "1": "An ode is a poem written in praise of a person or thing. Satire criticises rather than praises.", "3": "A biography is a factual account of a person''s life. It does not use exaggeration to mock."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0505' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Verbal irony is saying the opposite of what one really means, often to show frustration or humour. Losing 10-0 is a disaster, yet the coach calls it ''brilliant'', so his words mean the reverse of what they say.', wrong = '{"1": "A simile compares two things using ''like'' or ''as''. The coach''s remark contains no comparison.", "2": "Personification gives human qualities to something non-human. Nothing here is treated as a person.", "3": "Alliteration is the repetition of the same first sound in nearby words. ''Well, that went brilliantly'' has no such pattern."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0506' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Situational irony happens when the outcome of a situation is the opposite of what we would expect. A fire station exists to fight fires, so it is the last place we expect to burn down, especially because of its own fire alarm.', wrong = '{"0": "Dramatic irony needs an audience who knows something that a character does not. No such audience or character is involved here.", "1": "Verbal irony is something a person says that means the opposite. Here the irony is in an event, not in words.", "2": "Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration for effect. The event is described plainly, with nothing exaggerated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0507' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Connotation is the feeling a word carries beyond its basic meaning. All four words mean thin, but ''scrawny'' suggests unhealthily thin, weak and bony, so it has the most negative connotation.', wrong = '{"0": "''Slim'' is a positive word, often used as a compliment for an attractive, healthy thinness.", "2": "''Slender'' is positive, suggesting a graceful, elegant thinness.", "3": "''Lean'' is neutral to positive, suggesting someone fit and healthy with little fat."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0508' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To lampoon someone is to mock or ridicule them publicly, usually in satirical writing or cartoons. Cartoonists targeting a mayor''s endless broken promises are making fun of him to criticise him, so the word means ''to mock publicly through satire''.', wrong = '{"1": "Cartoonists who keep pointing at broken promises are criticising the mayor, not praising him.", "2": "Nothing in the sentence suggests a formal interview. Cartoonists express views through drawings.", "3": "Cartoonists do not elect anyone. Voters elect officials, and the sentence is about mockery."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0509' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All four words describe confidence, but they differ in connotation. ''Conceited'' means having too high an opinion of oneself and being proud in an unpleasant way, so it is the negative one.', wrong = '{"0": "''Self-assured'' is positive: it describes someone who is calmly confident in their own abilities.", "1": "''Assertive'' is usually positive: it describes someone who states their views confidently without being aggressive.", "3": "''Poised'' is positive: it suggests calm, graceful self-control, especially under pressure."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0510' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Obdurate means stubbornly refusing to change one''s opinion or behaviour, even when asked or persuaded. So the closest meaning is ''stubborn''. An obdurate patient, for example, refuses to follow advice no matter what is said.', wrong = '{"1": "''Obedient'' means willing to do what one is told, which is close to the opposite of obdurate.", "2": "''Obscure'' means unclear or little known. It only looks similar because both begin with ''ob-''.", "3": "''Durable'' means long-lasting. It only shares the letters ''dur'' with obdurate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0511' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ephemeral means lasting for a very short time, like a mayfly or a passing fashion. The word most nearly opposite is ''permanent'', which means lasting for ever or for a very long time.', wrong = '{"0": "''Brief'' means short in time, which is a synonym of ephemeral, not its opposite.", "1": "''Ethereal'' means delicate, light and almost unearthly. It only sounds similar to ephemeral.", "3": "''Fragile'' means easily broken. It shares a sense of delicacy but is not the opposite of short-lived."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0512' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Loquacious means very talkative, someone who talks a great deal. Its opposite is ''taciturn'', which describes a person who says very little and keeps their thoughts to themselves.', wrong = '{"0": "''Talkative'' has the same meaning as loquacious, so it is a synonym, not an opposite.", "1": "''Eloquent'' means speaking fluently and persuasively. It describes skill with words, not the opposite of talkativeness.", "3": "''Lucid'' means clear and easy to understand. It is not related to how much a person talks."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0513' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Enervate means to drain someone of energy or strength, so the closest meaning is ''weaken''. For example, a long fever can enervate a patient. The word is often wrongly guessed to mean ''energise'' because the two look alike.', wrong = '{"1": "''Energise'' means to fill with energy, which is nearly the opposite. The two words only look similar.", "2": "''Irritate'' means to annoy. It has nothing to do with draining energy.", "3": "Despite looking like ''nerve'', enervate does not mean to make nervous. It means to weaken."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0514' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Parsimonious means extremely unwilling to spend money or use resources, almost to the point of meanness. Its opposite is ''generous'', which describes someone who freely gives money, time or help to others.', wrong = '{"1": "''Stingy'' means unwilling to spend or give, which is a synonym of parsimonious.", "2": "''Poor'' means having little money. It describes how much one has, not one''s attitude to spending.", "3": "''Cautious'' means careful to avoid risk. It is related in feeling but is not the opposite of mean with money."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0515' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Pragmatic means dealing with problems in a sensible, realistic way based on what works in practice, rather than on theories or ideals. So the closest meaning is ''practical''.', wrong = '{"0": "''Idealistic'' means guided by high ideals that may not be realistic, which is roughly the opposite of pragmatic.", "1": "''Dogmatic'' means holding beliefs rigidly without considering evidence. It is not the same as being practical.", "3": "''Theoretical'' means based on ideas rather than practice, which is the opposite of practical."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0516' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To ostracise someone is to deliberately exclude them from a group or society and refuse to accept them. So the closest meaning is ''exclude''. For example, a student may be ostracised by classmates after a quarrel.', wrong = '{"0": "''Praise'' means to express approval, which is roughly the opposite of shutting someone out.", "1": "''Examine'' means to look at closely. It has nothing to do with excluding someone.", "2": "''Imitate'' means to copy someone''s actions or speech. It has nothing to do with shutting someone out."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0517' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Waive'' means to give up a right or to choose not to apply a rule or charge. The judge decided not to charge the late fee because the delay was not the student''s fault, so ''waive the late fee'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Wave'' sounds the same but means to move the hand to and fro, as in greeting. It does not fit a fee.", "2": "''Waver'' means to be unsure or to change between choices. One cannot ''waver a fee''.", "3": "''Wane'' means to become smaller or weaker, like the moon. It does not take an object like ''the fee''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0518' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Circumspect means cautious and careful to consider all facts before acting or deciding. The scientist refused to draw any conclusion until more data came in, which describes exactly this careful attitude.', wrong = '{"0": "''Reckless'' means acting without thinking about risks. A reckless scientist would jump to conclusions.", "2": "''Jubilant'' means overjoyed. It does not fit someone holding back judgement.", "3": "''Indifferent'' means not caring. Waiting for more data shows real interest in getting the answer right."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0519' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Frivolous means not serious, silly or lacking real purpose, as in ''a frivolous complaint'' that wastes the court''s time. Its opposite is ''serious'', which describes something important, sensible or thoughtful.', wrong = '{"1": "''Silly'' has much the same meaning as frivolous, so it is a synonym, not an opposite.", "2": "''Trivial'' means unimportant, which is close in meaning to frivolous.", "3": "''Generous'' means willing to give. It is unrelated to how serious something is."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0520' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gregarious means fond of company and enjoying being with other people. A gregarious student enjoys group work, parties and social events rather than being alone, so the closest meaning is ''sociable''.', wrong = '{"0": "''Greedy'' means wanting more than one needs. It only sounds a little similar.", "1": "''Solitary'' means preferring to be alone, which is the opposite of gregarious.", "2": "''Aggressive'' means ready to attack or argue. It has nothing to do with liking company."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0521' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Conspicuous means easy to see or notice because it stands out, like a bright red coat in a crowd of dark clothes. The opposite is ''unnoticeable'', meaning not easily seen or noticed at all.', wrong = '{"0": "''Obvious'' means easy to see or understand, which is a synonym of conspicuous.", "2": "''Conscious'' means aware or awake. It only sounds similar to conspicuous.", "3": "''Suspicious'' means causing or feeling distrust. It only shares a similar ending."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0522' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ameliorate means to make a bad or unpleasant situation better. For example, painkillers can ameliorate a patient''s discomfort, and better roads can ameliorate traffic problems. So the closest meaning is ''improve''.', wrong = '{"0": "''Worsen'' means to make something worse, which is the opposite of ameliorate.", "2": "''Amputate'' means to cut off a limb surgically. It only starts with similar letters.", "3": "''Ignore'' means to pay no attention to something. It is unrelated to making things better."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0523' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Affluent means having a lot of money, that is, wealthy, as in ''an affluent neighbourhood''. Its opposite is ''impoverished'', which means very poor and lacking the money needed for basic needs such as food and shelter.', wrong = '{"0": "''Wealthy'' means having much money, which is a synonym of affluent.", "1": "''Fluent'' means able to speak or write easily. It only shares letters with affluent.", "2": "''Influential'' means having power over others. Wealth and influence often go together, but it is not an opposite."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0524' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Tenacious means holding firmly to a goal and refusing to give up, even when things are difficult. A tenacious student keeps trying after failing. So the closest meaning is ''persistent''.', wrong = '{"1": "''Tender'' means gentle or soft. It only sounds a little similar to tenacious.", "2": "''Tense'' means stretched tight or nervous. It only begins with similar letters.", "3": "''Timid'' means shy and easily frightened, which is almost the opposite in spirit of a determined person."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0525' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Imagery is classified by the sense it appeals to. ''Sizzle'' is the hissing sound food makes when it fries in hot oil, so the line mainly appeals to hearing. This is called auditory imagery, and ''sizzle'' is also an example of onomatopoeia.', wrong = '{"0": "The line does not describe colours, shapes or light. Nothing is said about what the onions look like.", "1": "Nothing describes how something feels against the skin, such as heat or texture.", "3": "The onions are being cooked, not eaten, so the line does not describe any flavour."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0526' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Scent'' means a smell, especially a pleasant one, and jasmine is a strongly perfumed flower. The air being ''heavy'' with scent stresses how strong the smell is, so the line mainly appeals to smell (olfactory imagery).', wrong = '{"1": "No sound is described in the line, so it does not appeal to hearing.", "2": "''Sweet'' here describes the scent, not a flavour in the mouth. Nothing is being eaten.", "3": "The jasmine flowers are not described by colour or shape, so the focus is not on sight."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0527' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Rough'' describes texture, and ''scraped against her bare palms'' describes how the bark feels on her skin as she climbs. Both appeal to the sense of touch, which is called tactile imagery.', wrong = '{"0": "Roughness could be seen, but the line focuses on the scraping felt by her palms, not on how the bark looks.", "1": "No smell is mentioned. The line is about the feel of bark on skin.", "2": "No sound is described. Scraping is presented as a feeling on her palms, not as a noise."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0528' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Taste (gustatory) imagery describes flavours experienced in the mouth. ''Bitter'', ''tang'' and ''tongue'' all point to taste, so the line about bitter tea leaving a sharp tang on his tongue appeals mainly to taste.', wrong = '{"0": "Ringing bells across a valley describe a sound, so this line appeals to hearing.", "2": "Golden light spilling over hills describes colour and brightness, so this line appeals to sight.", "3": "An icy wind stinging the cheeks describes a feeling on the skin, so this line appeals to touch."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0529' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Silver moonlight'' paints a picture of colour and light, which is visual imagery. Moonlight lying ''quietly'' and fields that are ''sleeping'' give human actions to non-human things, which is personification. So the line combines these two devices.', wrong = '{"0": "There is no comparison using ''like'' or ''as'', so there is no simile, and nothing is exaggerated, so there is no hyperbole.", "2": "No word imitates a sound, so there is no onomatopoeia, and there is no clear run of repeated first sounds.", "3": "Nothing is said that means the opposite, and no statement contradicts itself, so there is no irony or paradox."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0530' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sensory imagery uses descriptive words that appeal to sight, sound, smell, taste and touch. Its main purpose is to let the reader experience the scene, seeing, hearing or feeling it, rather than simply being told about it.', wrong = '{"0": "Imagery adds vivid detail, which usually makes writing richer, not shorter. Brevity is not its purpose.", "1": "Proving an argument relies on evidence and reasoning. Imagery appeals to the senses, not to logic.", "2": "Rhyme is a pattern of matching sounds at line ends. It is unrelated to sensory imagery."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0531' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first sentence states that antibiotics have no effect on viruses, ''which is why they do not cure the common cold''. This shows that the common cold is caused by a virus, so antibiotics cannot treat it.', wrong = '{"0": "The passage does not say the cold is too mild. It gives a different reason: antibiotics do not work against viruses.", "1": "No law or rule against prescribing antibiotics for colds is mentioned in the passage.", "3": "The passage does not say the cold is caused by bacteria at all. It links the cold to viruses."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0532' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage says that when antibiotics are ''stopped too early'', the hardiest bacteria survive, multiply and pass on their resistance. Stopping a course as soon as one feels better is exactly this mistake, so it is most likely to increase resistance.', wrong = '{"1": "Finishing the full course is what doctors advise in the passage to prevent resistance from developing.", "2": "A cold is caused by a virus, so avoiding antibiotics for it prevents needless use and reduces resistance.", "3": "The passage presents limiting antibiotics in farm animals as a way to reduce the spread of resistant bacteria."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0533' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the passage, the ''hardiest'' bacteria are the ones that survive when antibiotics are misused, and they go on to pass on resistance. Bacteria that survive a drug must be the strongest, so ''hardiest'' means ''toughest''.', wrong = '{"0": "The weakest bacteria would be killed by the drug. The passage describes the ones that survive.", "1": "The age of the bacteria is not discussed. The word refers to their ability to survive.", "2": "The size of the bacteria is not discussed. ''Hardy'' refers to toughness, not size."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0534' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage first explains that antibiotics do not work on viruses, then describes how overuse or stopping early lets resistant bacteria survive and spread, and finally gives ways to prevent this. So it is mainly about how antibiotic misuse leads to resistant bacteria.', wrong = '{"0": "The common cold is mentioned only once, as an example. The passage does not explain how viruses cause it.", "2": "No dates, discoveries or history of antibiotics are given in the passage.", "3": "Farm animals appear only in the last sentence, as one more source of resistance. Farming is not the main topic."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0535' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The second sentence of the passage states that in summer, meltwater from the northern glaciers feeds the Indus and its tributaries. This water then supplies farms hundreds of kilometres downstream.', wrong = '{"0": "Monsoon rain is not mentioned anywhere in the passage. The summer source named is glacier meltwater.", "2": "Underground springs are not mentioned anywhere in the passage. The summer source named is meltwater.", "3": "Dams are not mentioned in the passage. The water comes from melting glaciers."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0536' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage says that if the wall of a glacial lake gives way, the lake ''can empty in minutes'' and the flood ''gives villagers little warning''. Because these floods arrive so suddenly, a system that warns people early can give them time to escape.', wrong = '{"0": "The passage says the glaciers are shrinking as temperatures rise, not growing larger.", "1": "Warning systems alert people so they can escape. They do not stop a lake wall from breaking.", "2": "Farmers'' need for water is a separate point about meltwater. It is not the reason warnings are useful."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0537' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the passage, if a wall of rock and ice ''gives way'', the lake behind it empties in minutes. For the water to escape, the wall must break or fall down, so ''gives way'' here means ''collapses''.', wrong = '{"1": "''Give way'' can mean letting others go first, as in traffic, but a wall of rock and ice cannot move aside politely.", "2": "A stronger wall would hold the water back. The lake empties, so the wall must have failed.", "3": "Freezing would keep the water in place. It would not release a sudden flood."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0538' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage links rising temperatures to shrinking glaciers, new lakes behind loose walls, and sudden floods that give villagers little warning. So the writer would agree that rising temperatures can make life in mountain valleys more dangerous.', wrong = '{"0": "The passage says glaciers are shrinking as temperatures rise, not growing.", "1": "The passage describes these floods happening in Pakistan''s northern valleys, not only in polar regions.", "3": "The passage says meltwater feeds the Indus and supplies water to farms far downstream, so it is clearly useful."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0539' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Quotation marks around a single word can show that the writer is repeating someone else''s label without agreeing with it. The writer calls the placebo a ''nuisance'' and then shows evidence that it has real effects, so the marks signal doubt about that earlier view.', wrong = '{"0": "No scientist is named or quoted in the passage. The marks repeat a common earlier attitude.", "1": "''Nuisance'' is an everyday word, not a medical term, so the marks are not there to highlight technical language.", "2": "The passage argues that placebos matter, so the writer is not stressing how annoying they are."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0540' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage directly states that the placebo effect is strongest for symptoms that the brain itself helps to produce, and gives pain, nausea and fatigue as examples. This matches the first option exactly.', wrong = '{"1": "The passage says belief cannot shrink a tumour, so tumours are where the effect is weakest, not strongest.", "2": "The passage says belief cannot mend a broken bone, so broken bones are not where the effect is strongest.", "3": "The passage stresses that the effect has limits and varies, so it does not act equally on all illnesses."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0541' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The writer says plainly that the findings do not mean ''belief can shrink a tumour''. So the statement that belief alone can make a tumour shrink is the one the writer would reject. The other three statements are supported by the passage.', wrong = '{"0": "The writer would accept this. The passage reports brain scans showing that the body releases natural painkillers.", "1": "The writer would accept this. The conclusion says the confident doctor and careful explanation may be part of treatment.", "3": "The writer would accept this. The first sentence says the placebo''s only job was to show how much better a real drug worked."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0542' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Decoration is added for appearance and does not change how something works, and ''mere'' stresses that it is only that. So calling the ritual of care ''mere decoration'' shows it was once thought to have no real effect on the patient''s outcome.', wrong = '{"0": "Decoration is seen as unimportant, not harmful. Nothing suggests the ritual of care was thought to hurt patients.", "1": "Cost is not discussed anywhere in the passage, so ''too expensive'' has no support.", "3": "The phrase shows the ritual was not taken seriously before. It was certainly not regarded as scientifically proven."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0543' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The writer gives evidence that placebos have real effects, then sets limits (they cannot shrink tumours or mend bones), and ends by saying the ritual of care may be part of treatment. So the main purpose is to argue the effect is real but limited, and that the manner of care matters.', wrong = '{"1": "The writer clearly limits the effect, saying belief cannot shrink a tumour or mend a bone. Placebos cannot cure any disease.", "2": "No criticism of doctors appears. The writer actually suggests that a confident, caring doctor helps patients.", "3": "Drug trials are mentioned only as evidence for the placebo effect. Their methods are not explained step by step."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0544' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage states that earlier thinkers argued about vision mainly through reasoning, and that ''some held that the eye sends out rays that touch the objects it sees''. This is the earlier belief Ibn al-Haytham set out to test.', wrong = '{"0": "This was Ibn al-Haytham''s own conclusion from his experiments, which replaced the earlier view.", "1": "Curved paths of light are not mentioned. Ibn al-Haytham showed that light travels in straight lines.", "2": "The upside-down image is mentioned as a result of Ibn al-Haytham''s experiment on a wall, not as an earlier belief."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0545' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The passage describes the experiment directly: light from outside came through a small hole in a darkened room, travelled in straight lines and formed an inverted (upside-down) image on the opposite wall. So the experiment showed both the straight-line path and the inverted image.', wrong = '{"0": "The experiment led Ibn al-Haytham to reject this idea. He concluded that light enters the eye instead.", "2": "Light bending around corners contradicts the result. The experiment showed that light travels in straight lines.", "3": "The passage says the image on the wall was inverted, meaning upside down, not the right way up."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0546' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Here ''elegant'' describes ''the argument'', not a person. When used of an argument or solution, elegant means neat, simple and pleasing in its reasoning. The writer''s point is that even an attractive argument must still be tested by observation.', wrong = '{"0": "''Fashionably dressed'' is the sense used for people and clothes. An argument cannot be dressed.", "2": "''Elegant'' does not mean long. An elegant argument is usually simple and neat rather than lengthy.", "3": "''Foreign'' has no connection with the word ''elegant'' in any of its senses."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0547' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The writer calls Ibn al-Haytham ''a pioneer of the experimental method'' and says his habit of testing theories is the habit ''on which modern science rests''. This is strong praise, so the writer''s attitude is admiring.', wrong = '{"0": "A sceptical writer would doubt his achievements. This writer presents them as facts and praises them.", "1": "There is no ridicule or mockery anywhere in the passage. The tone is respectful.", "2": "An indifferent writer would not care either way, but the final sentence gives him a central place in modern science."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0548' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The final sentence says that insisting a theory ''must answer to observation'' is the habit on which modern science rests. For this to make sense, the writer must assume that modern science values testing ideas against what is actually observed.', wrong = '{"1": "The writer says earlier thinkers relied on reasoning and some held a mistaken view, but never calls all of them foolish.", "2": "The writer stresses testing, not rejecting reasoning. A good argument still matters but must be checked by observation.", "3": "Cairo is mentioned only as where he worked. Nothing suggests it is still the centre of world science."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0549' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Metonymy is calling something by the name of a thing closely linked with it. The White House is the official home of the US President, so the name is used to stand for the US government. A building cannot announce sanctions; the government does.', wrong = '{"0": "Personification gives human qualities to non-human things for effect. Here the point is that a related place-name stands in for the government.", "1": "Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration. Nothing in the sentence is exaggerated.", "3": "A simile compares two things using ''like'' or ''as''. There is no such comparison here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0550' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Synecdoche is a figure of speech in which a part stands for the whole. Sailors work with their hands, so ''hands'' (a part of the body) is used to mean the sailors themselves (the whole person). ''All hands on deck'' means all sailors.', wrong = '{"0": "A simile compares two things using ''like'' or ''as''. No comparison is made here.", "1": "Alliteration is the repetition of the same first sound in nearby words. The phrase has no such pattern.", "3": "Irony means saying the opposite of what is meant. The captain''s words mean exactly what they say."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0551' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An oxymoron places two words with opposite or contradictory meanings side by side for effect. Silence means no sound, so it cannot literally be ''deafening''. The phrase describes a silence so complete that it feels overwhelming.', wrong = '{"1": "''Silent night'' joins two words that fit together naturally. There is no contradiction.", "2": "''Loud noise'' joins words that agree in meaning, so it is not an oxymoron.", "3": "''Cold ice'' joins words that agree in meaning. Ice is naturally cold, so there is no contradiction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0552' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A paradox is a statement that seems to contradict itself but contains a deeper truth. Learning more should make us feel we know more, yet it shows how much is still unknown. The statement sounds contradictory but is true, so it is a paradox.', wrong = '{"0": "An oxymoron is two contradictory words placed together, such as ''deafening silence''. This is a whole statement, not a word pair.", "1": "A simile compares two things using ''like'' or ''as''. There is no such comparison here.", "2": "Onomatopoeia is a word that imitates a sound, such as ''buzz''. No word here imitates a sound."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0553' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Understatement is deliberately describing something as much smaller or less serious than it really is, often for humour. Running a marathon in 40-degree heat would leave anyone exhausted, so calling herself ''a little tired'' is understatement.', wrong = '{"0": "Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration. Here she does the opposite, making her tiredness sound smaller.", "2": "A simile compares two things using ''like'' or ''as''. No comparison is made.", "3": "Personification gives human qualities to non-human things. She is describing herself, a person."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0554' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Dramatic irony occurs when the audience knows something important that a character on stage does not. The audience knows the cup holds poison while the king drinks happily, unaware. This gap in knowledge creates tension, which is dramatic irony.', wrong = '{"0": "Situational irony is an outcome that is the opposite of what is expected. Here the key point is the gap in knowledge between audience and character.", "2": "Verbal irony is saying the opposite of what one means. No one in the scene says anything ironic.", "3": "Satire uses humour or exaggeration to mock the faults of people or society. The scene is not mocking anyone."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0555' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With ''neither ... nor'' and ''either ... or'', the pronoun agrees with the nearer antecedent (the noun it refers to). The nearer one is ''the junior doctors'', which is plural, so the plural pronoun ''their'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Her'' would agree with ''the head nurse'', but that is the farther antecedent. The pronoun follows the nearer, plural one.", "1": "''His'' is singular. The nearer antecedent, ''the junior doctors'', is plural and needs ''their''.", "2": "''Its'' is used for things or animals, not people, and it is singular."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0556' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ''She likes you more than I do'', the word ''do'' stands for ''like you'', so the meaning is clearly ''more than I like you''. Adding ''do'' after ''I'' makes ''I'' the subject of a second, understood verb and removes any doubt.', wrong = '{"1": "With the object pronoun ''me'', this most naturally means ''more than she likes me'', which is a different meaning.", "2": "This compares how much she likes you with how much she likes me, which is a different comparison.", "3": "''Me'' is an object pronoun and cannot be the subject of ''do''. ''Me do'' is ungrammatical."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0557' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Between'' is a preposition, and a pronoun after a preposition must be in the object case. Both pronouns after ''between'' are objects, so it is ''between you and me'', not ''between you and I''.', wrong = '{"0": "''I'' is a subject pronoun. After the preposition ''between'', the object form ''me'' is needed.", "1": "''Myself'' is reflexive and needs ''I'' as the subject of the same clause to refer back to. There is none here.", "3": "''Mine'' is a possessive pronoun meaning ''belonging to me''. It does not fit after ''between you and''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0558' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To find the right case, remove the inserted words ''I thought'': ''The doctor who would operate was on leave.'' The pronoun is the subject of ''would operate'', so the subject form ''who'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "''Whom'' is the object form. Here the pronoun is the subject of ''would operate'', so ''who'' is needed.", "2": "''Whose'' shows possession, as in ''the doctor whose patient''. Nothing is owned here.", "3": "''Which'' is used for things and animals, not for people like a doctor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0559' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The whole clause ''whoever solves the puzzle first'' is the object of ''to''. But inside that clause, the pronoun is the subject of ''solves''. The case is set by its job inside the clause, so the subject form ''whoever'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Whomever'' is the object form. Inside its clause, the pronoun is the subject of ''solves'', so ''whoever'' is needed.", "1": "''Whom'' is an object form and cannot be the subject of ''solves''. It also lacks the meaning ''any person who''.", "2": "As a pronoun meaning ''any person who'', ''whoever'' is written as one word, not ''who ever''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0560' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A pronoun reference is unclear when a pronoun could point to more than one noun. In ''When Ali met Bilal, he was nervous'', ''he'' could mean either Ali or Bilal, so the reader cannot be sure who was nervous.', wrong = '{"0": "The name ''Ali'' is repeated instead of using ''he'', so it is clear who was nervous.", "2": "''He'' naturally refers back to ''Ali'', the subject of the sentence, and Bilal is named only after it.", "3": "Both people are named with their own feelings, and ''they'' clearly means both of them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0561' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A reflexive pronoun (myself, herself, themselves) is used when it refers back to the subject of the same clause. In ''The surgeon reminded herself'', ''herself'' refers to ''the surgeon'', the subject, so the reflexive is used correctly.', wrong = '{"0": "There is no ''I'' in this clause for ''myself'' to refer back to. The object form ''me'' is needed: ''to Sara or me''.", "2": "A subject needs the subject pronoun ''I'': ''My brother and I attended''. ''Myself'' cannot be a subject.", "3": "''Themselves'' cannot be part of a subject. A reflexive pronoun must refer back to an earlier subject."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0562' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The clause ''I borrowed from the library'' is set off by commas, so it is a non-defining clause that just adds extra information. Such clauses use ''which'' for things, never ''that'', so ''which'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''That'' is used only in defining clauses without commas. It cannot introduce a non-defining clause.", "1": "''Who'' is used for people. A textbook is a thing, so ''which'' is needed.", "3": "''What'' means ''the thing that'' and cannot follow a noun to start a relative clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0563' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Whose'' is the possessive relative pronoun, meaning ''of whom'' or ''belonging to whom''. The X-ray belonged to the patient, so ''The patient whose X-ray was missing'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Who''s'' is a contraction of ''who is'' or ''who has''. ''The patient who is X-ray was missing'' makes no sense.", "1": "''Whom'' is an object pronoun. It cannot show that the X-ray belonged to the patient.", "3": "''Which'' is used for things, not people. For possession with people, ''whose'' is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0564' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Inside the clause, ''the panel'' is the subject and ''selected'' is the verb, so the relative pronoun is the object of ''selected'' (the panel selected him or her). Formal written English uses the object form ''whom''.', wrong = '{"0": "''Who'' is the subject form. It is common in speech, but formal English uses ''whom'' for the object of a verb.", "1": "''Whose'' shows possession, as in ''the candidate whose papers''. Nothing is owned here.", "3": "''Which'' is used for things, not for people like a candidate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0565' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Let'' is a verb that takes an object, so the pronouns after it must be in the object case. ''You'' works for both cases, and the first-person object form is ''me'', so ''Let you and me settle this matter ourselves'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''I'' is a subject pronoun. As the object of ''let'', the form must be ''me''.", "1": "''Myself'' needs ''I'' as the subject of the same clause. Here it would wrongly replace the object ''me''.", "3": "''Mine'' is a possessive pronoun meaning ''belonging to me''. It cannot be the object of ''let''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0566' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Except'' works as a preposition here, meaning ''but'' or ''not including''. A pronoun after a preposition takes the object case, so the correct form is ''Everyone except him passed the test.''', wrong = '{"1": "''He'' is a subject pronoun. After the preposition ''except'', the object form ''him'' is needed.", "2": "''His'' is a possessive form, as in ''his book''. It does not fit after ''except'' here.", "3": "''Himself'' is reflexive and needs a matching subject like ''he'' in the same clause. There is none."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0567' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A reflexive form placed just after a noun can be used for emphasis; this is called an emphatic or intensive pronoun. ''The principal herself handed out the prizes'' stresses that she did it in person, which is why the students were delighted.', wrong = '{"0": "''Her'' is an object or possessive form and cannot be placed after the subject to add emphasis.", "1": "''Hers'' is a possessive pronoun meaning ''belonging to her''. It does not fit after ''the principal''.", "2": "''She'' repeats the subject unnecessarily. ''The principal she handed out'' is not correct English."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0568' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A wish about the present describes something unreal now, so it uses the past simple, even though the time is present. ''I wish I knew the answer now'' means ''I do not know it, and I would like to.''', wrong = '{"0": "The present simple ''know'' is not used after ''wish''. An unreal present wish takes the past simple.", "2": "''Will know'' is not used after ''I wish'' for a present wish. It cannot express an unreal situation now.", "3": "The present perfect ''have known'' is not used after ''wish''. A past regret would need ''had known''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0569' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A wish about the past expresses regret for something that did or did not happen, and it uses the past perfect. The time phrase ''last year'' shows the past, so ''She wishes she had studied harder last year'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Studied'' (past simple) after ''wish'' refers to an unreal present, so it does not fit ''last year''.", "1": "The present perfect ''has studied'' is not used after ''wish'', and it cannot go with ''last year''.", "2": "''Would study'' after ''wish'' refers to a desired future change or someone else''s habit, not a past regret."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0570' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''If I were you'' describes an imaginary present situation (I am not you), which is a second conditional. In unreal conditions, formal English uses ''were'' for every person, including ''I''. The result clause ''I would see a doctor'' confirms this pattern.', wrong = '{"1": "''Am'' is a present tense used for real, possible conditions. Being someone else is impossible, so the unreal ''were'' is needed.", "2": "''Was being'' is a past continuous form and does not fit the fixed advice pattern ''If I were you''.", "3": "''Will'' is not used in the if-clause of a conditional, and it cannot express an unreal situation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0571' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The if-clause is about the past (''had taken ... yesterday'') but the result is about the present (''now''). This is a mixed conditional, so the result uses would + base verb: ''she would feel better now''.', wrong = '{"0": "''Would have felt'' describes an unreal past result. The word ''now'' shows the result is in the present.", "1": "''Will feel'' is a first-conditional result for real possibilities. It cannot follow an unreal past condition.", "3": "''Felt'' is a simple past form. The result of an unreal condition needs ''would''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0572' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After ''It''s time'' or ''It''s high time'' + subject, English uses the past simple, even though the meaning is present: it shows the action should already be happening. So ''It''s high time we left for the airport'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "The base form ''leave'' is not used after ''It''s high time we''. The structure needs the past simple ''left''.", "2": "''Will leave'' is a future form and is not used in this structure.", "3": "''Are leaving'' describes something already in progress, which does not fit the idea that it should happen now."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0573' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After verbs such as insist, demand, recommend and suggest + that, formal English uses the subjunctive, which is the base form of the verb for every person. So it is ''that the patient stay'', with no -s. British English may also use ''should stay''.', wrong = '{"0": "''Stays'' adds the third-person -s. In the subjunctive the base form is used for all persons, so it is ''stay''.", "1": "''Stayed'' is the past tense. The subjunctive after ''insisted that'' uses the base form, not a past form.", "2": "''Will stay'' is a future form and is not used after ''insisted that'' in this meaning."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0574' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Used to'' + base verb describes a past habit or situation that is no longer true. The sentence contrasts childhood in Quetta with living in Karachi now, so ''I used to live in Quetta'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "''Am used to living'' means being accustomed to living somewhere now. It does not describe a finished past situation.", "2": "''Be used to'' must be followed by a gerund (''was used to living''), and it means ''accustomed to'', which changes the meaning.", "3": "In a positive past statement, the correct spelling is ''used to''. ''Use to'' appears only after ''did'' in questions and negatives."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0575' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The waiting began before the ambulance arrived and continued up to that past moment. An action continuing up to a point in the past, with a duration like ''for an hour'', uses the past perfect continuous: ''had been waiting''.', wrong = '{"0": "''Has been waiting'' is present perfect continuous, which links to now. The reference point here is a past moment.", "1": "''Was waiting'' shows an action in progress but does not express the duration up to that point with ''for an hour''.", "2": "''Waited'' is a simple past action. It does not show the waiting continuing up to the ambulance''s arrival."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0576' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The fixed pattern ''This is the first time'' (or second, third time) is followed by the present perfect, because it links an experience to the present moment. So ''This is the first time I have watched a live operation'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The present simple ''watch'' is not used after ''This is the first time''. The pattern needs the present perfect.", "2": "The simple past follows ''It was the first time'' (with ''had watched'' preferred). With ''This is'', the present perfect is used.", "3": "''Am watching'' is commonly heard but is not the standard form after this pattern. Exam English uses the present perfect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0577' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The future perfect continuous (will have been + -ing) shows an action continuing up to a point in the future, with a duration. ''By next June'' gives the future point and ''for ten years'' gives the duration, so ''will have been working'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Will work'' is the simple future. It does not show how long the action will have lasted by a future point.", "2": "''Will be working'' shows an action in progress at a future time but not the length of time up to it.", "3": "''Has been working'' links to the present, not to the future point ''next June''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0578' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The result ''would not have happened'' is an unreal past result, so this is a third conditional. The if-clause of a third conditional uses the past perfect, so ''If he had heeded the warning'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "''Heeded'' (past simple) belongs in a second conditional about the present. It does not match ''would not have happened''.", "2": "''Would'' is placed in the result clause, not the if-clause. ''If he would have heeded'' is incorrect.", "3": "The present perfect ''has heeded'' is not used in conditional if-clauses about an unreal past."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0579' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Unless'' means ''if not''. With a future result (''will not come down''), this is a first conditional, so the unless-clause uses the present simple: ''Unless you increase the dose, the fever will not come down.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Will'' is not used in the unless/if-clause of a first conditional, even though the meaning is future.", "1": "''Increased'' is a past form used in second conditionals. It does not match ''will'' in the main clause.", "3": "''Would'' is used in the result clause of unreal conditionals. It does not belong in this unless-clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0580' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Since'' marks a starting point in the past and links it to the present, so it needs a perfect tense. ''Know'' is a stative verb that is not used in the continuous, so the present perfect simple is correct: ''I have known him since we were at school together.''', wrong = '{"1": "The present simple ''know'' cannot be used with ''since'' to show a state lasting from the past to now.", "2": "''Know'' is a stative verb describing a state, so it is not normally used in continuous forms like ''am knowing''.", "3": "''Knew'' is simple past and suggests the knowing is over. ''Since'' requires a link to the present."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0581' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The past continuous shows an action already in progress when another past action happened. The nurse was in the middle of taking the temperature when I entered, which is why I waited, so ''was taking'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Took'' would mean she began after I entered, so there would be nothing to wait for.", "1": "''Has taken'' is present perfect, which does not fit a story told in the past.", "2": "''Had been took'' is not a correct verb form. After ''had been'', the -ing form or past participle is required."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0582' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In time clauses about the future (after when, as soon as, until), English uses a present form, not ''will''. The present perfect ''have read'' stresses that reading will be complete before the call. So ''as soon as I have read the report'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Will'' is not used in a time clause after ''as soon as'', even when the meaning is future.", "2": "''Had read'' is past perfect and refers to the past, which does not fit ''I will call you''.", "3": "''Would read'' is used for unreal or past-future meanings. It does not fit a future time clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0583' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Were I to be offered the post'' is the inverted, formal form of ''If I were to be offered the post'', used for an unlikely future situation. The person would receive the offer, so the passive infinitive ''to be offered'' is needed.', wrong = '{"0": "''Were I offer'' is ungrammatical, and the base form ''offer'' would make me the one doing the offering.", "2": "''Were I offered'' is possible in English, but this pattern with ''were ... to'' expects the to-infinitive form, completing ''were to be offered''.", "3": "''To offer'' is active, so it would mean I am the one offering the post, which changes the meaning."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0584' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When ''would rather'' is followed by a different subject, the next verb is in the past simple, even though the meaning is present or future. So ''I would rather you finished the details tonight'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "With a different subject after ''would rather'', the past form ''finished'' is normally used, not the base form.", "1": "''Will'' is not used after ''would rather'' + subject. The structure needs the past simple ''finished''.", "2": "The present perfect is not used in this structure. The past simple expresses the preference."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0585' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''If she had not been ill'' is an unreal past condition, so this is a third conditional. Its result clause uses would have + past participle: ''She would have gone to the lecture if she had not been ill.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Would go'' is a second-conditional result for an unreal present. It does not match the past perfect if-clause.", "1": "''Will go'' is a first-conditional result for real future possibilities, which does not fit an unreal past.", "2": "''Had gone'' is past perfect. The result clause of a conditional needs ''would have gone''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0586' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Weigh'' has two meanings. When it describes how heavy something is, it is a stative verb and takes the simple form, not the continuous. The parcel simply has a weight, so ''This parcel weighs two kilograms'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Is weighing'' is used only for the action sense, as in ''The nurse is weighing the baby''. A parcel cannot actively weigh.", "2": "''Has been weighing'' also uses the action sense of the verb, which does not fit a parcel''s weight.", "3": "''Weighing'' alone is not a finite verb, so ''This parcel weighing two kilograms'' is not a complete sentence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0587' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The future continuous (will be + -ing) describes an action that will be in progress at a particular future time. At nine o''clock the exam will be going on, so ''I will be taking my exam then'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Will take'' is the simple future. It suggests the exam starts at nine, not that it is already in progress then.", "2": "The present simple ''take'' is used for timetables and habits, not for an action in progress at a future moment.", "3": "''Will have taken'' is the future perfect, meaning the exam will be finished by nine, which changes the meaning."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0588' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The not-eating continued from the previous night up to a past moment, the time he told us. With ''since'' and a past reference point, the past perfect is used: ''he had not eaten any food since the previous night.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Has not eaten'' is present perfect and links to now. The reference point here is in the past.", "1": "''Did not eat'' is simple past and cannot be used with ''since'' to show a period up to a past moment.", "2": "''Was not eating'' is past continuous and does not combine with ''since'' to show duration up to a past point."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0589' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The perfect infinitive (to have + past participle) shows an action finished before the time of the main verb. ''Already'' and her smile show that she found the answer before now, so ''She seems to have found the answer already'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The simple infinitive ''to find'' refers to the same time or later. It does not show a completed earlier action.", "2": "''Seem'' is followed by a to-infinitive, not an -ing form. ''She seems finding'' is ungrammatical.", "3": "''To be found'' is passive, which would mean she is the thing that has been found."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0590' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Have to'' expresses obligation. The forms cannot submit themselves; someone must submit them. So a passive infinitive (to be + past participle) is needed: ''The forms have to be submitted by Friday.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Have to submit'' is active and would mean the forms must submit something, which makes no sense.", "2": "''Have to'' needs an infinitive, and ''have submitting'' is not a correct verb form.", "3": "When ''have'' means obligation, it must be followed by ''to''. ''Have be submitted'' is ungrammatical."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0591' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A gerund is an -ing form used as a noun. In ''Swimming strengthens the heart'', ''swimming'' is the thing doing the strengthening and comes before the verb ''strengthens'', so it is the subject of the sentence.', wrong = '{"0": "The object is ''the heart'', which receives the action. ''Swimming'' performs it.", "1": "A subject complement follows a linking verb like ''is''. ''Strengthens'' is an action verb, so there is no complement.", "2": "An object of a preposition follows a word like ''in'' or ''of''. No preposition comes before ''swimming''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0592' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In formal English, a pronoun before a gerund is put in the possessive form, because the gerund acts like a noun. The doctor objected to the action of leaving, which was his, so ''objected to his leaving'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''He'' is a subject pronoun and cannot follow the preposition ''to''.", "1": "''Himself'' would refer back to the doctor and mean the doctor was leaving, which changes the meaning.", "3": "''He''s'' is a contraction of ''he is'' or ''he has''. ''Objected to he is leaving'' is ungrammatical."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0593' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The to-infinitive is used to express a person''s purpose, answering ''why?''. She went to the library in order to prepare for the exam, so ''She went to the library to prepare for the exam'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''For + -ing'' describes what a thing is used for, as in ''a knife for cutting''. It does not express a person''s purpose.", "1": "''For'' is a preposition and cannot be followed by the base form of a verb.", "3": "''Preparing'' after ''library'' does not express purpose; it reads as describing what she was doing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0594' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ''To become a surgeon requires years of training'', the infinitive phrase ''To become a surgeon'' is the thing that requires training. It comes before the verb ''requires'' and acts as a noun, so it is the subject.', wrong = '{"1": "The object is ''years of training'', which follows ''requires''. The infinitive phrase is the subject.", "2": "An adverbial infinitive tells why or how, like ''He came to help''. This phrase does not modify a verb.", "3": "An adjectival infinitive describes a noun, as in ''a book to read''. This phrase does not describe a noun."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0595' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The pattern ''too + adjective + for someone + to-infinitive'' means something goes beyond what is possible. ''The box is too heavy for me to lift'' means it is so heavy that I cannot lift it.', wrong = '{"0": "''So heavy'' is followed by a that-clause, as in ''so heavy that I cannot lift it'', not by ''for me to''.", "2": "''Very heavy'' only says the box is heavy. It does not connect to ''for me to lift'' to show impossibility.", "3": "''Much'' is not used before a plain adjective like ''heavy''. It goes with comparatives, as in ''much heavier''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0596' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The gerund phrase ''walking every morning'' answers the question ''recommended what?''. It receives the action of the verb ''recommended'', so it is the direct object of the sentence.', wrong = '{"0": "The subject is ''The doctor'', who does the recommending.", "2": "A subject complement follows a linking verb like ''is''. ''Recommended'' is an action verb.", "3": "An object of a preposition follows a word like ''in'' or ''about''. No preposition comes before this phrase."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0597' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ''She was happy to help'', the infinitive ''to help'' completes the adjective ''happy'' by explaining in what way or why she was happy. So it modifies the adjective, working adverbially. Compare ''ready to go'' and ''eager to learn''.', wrong = '{"0": "The infinitive does not describe ''She'' directly. It explains the adjective ''happy''.", "1": "The subject of the sentence is ''She''. ''To help'' comes after the adjective and is not the subject.", "2": "''Was'' is a linking verb and does not take an object, so ''to help'' cannot be its object."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0598' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Remember'' changes meaning with the form after it. ''Remember + -ing'' means having a memory of something done earlier. The speaker recalls locking the door, yet now finds it open, so ''I clearly remember locking the door'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Remember to lock'' means not forgetting a task still to be done. The speaker is recalling a past action.", "1": "''Remember'' is not followed by a bare infinitive, so ''remember lock'' is ungrammatical.", "3": "''To have locking'' mixes a perfect infinitive with an -ing form and is not a correct verb form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0599' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Try + to-infinitive'' means to make an effort to do something difficult. ''But it was stuck'' shows that she attempted to open the window and failed, so ''She tried to open the window'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Try + -ing'' means testing a method to see if it works, assuming the action itself is possible. Here she could not open it.", "1": "''Try'' is not followed by a bare infinitive, so ''tried open'' is ungrammatical.", "2": "The perfect infinitive ''to have opened'' does not fit an attempt made at that time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0600' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Regret'' changes meaning with the form after it. ''Regret + to-infinitive'' is used to announce bad news politely at the present moment, usually with verbs like inform, tell or announce. So ''We regret to inform you that your application has been unsuccessful'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Regret + -ing'' means being sorry about something already done, as in ''I regret shouting''. Here the news is being given now.", "2": "''Regret'' is not followed by a bare infinitive, so ''regret inform'' is ungrammatical.", "3": "''To informing'' is not a correct form after ''regret''. The verb needs the plain to-infinitive ''to inform''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0601' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the active voice, ''make'' is followed by the bare infinitive: ''They made him take the medicine.'' In the passive, ''to'' returns before the verb: ''He was made to take the medicine.'' So ''to take'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The bare infinitive ''take'' follows ''make'' only in the active voice. After ''was made'', ''to'' is needed.", "1": "The -ing form is not used after the passive of ''make''. ''He was made taking'' is ungrammatical.", "2": "''Taken'' is a past participle and cannot follow ''was made'' in this structure."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0602' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Would rather'' (short form ''I''d rather'') expresses preference and is followed by the bare infinitive, the base form without ''to'', when the subject stays the same. So ''I''d rather stay at home tonight'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''To'' is not used after ''would rather''. It takes the bare infinitive, unlike ''would prefer to''.", "1": "''Would rather'' is not followed by an -ing form, so ''I''d rather staying'' is ungrammatical.", "3": "The past form follows ''would rather'' only when a different subject comes next, as in ''I''d rather you stayed''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0603' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Deny'' is followed by a gerund (-ing form), never an infinitive. The perfect gerund ''having deleted'' stresses that the deleting would have happened before the denial, so ''She denied having deleted the files'' is correct. ''She denied deleting'' would also be correct.', wrong = '{"1": "''Deny'' does not take a to-infinitive, so ''denied to delete'' is ungrammatical.", "2": "''Deny'' does not take any infinitive, including the perfect infinitive ''to have deleted''.", "3": "A bare verb cannot follow ''denied''. The verb must be a gerund such as ''deleting'' or ''having deleted''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0604' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An introductory infinitive phrase like ''To pass the exam'' must refer to the subject of the main clause, or it ''dangles''. Students are the ones who pass exams, so the main clause must begin with them: ''students must revise every chapter.''', wrong = '{"0": "This makes ''every chapter'' the subject, so it suggests the chapters are trying to pass the exam.", "1": "This makes ''hard work'' the subject, but hard work cannot pass an exam. The opening phrase dangles.", "2": "This makes ''the syllabus'' the subject, suggesting the syllabus passes the exam, which is illogical."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0605' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After ''is said'', ''is believed'' or ''is thought'', the perfect infinitive (to have + past participle) is used for an action that happened before the time of saying. He founded the company in 2010, before now, so ''He is said to have founded the company in 2010'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The simple infinitive ''to found'' refers to the present or a general fact. It does not fit a past date like 2010.", "2": "''Is said'' must be followed by a to-infinitive, not an -ing form.", "3": "''To be founding'' describes an action in progress now, which does not fit a completed action in 2010."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0606' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Afford'', especially after ''can'' or ''can''t'', is followed by a to-infinitive. Other verbs in this group include decide, hope and refuse. So ''She can''t afford to buy a new laptop'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Afford'' is not followed by an -ing form, so ''afford buying'' is incorrect.", "2": "''Afford'' needs ''to'' before the verb. ''Afford buy'' is ungrammatical.", "3": "''For'' is a preposition and is not used between ''afford'' and a verb here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0607' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Mean'' changes meaning with the form after it. ''Mean + to-infinitive'' means to intend or plan to do something. The speaker intended to call yesterday but forgot, so ''I meant to call you yesterday'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "''Mean + -ing'' means to involve or result in, as in ''Missing the bus means waiting''. It does not express intention.", "2": "''Mean'' is not followed by a bare infinitive, so ''meant call'' is ungrammatical.", "3": "''To calling'' is not a correct form after ''mean''. It needs the plain to-infinitive ''to call''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0608' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Mean + -ing'' means to involve or have as a result. Missing the last bus leads to waiting for an hour, so the waiting is the result: ''Missing the last bus means waiting for an hour'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Mean + to-infinitive'' means to intend. A missed bus cannot intend anything, so this does not fit.", "1": "''Means'' is not followed by a bare verb. ''Means wait'' is ungrammatical.", "3": "''Waited'' is a past form and cannot follow ''means'' in this structure."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0609' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ''object to'', the word ''to'' is a preposition, so it must be followed by a gerund, not a base verb. The speaker is the one being treated, so the passive gerund ''being + past participle'' is needed: ''I object to being treated like a child.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Be'' is a base verb. Since ''to'' here is a preposition, it needs a gerund such as ''being''.", "1": "''Been'' is a past participle and cannot come directly after the preposition ''to''.", "3": "''Have been'' would form a perfect infinitive, but ''object to'' takes a gerund, not an infinitive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0610' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Go on + -ing'' means to continue doing the same thing without stopping. Even after being asked to stop, she continued talking about her new job, so ''she went on talking'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "''Go on + to-infinitive'' means to move on to a new activity, as in ''went on to become a surgeon''. She did not change activity.", "2": "A bare verb cannot follow ''went on'', so ''went on talk'' is ungrammatical.", "3": "''Went on talked'' uses two past forms together, which is ungrammatical."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0611' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Late'' works as both adjective and adverb. As an adverb it means after the expected or proper time. The patient came after his appointment time, so ''The patient arrived late for his appointment'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Lately'' means recently, as in ''I have been tired lately''. It does not mean after the expected time.", "1": "''Latter'' means the second of two things mentioned. It does not describe arriving after time.", "3": "''Latest'' means most recent or last of all. It does not fit ''arrived ___ for his appointment''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0612' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Here ''feel'' is a linking verb: it connects the subject to a word describing their state. A linking verb is followed by an adjective, not an adverb. So ''I feel bad about missing your wedding'' correctly describes how the speaker feels.', wrong = '{"1": "''Badly'' is an adverb and would describe how well the act of feeling is done, such as having a poor sense of touch.", "2": "''Worst'' is a superlative used to compare three or more things. Nothing is being compared here.", "3": "''Badness'' is a noun. After the linking verb ''feel'', an adjective is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0613' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An interrogative adverb asks a question about place, time, manner or reason (where, when, how, why). In ''Where did you put the slides?'', ''where'' asks about place in a direct question, so it is an interrogative adverb.', wrong = '{"0": "A relative adverb joins a clause to a noun, as in ''the room where we met''. Here ''where'' begins a question.", "1": "A pronoun stands in for a noun. ''Where'' here asks about place and modifies the verb ''put''.", "2": "A preposition is followed by an object, as in ''under the table''. ''Where'' has no object here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0614' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A relative adverb (where, when, why) introduces a clause that describes a noun. In ''the lab where we tested the samples'', ''where'' begins a clause that tells us which lab, so it is a relative adverb.', wrong = '{"0": "An interrogative adverb asks a question. This sentence is a statement, not a question.", "2": "A preposition is followed by an object noun. ''Where'' here introduces a whole clause.", "3": "A demonstrative (this, that, these, those) points out a noun. ''Where'' does not point to anything."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0615' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A sentence adverb comments on the whole statement rather than on one verb. In ''Hopefully, the results will be ready by Monday'', ''hopefully'' means ''it is hoped that'', and it is separated by a comma at the start. So it acts as a sentence adverb.', wrong = '{"0": "Here ''hopefully'' tells how she waited, meaning ''in a hopeful way''. It is an adverb of manner.", "2": "Here ''hopefully'' tells how he looked at the screen. It is an adverb of manner.", "3": "Here ''hopefully'' tells how they watched. It is an adverb of manner, not a comment on the sentence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0616' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When ''enough'' modifies an adjective, it comes after the adjective: ''warm enough'', ''old enough'', ''big enough''. So the correct question is ''Is the water warm enough to bathe the baby?''', wrong = '{"0": "''Enough warm'' puts ''enough'' before the adjective. As an adverb, ''enough'' must follow it: ''warm enough''.", "1": "''Enough'' cannot come before the subject ''the water'' here. The word order is jumbled.", "2": "''Enough'' is placed after ''bathe'', so it no longer modifies ''warm''. The sentence becomes confused."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0617' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Hardly ever'' is a fixed phrase meaning almost never. ''She hardly ever misses a lecture'' means she almost never misses one. ''Scarcely ever'' and ''seldom'' have the same meaning.', wrong = '{"0": "''Hard'' means with great effort, as in ''work hard''. ''Hard ever'' is not an English phrase.", "1": "''Seldom'' is already an adverb and has no -ly form in standard English. ''Seldomly'' is not accepted.", "3": "''Scarce'' is an adjective meaning in short supply. The adverb form is ''scarcely''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0618' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Adverbs of frequency like always, often and never usually go after the first auxiliary verb and before the main verb. In ''has been interested'', the first auxiliary is ''has'', so ''She has always been interested in medicine'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Always'' appears twice, which is repetitive and incorrect.", "1": "Placing ''always'' at the very start of this statement is not normal word order in English.", "3": "''Always'' should come after the first auxiliary ''has'', not after ''been''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0619' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Even'' focuses on the word that follows it and shows surprise. Placed before ''the professor'', it means that the professor, of all people, who would be expected to solve it, could not. So ''Even the professor could not solve the problem'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "Here ''even'' focuses on the verb phrase, meaning he could not manage even this simple task. The focus is not on the professor.", "1": "Here ''even'' points to ''the problem'', suggesting this problem was the easiest one, not stressing the professor.", "2": "''Even could not'' is not natural word order. ''Even'' would normally come after ''could not'' or before the subject."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0620' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Yet'' is used at the end of negative sentences and questions to talk about something that is expected to happen but has not happened so far. So ''I haven''t finished my report yet'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Already'' is used in positive sentences, meaning sooner than expected. It does not fit a negative sentence here.", "2": "''Still'' is used in negatives but goes before the verb: ''I still haven''t finished my report''.", "3": "''Ever'' means at any time and is used in questions like ''Have you ever...''. It does not fit here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0621' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Still'' shows that an action or situation is continuing longer than expected. He is waiting for his results and they have not arrived, so ''He is still waiting for his results'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Yet'' is used in negatives and questions, usually at the end, as in ''They have not come yet''. It does not fit here.", "2": "''Already'' means sooner than expected. It would not fit an ongoing wait for something that has not arrived.", "3": "''Ever'' means at any time and is used mainly in questions and negatives. ''He is ever waiting'' is not natural."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0622' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Fast'' can be an adjective, an adverb, a noun or a verb. In ''She runs fast'', it tells how she runs, so it modifies the verb ''runs'' and works as an adverb. Note that ''fastly'' is not a word.', wrong = '{"0": "In ''a fast runner'', ''fast'' describes the noun ''runner'', so it is an adjective.", "1": "In ''These colours are fast'', ''fast'' means not fading and describes ''colours'', so it is an adjective.", "2": "In ''broke their fast'', ''fast'' is a noun meaning a period without food."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0623' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Prior'' is always followed by ''to'', in the fixed phrase ''prior to'', meaning before. Like ''senior to'' and ''superior to'', it never takes ''than''. So ''submitted prior to the deadline'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Prior than'' is a common error. Words like prior, senior and superior take ''to'', not ''than''.", "1": "''Prior before'' repeats the same idea, since ''prior to'' already means before. It is not standard.", "2": "''Prior from'' is not used in English. The only correct partner is ''to''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0624' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Suffer from'' is the standard combination used for illnesses and conditions, as in ''suffering from fever'' or ''suffers from asthma''. So ''The patient has been suffering from fever for three days'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Suffering with'' is heard informally but is not the standard form in exams. Use ''suffer from''.", "2": "''By'' does not follow ''suffer'' to name an illness. ''Suffering by fever'' is incorrect.", "3": "''Suffer of'' is not used in English, though it is a common error among learners."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0625' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'These are two fixed combinations. We are ''accused of'' a wrongdoing, and ''charged with'' a crime when formally accused by police or a court. So the sentence is ''He was accused of cheating, and later charged with fraud.''', wrong = '{"0": "The prepositions are reversed. It should be ''accused of'' and ''charged with'', not ''accused with'' and ''charged of''.", "2": "''Accused for'' is a common error. ''Accuse'' takes ''of'', although ''charged with'' in the second blank is right.", "3": "''Charged for'' means being asked to pay a price, as in ''charged for parking''. A crime takes ''charged with''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0626' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Refrain'' means to stop oneself from doing something, and it always takes ''from'', followed by a gerund. So ''The patient was told to refrain from eating for eight hours'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Refrain to'' is a common error. The fixed partner of refrain is ''from'', followed by an -ing form.", "1": "''Refrain of'' is not used in English. ''Refrain'' always takes ''from''.", "2": "''Refrain with'' is not used in English. Only ''refrain from'' is correct."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0627' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Arrive in'' is used for cities, countries and large areas, so we arrive in Lahore. ''At'' is used for exact points in time, such as at noon or at midnight. So ''We arrived in Lahore at midnight'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "''Arrive at'' is used for smaller places like a station or building, not a city, and ''in midnight'' is wrong.", "2": "''Arrive to'' is not used in English. We arrive in a city or at a place.", "3": "''On'' is used with days and dates, not with clock times. ''On midnight'' is incorrect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0628' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'We are angry ''with'' a person and angry ''for'' or ''about'' what they did. So ''She was angry with her brother for breaking her phone'' uses the right preposition in both blanks.', wrong = '{"0": "''Angry at her brother'' is used informally, but ''angry with breaking'' is wrong. The reason takes ''for'' or ''about''.", "1": "''Angry on'' is not standard English. A person takes ''with'' (or ''at'').", "3": "''Angry to'' is not used in English, so the first blank is wrong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0629' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Between'' is used when two people or things are involved, and ''among'' when there are three or more. The money is shared by two sisters, so ''The money was shared between the two sisters'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Among'' is used for three or more. With just two sisters, ''between'' is needed.", "1": "''Shared with'' needs a different structure, such as ''one sister shared it with the other''. It does not fit here.", "3": "''Shared in'' would mean taking part in something. It does not show division of money between people."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0630' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Onto'' shows movement to a position on top of a surface, so the cat jumped onto the table. ''Under'' shows a position below something, so it then hid under the table. Together: ''The cat jumped onto the table and then hid under it.''', wrong = '{"1": "''Into'' means movement to the inside of something. A table has no inside, so a cat cannot jump into it.", "2": "''In the table'' is wrong because a table is not a container, and ''hid over it'' makes no sense for hiding.", "3": "''Jumped at the table'' would mean leaping towards it as if to attack. ''Below'' is possible, but the first blank fails."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0631' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'We say ''in the morning'' for mornings in general, but when the morning belongs to a particular dated day, we use ''on'', because dates and days take ''on''. So ''She was born on the morning of 14 August'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''In the morning'' is used for mornings in general. A specific dated morning, like that of 14 August, takes ''on''.", "1": "''At the morning'' is not correct English. ''At'' is used with clock times and ''night'', not with ''morning''.", "2": "''By'' means ''not later than'', as in ''by Monday''. It does not state when a birth took place."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0632' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Through'' describes movement from one end of an enclosed space, like a tunnel, pipe or forest, to the other end. A train enters a tunnel at one end and leaves at the other, so ''The train passed through a long tunnel'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Across'' means moving over a surface from one side to the other, like across a road or river. A tunnel is enclosed.", "2": "''Over'' means above something. A train goes inside a tunnel, not above it.", "3": "''Onto'' means moving to a position on a surface. It does not describe passing along inside a tunnel."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0633' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both adjectives take ''of'' as a fixed partner. ''Fond of'' means liking something, and ''proud of'' means feeling pleased about one''s own or another''s quality or achievement. So ''She is fond of music and proud of her voice'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Fond with'' is incorrect, although ''proud of'' is right. ''Fond'' always takes ''of''.", "2": "Neither ''fond for'' nor ''proud about'' is standard. Both adjectives take ''of''.", "3": "''Fond of'' is right, but ''proud with'' is incorrect. ''Proud'' takes ''of''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0634' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Complain of'' is used when describing symptoms of illness, as in ''complained of a headache''. Pain is felt ''in'' a part of the body. So ''The patient complained of pain in his chest'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Complain for'' is not standard, and pain is felt ''in'' the chest, not ''at'' it.", "1": "''Complain with'' and ''pain on his chest'' are both incorrect for describing symptoms.", "2": "''Complain to'' is used for the person who hears the complaint, as in ''complained to the doctor'', not for the symptom."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0635' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Bound for'' means travelling towards or heading to a place. A train that is going to Karachi is ''bound for Karachi''. This phrase is often used for trains, ships and planes.', wrong = '{"1": "''Bound to'' means certain to happen, as in ''He is bound to win''. It does not show the destination.", "2": "''Bound at'' is not used in English to show where a train is going.", "3": "''Bound in'' means covered or tied in something, as in ''a book bound in leather''. It does not show direction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0636' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''In fact'' adds a stronger, more specific point that supports and emphasises the first statement. Saying smoking damages the lungs is then strengthened by saying it is the leading cause of preventable lung cancer.', wrong = '{"0": "''However'' signals contrast. The second sentence supports the first rather than opposing it.", "1": "''Otherwise'' means ''if not'', introducing what happens if something does not occur. That makes no sense here.", "2": "''Meanwhile'' shows two things happening at the same time. It does not add emphasis."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0637' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''In other words'' introduces a restatement of the same idea in simpler language. ''Contraindicated in pregnancy'' is a technical phrase, and ''pregnant women must not take it'' explains it plainly, so ''in other words'' fits.', wrong = '{"1": "''On the other hand'' introduces a contrasting point. The second clause repeats the first in plain words.", "2": "''Nevertheless'' introduces something true despite the first idea. Here there is no contrast.", "3": "''Similarly'' introduces a separate but parallel case. The second clause is the same idea restated."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0638' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Afterwards'' means ''after that'' and shows the order of steps in time. In a sterile procedure, hands are washed first and then the gloves are put on, so ''Afterwards'' correctly links the two steps.', wrong = '{"0": "''Nevertheless'' signals contrast. The two steps follow each other; they do not contrast.", "1": "''Instead'' would mean the gloves replace hand-washing, but both steps are needed.", "3": "''Otherwise'' means ''if you do not do this''. Wearing gloves is not a consequence of failing to wash."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0639' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Admittedly'' concedes a point that seems to weaken the writer''s case before giving the main point. The writer admits the hospital is far from the city, then says ''still, its facilities are excellent''. This concession pattern fits.', wrong = '{"1": "''Consequently'' introduces a result, but no cause has been given before the distance is mentioned.", "2": "''Similarly'' needs an earlier parallel idea to compare with. The sentence begins with this point.", "3": "''For example'' needs a general statement before it to illustrate. There is none."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0640' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An emphasis transition highlights the most important point. ''Above all'' means ''most importantly of everything'', as in ''Above all, wash your hands'', so it signals emphasis.', wrong = '{"0": "''In contrast'' signals a difference between two ideas, not emphasis.", "1": "''As a result'' signals cause and effect, showing what happened because of something.", "2": "''For example'' introduces an illustration of a general idea."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0641' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Meanwhile'' means ''at the same time'' and shows that two actions happen together. The nurse checked the drip while the doctor reviewed the chart at the desk, so ''Meanwhile'' fits.', wrong = '{"0": "''Therefore'' shows result. The doctor reviewing the chart is not caused by the drip check.", "2": "''Instead'' would mean the doctor''s action replaced the nurse''s, but both happened.", "3": "''Otherwise'' means ''if not''. There is no condition here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0642' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''All in all'' sums up the points just made and gives an overall conclusion. The drug is cheap, safe and effective, so all things considered it is the best choice for rural clinics.', wrong = '{"0": "''For instance'' introduces an example. The second sentence is a conclusion, not an example.", "1": "''In contrast'' introduces a difference. The second sentence agrees with the first.", "3": "''Meanwhile'' shows things happening at the same time. It does not sum up."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0643' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''For instance'' introduces examples of a general statement. Oranges and guavas are examples of fruits rich in vitamin C, so ''oranges and guavas, for instance, contain large amounts'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "''However'' signals contrast. The second clause supports the first rather than opposing it.", "2": "''Therefore'' shows a result. Oranges being rich in vitamin C is not a result of the first clause.", "3": "''Otherwise'' means ''if not''. There is no condition in the sentence."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0644' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A very small trial gives less reliable results, so the need for caution is a result of its small size. ''Consequently'' is a result transition meaning ''because of this'', so it fits after the semicolon.', wrong = '{"1": "''However'' signals contrast. The caution follows naturally from the small size, so there is no contrast.", "2": "''Moreover'' adds another point of the same kind. Here the second clause is a consequence, not an extra point.", "3": "''Nevertheless'' means ''in spite of this''. Being cautious is expected after a small trial, not surprising."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0645' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Moreover'' adds another point that supports the first. The drug is effective, and in addition it is cheap; together these make it ideal for rural clinics. So an addition transition is needed.', wrong = '{"0": "''Nevertheless'' signals contrast. Being effective and being cheap are both positive, so they do not contrast.", "1": "''Otherwise'' means ''if not''. There is no condition linking the two sentences.", "3": "''Instead'' signals replacement. Being cheap does not replace being effective."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0646' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Otherwise'' means ''if you do not do this'', and introduces the result of not following an instruction. If the tablets are not taken with food, they may upset the stomach, so ''otherwise'' fits.', wrong = '{"0": "''However'' signals contrast. The second clause is the consequence of not following the advice, not a simple contrast.", "1": "''Moreover'' adds another point. A stomach upset is not an additional instruction.", "3": "''Similarly'' introduces a parallel case. Nothing here is being compared."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0647' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Although'' is a conjunction, so it introduces a full clause with a subject and verb: ''it rained heavily''. The match continuing despite rain is a contrast, so ''Although it rained heavily, the match continued'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Despite'' is a preposition and must be followed by a noun phrase, as in ''Despite the heavy rain''.", "1": "''In spite of'' also needs a noun phrase or gerund, as in ''In spite of the rain''. It cannot take a full clause.", "2": "''However'' is a linking adverb and cannot join a dependent clause to a main clause like this."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0648' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Whereas'' joins two contrasting facts within one sentence. Some patients recover within days, while others take several weeks, which is a direct contrast between two groups, so ''whereas'' fits.', wrong = '{"0": "''Therefore'' shows result. Others taking longer is not caused by some recovering quickly.", "2": "''So that'' expresses purpose. Neither group recovers in order to affect the other.", "3": "''Because'' gives a reason. The second fact does not explain the first."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0649' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''In order to'' is followed by the base form of a verb and expresses purpose. He saved money for the purpose of buying a microscope, and the blank is followed by the base verb ''buy'', so ''in order to'' fits.', wrong = '{"0": "''So that'' expresses purpose but must be followed by a clause with a subject, such as ''so that he could buy''.", "1": "''In order that'' also needs a full clause with a subject, such as ''in order that he might buy''.", "2": "''For'' is a preposition and cannot be followed by a base verb like ''buy''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0650' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Granted'' concedes a weakness (the method is slow), and ''still'' then introduces the main point that outweighs it (it is very accurate). ''Granted ... still'' is a standard concession pattern, so ''still'' fits.', wrong = '{"1": "''Therefore'' shows result. Accuracy is not caused by slowness.", "2": "''For example'' introduces an illustration. Accuracy is not an example of slowness.", "3": "''Similarly'' introduces a parallel idea. Accuracy is a strength that contrasts with slowness, not a similar point."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0651' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Higher vaccination protects more people, so measles cases fall. The second sentence is the effect of the first, so a result transition is needed: ''As a result, measles cases fell to a record low.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Rose'' and ''fell'' look like opposites, but the real link between the sentences is cause and effect, not contrast.", "1": "''Nevertheless'' signals something surprising despite the first fact. Fewer cases after more vaccination is expected.", "3": "''For instance'' introduces an example. Fewer measles cases is a consequence, not an example of rising vaccination."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0652' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Accordingly'' means ''for that reason'' or ''as a result''. The allergy to penicillin is the reason the doctor chose a different antibiotic, so ''accordingly'' correctly shows this cause and result.', wrong = '{"0": "''Nonetheless'' means ''in spite of this''. Choosing another drug is the expected response, not a surprising contrast.", "1": "''In contrast'' introduces an opposing idea. The two clauses are linked by cause and effect.", "3": "''Similarly'' introduces a parallel case. The prescription is a result of the allergy, not a similar situation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0653' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When ''however'' links two independent clauses, it needs a semicolon (or full stop) before it and a comma after it. ''The test was hard'' and ''everyone passed'' are both complete clauses, so ''The test was hard; however, everyone passed.'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "Using only a comma before ''however'' to join two complete sentences creates a comma splice.", "2": "A semicolon or full stop is needed before ''however''. Without it, the two clauses run together.", "3": "The semicolon is right, but the comma should follow ''however'', not split ''everyone'' from ''passed''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0654' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''In spite of'' is a prepositional phrase, so it is followed by a noun phrase, not a clause. ''His injury'' is a noun phrase, so ''In spite of his injury, he completed the race'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Although'' is a conjunction and needs a clause with a verb, such as ''Although he was injured''.", "1": "''However'' is a linking adverb. It cannot take a noun phrase like ''his injury'' as its object.", "3": "''Even though'' is a conjunction and needs a clause with a verb, such as ''Even though he was injured''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0655' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Nonetheless'' means ''in spite of this''. The drug is expensive, yet hospitals still buy it because it is the only effective option. The second clause holds true despite the first, so a concession word is needed.', wrong = '{"0": "''Therefore'' shows result. Being the only effective option is not caused by being expensive.", "1": "''For instance'' introduces an example. The second clause is not an example of the drug''s cost.", "3": "''Likewise'' shows similarity. Price and effectiveness are being weighed against each other, not compared as alike."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0656' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A dash can be used near the end of a sentence to introduce an explanation or add emphasis. In ''She had one goal'' followed by a dash and ''to become a surgeon'', the dash introduces what that goal was. It is placed after the complete idea, which is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The dash splits the verb ''had'' from its object ''one goal'' for no reason, breaking the sentence.", "1": "The dash separates the subject ''She'' from its verb ''had'', which is not a correct use.", "2": "The dash splits ''one'' from ''goal'', breaking up a single noun phrase."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0657' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Brackets (parentheses) enclose extra information that is not needed for the grammar of the sentence. A common use is giving an abbreviation after the full name the first time it appears: ''The World Health Organization (WHO) issued new guidelines.''', wrong = '{"1": "The brackets cut the name in half, separating ''Organization'' from the rest of the name.", "2": "The whole subject is put inside brackets, and the abbreviation is not separated from the full name.", "3": "The verb ''issued'' is an essential part of the sentence, not extra information, and ''WHO'' needs brackets."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0658' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''i.e.'' stands for the Latin ''id est'', meaning ''that is'' or ''in other words''. It is used to explain or restate something more precisely, as in ''the largest organ, i.e. the skin''.', wrong = '{"0": "''e.g.'' stands for ''exempli gratia'' and means ''for example''. It introduces examples, not a restatement.", "1": "''etc.'' stands for ''et cetera'' and means ''and so on''. It is used at the end of a list.", "3": "''et al.'' means ''and others'' and is used mainly when citing several authors."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0659' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a sentence ends with an abbreviation that already has a full stop, such as ''etc.'', that one full stop also ends the sentence. No second full stop is added. So ''Bring pens, pencils, erasers, etc.'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "A double full stop is never used. The full stop of ''etc.'' also ends the sentence.", "1": "Without any full stop, neither the abbreviation ''etc.'' nor the sentence is properly finished.", "3": "A comma after ''Bring'' wrongly separates the verb from its objects."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0660' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Three dots in a row form an ellipsis. In dialogue, an ellipsis shows that the speaker pauses, trails off or leaves something unsaid. Here the speaker starts to say something, then stops and changes their mind with ''never mind''.', wrong = '{"1": "A list is usually introduced by a colon, not by three dots.", "2": "A quotation is marked by quotation marks, not by an ellipsis.", "3": "Possession is shown by an apostrophe, as in ''the doctor''s bag'', not by three dots."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0661' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Proper nouns take capitals: the month Ramadan, the name of a particular building (Badshahi Mosque, where ''Mosque'' is part of the name) and the city Lahore. ''Aunt'' after ''my'' is used as a common noun, so it stays lower case. The sentence also begins with a capital.', wrong = '{"0": "''Ramadan'' and ''Badshahi Mosque'' need capitals, and ''aunt'' after ''my'' should be lower case.", "1": "''Lahore'' is a city and needs a capital, ''Mosque'' is part of the building''s name, and ''aunt'' should be lower case.", "2": "Every sentence must begin with a capital letter, so ''last'' should be ''Last''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0662' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Compass directions used as directions (drove north) and seasons (summer) are common nouns, so they stay lower case. ''Swat'' is the name of a place, so it is a proper noun and needs a capital. The sentence also starts with a capital.', wrong = '{"0": "''North'' and ''Summer'' should be lower case, and the place name ''Swat'' must be capitalised.", "2": "Seasons such as summer are not capitalised unless they begin a sentence or are part of a name.", "3": "The first word of a sentence must always begin with a capital letter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0663' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In titles, the first word and all main words (nouns, verbs, adjectives) take capitals. Short articles, conjunctions and prepositions such as ''the'' and ''and'' stay lower case unless they come first. So ''The Old Man and the Sea'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The main words ''Old'', ''Man'' and ''Sea'' must be capitalised in a title.", "2": "Short words like ''and'' and ''the'' in the middle of a title stay lower case.", "3": "The first word of a title is always capitalised, even if it is ''the''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0664' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Names of languages, such as English and Urdu, are proper nouns and always take a capital. Ordinary school subjects like chemistry or biology are common nouns and stay lower case. So ''She speaks English and is studying chemistry'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''English'' is a language and needs a capital, while ''chemistry'' is a common noun and should be lower case.", "1": "''English'' is the name of a language, so it must be capitalised.", "2": "''Speaks'' is an ordinary verb in the middle of a sentence and should not have a capital."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0665' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Women'' is an irregular plural that does not end in -s. To make such a plural possessive, add apostrophe + s, just like a singular noun: women''s. So ''The women''s hostel is next to the library'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Womens'' is not a word, since ''women'' is already plural, so ''womens'''' is also wrong.", "1": "''Womens'' has no apostrophe and is not a real word. The possessive is ''women''s''.", "2": "''Womans'''' does not exist. The singular is ''woman'' (possessive ''woman''s'') and the plural is ''women''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0666' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The car belongs to the whole Khan family, called ''the Khans'' in the plural. For a plural noun ending in -s, the possessive adds only an apostrophe after the s, so the correct form is ''The Khans'' car''.', wrong = '{"0": "''Khan''s'' is singular possessive, meaning the car belongs to one person named Khan, not the family.", "2": "''Khans'' with no apostrophe is a plain plural. It does not show that the car belongs to them.", "3": "A plural ending in -s takes only an apostrophe. ''Khans''s'' adds an unnecessary extra s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0667' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Its'' (no apostrophe) is the possessive form, meaning ''belonging to it'', so ''its website'' is right. ''It''s'' is a contraction of ''it is'' or ''it has'', so ''it''s now much easier to use'' means ''it is now much easier''.', wrong = '{"0": "The two forms are reversed. The first blank shows possession (''its''), and the second needs ''it is'' (''it''s'').", "2": "The first blank is right, but the second needs ''it is'', so it must be ''it''s''.", "3": "The second blank is right, but the first shows possession, so it needs ''its'' with no apostrophe."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0668' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the items in a list already contain commas, such as ''Lahore, Punjab'', semicolons are used between the items to keep them clear. Each city stays joined to its province by a comma, and semicolons separate the city-province pairs.', wrong = '{"0": "With only commas, the list looks like six separate places, and it is unclear which city belongs to which province.", "1": "The semicolons split each city from its own province, and the colon after ''from'' wrongly separates the verb from the list.", "2": "Semicolons separate the pairs correctly, but each city and province still need a comma between them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0669' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A defining relative clause, written without commas, tells us which people are meant. ''Students who cheat will be expelled'' limits the rule to those students who cheat, so only they will be expelled.', wrong = '{"0": "With commas, ''who cheat'' becomes extra information about all students, suggesting every student cheats and all will be expelled.", "1": "The comma after ''who'' splits the relative clause in the middle, which is incorrect.", "3": "A single comma after ''Students'' separates the subject from its own clause and leaves the clause unclosed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0670' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A comma introduces the quotation after ''She asked''. The quoted words are themselves a question, so the question mark goes inside the closing quotation marks, and it also ends the sentence, with no extra full stop. The quote begins with a capital.', wrong = '{"0": "The quoted words form the question, so the question mark belongs inside the quotation marks, not outside.", "1": "A comma is needed after ''asked'' to introduce the quote, and no full stop is added after the question mark.", "3": "A quoted full sentence begins with a capital letter, so ''is'' should be ''Is''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0671' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When several words together act as one adjective before a noun, they are joined by hyphens. In such a compound adjective, the noun ''year'' stays singular. So ''a five-year-old child'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "All three words act as one adjective, so ''five'' must also be joined with a hyphen.", "2": "In a compound adjective before a noun, ''year'' stays singular, so ''five-years-old'' is wrong.", "3": "The hyphen joins ''old'' to ''child'', but ''child'' is the noun being described and should stand alone."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0672' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a coordinating conjunction such as ''but'', ''and'' or ''so'' joins two independent clauses, a comma is placed before the conjunction. ''I wanted to come'' and ''I was ill'' are both complete clauses, so ''I wanted to come, but I was ill.'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The comma should come before ''but'', not after it, because it separates the two clauses.", "2": "The comma after ''wanted'' splits the verb from its object ''to come'' and leaves no comma before ''but''.", "3": "A semicolon before ''but'' and a comma after it are unnecessary. A single comma before ''but'' is enough."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0673' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An interrupting phrase like ''though delayed'' must be enclosed by a matching pair of marks, either two dashes or two commas. This option uses a dash before and a dash after the phrase, so the interruption is clearly set off.', wrong = '{"0": "A comma opens the interruption and a dash closes it. The marks must match: two commas or two dashes.", "1": "A dash opens the interruption and a comma closes it. The pair is mismatched.", "2": "The dash splits ''though'' from ''delayed'', breaking the phrase apart, and no closing mark is used."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0674' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A title used directly before a name (Professor Rahman), the name of a particular building (Faisal Mosque) and days of the week (Friday) all take capitals. ''Principal'' used as an ordinary noun stays lower case. Only this sentence gets every capital right.', wrong = '{"1": "''Professor'' before a name is part of the title and ''Mosque'' is part of the building''s name, so both need capitals.", "2": "Days of the week are proper nouns, so ''friday'' must be ''Friday''.", "3": "''Met'' is an ordinary verb and ''principal'' here is a common noun, so neither should have a capital."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0675' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A colon can follow a complete clause to introduce an explanation of it. ''The result was clear'' is a complete clause, and ''the drug did not work'' explains what the clear result was, so the colon is used correctly.', wrong = '{"1": "The colon splits the verb ''was'' from its complement ''clear'', so the first part is not a complete clause.", "2": "A colon cannot come after the article ''The'', and a comma between the two clauses would create a comma splice.", "3": "The colon splits the subject ''The result'' from its verb ''was'', which is incorrect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0676' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A hyphen is used after a prefix when the word without it would be confused with a different word. ''Re-cover'' means to cover again with new fabric, while ''recover'' means to get better or get back. The sofa was covered again, so ''re-covered'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Recovered'' means got better or got something back. It would mean the sofa got well, which changes the meaning.", "1": "A hyphen is never placed before an ending like ''-ed''. ''Recover-ed'' is not a correct form.", "2": "A prefix is joined to its word with a hyphen or directly, not separated by a space."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0677' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a person is spoken to directly (direct address), their name or title is set off with a comma. A direct question also ends with a question mark. So ''Doctor, is the patient awake?'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The comma after ''Doctor'' is missing, so the word looks like part of the question rather than the person addressed.", "2": "The comma is right, but a direct question must end with a question mark, not a full stop.", "3": "The comma is placed after ''is'', splitting the question, and ''Doctor'' is not set off."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0678' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A complete sentence needs at least one independent clause that can stand alone. ''More trials are needed'' is independent, and the ''although'' clause is attached to it. So ''Although the results were promising, more trials are needed.'' is complete.', wrong = '{"0": "''Although'' makes the clause dependent, so it cannot stand alone. It needs a main clause to finish the thought.", "1": "''Because'' makes the clause dependent. On its own it gives a reason without the main idea.", "2": "This has a subject, ''The results'', and a relative clause, but no main verb, so it is a fragment."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0679' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An exclamatory sentence expresses strong feeling and ends with an exclamation mark. ''How quickly the wound has healed!'' expresses surprise or delight at the speed of healing, so it is exclamatory.', wrong = '{"0": "It begins with ''How'', but it does not ask a question. The subject ''the wound'' comes before the verb, as in a statement.", "2": "An imperative sentence gives a command or request. This sentence does not tell anyone to do anything.", "3": "A declarative sentence makes a plain statement and ends with a full stop. This one expresses strong feeling."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0680' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An imperative sentence gives a command, instruction or request. The subject ''you'' is understood rather than stated. ''Please hand me the forceps'' is a polite request to someone, so it is imperative.', wrong = '{"0": "A declarative sentence states a fact. This sentence asks someone to do something.", "2": "An interrogative sentence asks a direct question and ends with a question mark. This is a request.", "3": "An exclamatory sentence expresses strong feeling, usually with an exclamation mark. This is a calm request."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0681' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An interrogative sentence asks a direct question, often with the auxiliary before the subject, and ends with a question mark. ''Has the report arrived yet?'' places ''has'' before ''the report'' and ends with ''?'', so it is interrogative.', wrong = '{"0": "This seeks information but is phrased as a command beginning with ''Tell'', so it is imperative.", "1": "This is a statement about the speaker wondering. The question is only indirect, so the sentence is declarative.", "2": "This expresses strong feeling and ends with an exclamation mark, so it is exclamatory."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0682' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A participial phrase begins with a participle and acts like an adjective. ''Exhausted by the long shift'' begins with the past participle ''exhausted'' and describes the nurse, so it is the participial phrase in the sentence.', wrong = '{"1": "''The nurse'' is the subject of the sentence, a simple noun phrase with no participle.", "2": "''Fell asleep'' is the main verb and its complement, not a participial phrase.", "3": "''The long shift'' is a noun phrase that sits inside the participial phrase. It is not the participial phrase itself."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0683' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An absolute phrase is a noun plus a modifier with no finite verb, and it modifies the whole sentence. ''The exam over'' has the noun ''the exam'' and the modifier ''over'', with no verb, and it sets the situation for the main clause.', wrong = '{"0": "A clause needs a finite verb. ''The exam over'' has none, so it cannot be a noun clause.", "1": "An adverb clause needs a subordinating conjunction and a verb, as in ''When the exam was over''. This has neither.", "3": "An infinitive phrase begins with ''to'' + verb, as in ''to finish the exam''. There is no infinitive here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0684' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An appositive is a noun or noun phrase placed next to another noun to rename or identify it. ''Our new cardiologist'' renames ''Dr Ayesha'' and is set off by commas, so it is an appositive phrase.', wrong = '{"0": "An adjective clause has a subject and a verb, such as ''who is our new cardiologist''. This phrase has no verb.", "1": "A participial phrase begins with a participle, such as ''working as our cardiologist''. There is no participle here.", "3": "An adverb phrase modifies a verb, telling how, when or where. This phrase renames a noun."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0685' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A modifying phrase should be placed right next to the word it describes. In ''Wearing a white coat, the doctor examined the patient'', the opening phrase comes directly before ''the doctor'', so it clearly describes the doctor.', wrong = '{"0": "The phrase ''wearing a white coat'' sits right after ''the patient'', so it can be read as describing the patient.", "1": "The clause ''who was wearing a white coat'' clearly refers to the patient, which is the opposite meaning.", "2": "''The patient wearing a white coat'' makes the patient the one in the coat."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0686' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A prepositional phrase can work as an adjective or an adverb. ''Under the microscope'' tells where she placed the slide, so it modifies the verb ''placed'' and works as an adverb (of place).', wrong = '{"0": "As an adjective, the phrase would describe which slide, as in ''the slide under the microscope was clean''. Here it tells where.", "2": "A noun phrase works as a subject or object. The object here is ''the slide'', not this phrase.", "3": "The phrase contains a preposition and a noun but no verb, so it cannot work as a verb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0687' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Until you return'' has its own subject (''you'') and verb (''return''), so it is a clause. It tells when I will wait, so it modifies the verb ''wait''. A clause that answers ''when?'' is an adverb clause of time.', wrong = '{"0": "A noun clause works as a subject or object. This clause tells when, not what.", "1": "An adjective clause describes a noun. This clause does not describe any noun; it modifies the verb.", "3": "A prepositional phrase has no subject and verb. ''Until you return'' contains ''you return'', so it is a clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0688' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Why he left'' follows the noun ''reason'' and tells us which reason is meant. A clause that describes a noun is an adjective (relative) clause, and ''why'' here is a relative adverb, as in ''the reason why''.', wrong = '{"1": "The subject of the sentence is ''The reason''. The clause describes that noun rather than acting as the subject itself.", "2": "An adverb clause modifies a verb. This clause modifies the noun ''reason''.", "3": "''Why he left'' cannot stand alone as a sentence, so it is a dependent clause, not an independent one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0689' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Where he lives'' answers the question ''I know what?'', so it is the object of the verb ''know''. A clause that works as a subject or object is a noun clause. A test: replace it with ''it'', and ''I know it'' still makes sense.', wrong = '{"0": "An adjective clause describes a noun, as in ''the house where he lives''. Here no noun comes before the clause.", "1": "An adverb clause of place would tell where the knowing happens. This clause tells what is known.", "2": "A prepositional phrase has no subject and verb. This group contains ''he lives'', so it is a clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0690' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Where the work was'' has a subject (''the work'') and a verb (''was''), so it is a clause. It tells where she went, modifying the verb ''went''. A clause that answers ''where?'' about a verb is an adverb clause of place.', wrong = '{"0": "A noun clause works as a subject or object. ''Went'' does not take an object, and this clause tells where.", "2": "An adjective clause describes a noun, as in ''the town where the work was''. Here no noun comes before the clause.", "3": "A participial phrase has no finite verb. This group contains the finite verb ''was'', so it is a clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0691' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A compound-complex sentence has at least two independent clauses and at least one dependent clause. Here ''she finished the report'' and ''then she went to bed'' are independent clauses joined by ''and'', and ''Although she was tired'' is dependent.', wrong = '{"0": "A complex sentence has only one independent clause. This sentence has two, joined by ''and''.", "2": "A compound sentence has two or more independent clauses but no dependent clause. This one has ''Although she was tired''.", "3": "A simple sentence has only one clause. This sentence has three."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0692' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Sitting near the window'' begins with the present participle ''sitting'' and has no subject or finite verb, so it is a phrase. It tells us which boy is meant, so it describes the noun ''boy'' and works as an adjective.', wrong = '{"0": "A gerund phrase acts as a noun, such as a subject or object. This phrase describes a noun instead.", "1": "An adverb clause needs a subject and a finite verb, such as ''who is sitting''. This group has neither.", "2": "An infinitive phrase begins with ''to'' + verb. There is no ''to'' here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0693' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a negative expression such as ''Never before'' begins a sentence, the auxiliary verb comes before the subject, as in a question. With the past perfect, the auxiliary is ''had'', so ''Never before had the doctors seen such a rare case'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "This keeps normal statement order. After a fronted ''Never before'', the auxiliary must come before the subject.", "1": "Putting ''seen'' before ''the doctors'' turns the doctors into the object, as if someone saw the doctors.", "3": "The past participle ''seen'' cannot come first. Only the auxiliary ''had'' moves before the subject."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0694' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When ''So + adjective'' is placed at the start of a sentence for emphasis, the verb comes before the subject. With the verb ''be'', the verb itself moves, so ''So severe was the storm that the school was closed'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "This keeps normal word order. After a fronted ''So severe'', the verb must come before the subject.", "2": "''Did'' is not used with the verb ''be''. The verb ''was'' itself is inverted.", "3": "This keeps normal order and uses the present ''is'', which does not match ''was closed''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0695' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Little'' used at the start with a negative meaning (''not at all'') causes inversion. In the simple past, the auxiliary ''did'' comes before the subject and the main verb is in base form: ''Little did he know that his life was about to change.''', wrong = '{"0": "This keeps normal statement order. After a fronted negative ''Little'', inversion with ''did'' is required.", "1": "''Did'' is used, but it must come before the subject: ''did he know'', not ''he did know''.", "2": "In modern English the main verb does not move before the subject; the auxiliary ''did'' is used instead."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0696' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A fronted ''Rarely'' requires inversion with an auxiliary. In the simple present, the auxiliary is do/does, and ''a patient'' is singular, so it takes ''does'', followed by the base verb ''recover'': ''Rarely does a patient recover so quickly.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Do'' is used with plural or ''I/you'' subjects. ''A patient'' is singular, so ''does'' is needed.", "2": "''Is'' cannot be followed by the base verb ''recover''. ''Is a patient recover'' is ungrammatical.", "3": "''Are'' does not agree with singular ''a patient'' and cannot be followed by the base verb ''recover''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0697' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The result clause ''they would have evacuated'' shows a third conditional, which needs the past perfect in the condition. ''If they had received'' can be inverted by dropping ''if'' and moving ''had'' first: ''Had they received the warning earlier''.', wrong = '{"0": "''If they received'' is past simple, used in second conditionals. A ''would have'' result needs the past perfect.", "2": "''They had received'' is an ordinary statement, not a condition. Without ''if'' or inversion, it cannot introduce the result.", "3": "''Have they received'' is a present perfect question and does not fit an unreal past condition."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0698' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ''The fact that he lied'', the clause ''that he lied'' tells us what the fact is, so it renames ''the fact''. A clause that renames a noun is a noun clause in apposition. Here ''that'' is a conjunction and plays no part inside the clause.', wrong = '{"0": "In an adjective clause, ''that'' would stand for a missing part inside the clause. ''He lied'' is complete, so ''that'' is a conjunction.", "2": "An adverb clause modifies a verb, telling when, why or how. This clause explains the noun ''fact''.", "3": "''That he lied'' depends on ''the fact'' and cannot stand alone here, so it is not independent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0699' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a sentence begins with ''Not until ...'', the main clause that follows is inverted. In the simple past, ''did'' comes before the subject and the main verb is in base form: ''did the doctors understand the extent of his injuries.''', wrong = '{"0": "This keeps normal word order. After a fronted ''Not until'' clause, the main clause must be inverted.", "1": "In modern English, main verbs like ''understood'' are not moved before the subject. The auxiliary ''did'' is used.", "2": "''Did'' is used, but it must come before the subject: ''did the doctors understand''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0700' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''If it had not been for your help'' is a third conditional, matched by ''I would have failed''. It can be inverted by dropping ''if'' and placing ''had'' first: ''Had it not been for your help, I would have failed.''', wrong = '{"1": "''Were it not for your help'' is correct without ''been'' and refers to the present. ''Were it not been'' is ungrammatical.", "2": "With ''if'', the verb ''had'' is needed: ''If it had not been''. ''If it not been'' is incorrect.", "3": "''Should'' is used for unlikely future conditions and does not combine with ''not been'' in this pattern."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0701' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A sentence like ''People say that he is honest'' can be made passive by making ''he'' the subject, then using ''is said'' + to-infinitive. So ''He is said to be honest'' is correct. The other passive pattern is ''It is said that he is honest''.', wrong = '{"0": "After ''is said'', a to-infinitive is needed, not an -ing form. ''Is said being'' is ungrammatical.", "2": "With ''It is said'', a that-clause must follow: ''It is said that he is honest''. ''It is said him to be'' is wrong.", "3": "''He said to be honest'' is active and changes the meaning. The passive needs ''is'': ''He is said''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0702' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ''It is believed that the virus spread from bats'', the spreading happened in the past, before the believing now. The personal passive uses a perfect infinitive (to have + past participle) for an earlier action: ''The virus is believed to have spread from bats.''', wrong = '{"0": "The simple infinitive ''to spread'' suggests a present habit or general fact, not a past event.", "2": "''To be spread'' is a passive infinitive in the present, which changes the meaning to something being spread now.", "3": "''Is believed'' must be followed by a to-infinitive, not an -ing form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0703' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The causative form ''have + object + past participle'' shows that someone else did a job for you, usually because you arranged it. The mechanic repaired the car at my request, so ''I had my car repaired'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "After the object, the causative needs the past participle ''repaired'', not the base form ''repair''.", "1": "''I had repaired my car'' is the past perfect, meaning I repaired it myself. That changes the meaning.", "2": "''Was had'' is not a correct verb form. The causative uses ''had'' as the main verb: ''I had my car repaired''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0704' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Get + object + past participle'' is a causative form like ''have something done''. It shows that someone else did the action for the subject. A clinic worker checked her blood pressure, so ''She got her blood pressure checked'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "After the object in a causative, the past participle ''checked'' is needed, not the base form ''check''.", "2": "The -ing form would suggest the blood pressure was doing the checking, which makes no sense.", "3": "''Got her blood pressure to check'' would mean the blood pressure checks something. A past participle is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0705' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Are repairing'' is the present continuous. Its passive is formed with am/is/are + being + past participle. ''The road'' is singular, so it takes ''is'': ''The road is being repaired.'' The doer ''by them'' is left out as unimportant.', wrong = '{"1": "''Is repaired'' is the simple present passive. It loses the idea of work happening right now.", "2": "''Has been repairing'' is an active present perfect continuous form. It would mean the road is doing the repairing.", "3": "''Is been'' is not a correct verb form. The continuous passive needs ''is being''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0706' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When an active sentence begins with ''let'', the passive keeps ''let'', then the new subject (the object), then ''be'' + past participle. So ''Let him close the door'' becomes ''Let the door be closed by him.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Let'' is followed by the base form ''be'', not ''is''. ''Let the door is closed'' is ungrammatical.", "1": "''Be'' is missing. The passive after ''let'' needs ''be'' + past participle: ''be closed''.", "3": "The word order is wrong. ''Let'' must come first, followed by ''the door''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0707' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Should have informed'' is a perfect modal referring to the past. Its passive is modal + have been + past participle. The object ''us'' becomes the subject ''we'', giving ''We should have been informed.''', wrong = '{"1": "''Should be informed'' is a present passive. It loses the past meaning of ''should have''.", "2": "''We should have informed'' is active and means we were the ones who should have informed someone.", "3": "A modal is always followed by the base form ''have'', never ''had''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0708' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Laugh at'' is a prepositional verb, so the preposition stays with the verb in both voices. ''Was laughed at'' is simple past, so the active is simple past too, and ''she'' becomes the object ''her'': ''The class laughed at her.''', wrong = '{"0": "The preposition ''at'' has been lost. ''Laugh'' needs ''at'' before its object.", "1": "The tense changes to past continuous, and the object form must be ''her'', not ''she''.", "2": "The roles are reversed. In the original, the class laughed at her, not the other way round."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0709' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ''They elected him captain'', ''him'' is the object and ''captain'' is a complement that describes him. In the passive, the object becomes the subject ''He'', and the complement stays after the verb: ''He was elected captain.''', wrong = '{"0": "The complement ''captain'' cannot become the subject. Only the object ''him'' can.", "2": "''He elected captain'' is active and means he did the electing, which changes the meaning.", "3": "''To'' is not used before this complement. ''He was elected captain'' is the correct form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0710' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To make an infinitive passive, use ''to be'' + past participle. The object ''the shop'' becomes the subject of the infinitive and is introduced by ''for''. So ''It is time to close the shop'' becomes ''It is time for the shop to be closed.''', wrong = '{"0": "''To be closed the shop'' puts the infinitive before its subject, which is wrong word order.", "1": "''For the shop being closed'' uses an -ing form. This pattern needs a to-infinitive.", "2": "''For'' is needed to introduce ''the shop'' as the subject of the infinitive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0711' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a passive sentence names no doer, the active form uses a general subject such as ''someone'' or ''they''. ''Was caught'' is simple past, so the active keeps the simple past: ''Someone caught the thief red-handed.''', wrong = '{"1": "''The thief caught red-handed'' is active in form and makes the thief the one doing the catching.", "2": "''Was catching'' is past continuous, so the tense of the original is not kept.", "3": "''Had caught'' is past perfect, so the tense of the original is not kept."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0712' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Why did he punish you?'' is a simple past question. Its passive is Why + was/were + subject + past participle. ''You'' always takes ''were'', and the doer ''him'' follows ''by'': ''Why were you punished by him?''', wrong = '{"0": "''You'' takes ''were'', even when it refers to one person. ''Was you'' is ungrammatical.", "1": "''Did'' is not used to form the passive, and ''did'' cannot be followed by ''punished''.", "3": "A direct question needs the auxiliary ''were'' before the subject ''you''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0713' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Verbs like see, hear and make take a bare infinitive in the active (''saw him leave''). In the passive, ''to'' is added: ''He was seen to leave the building.'' ''Seen'' is the past participle of ''see''.', wrong = '{"0": "In the passive, the bare infinitive becomes a to-infinitive, so ''to'' is needed before ''leave''.", "1": "''Saw'' is the past tense, not the past participle. The passive needs ''was seen''.", "3": "The auxiliary ''was'' is missing, so ''He seen'' is not a correct passive verb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0714' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Is going to'' expresses a future plan and stays in the passive. The infinitive after it becomes passive: to be + past participle. So ''The doctor is going to discharge the patient'' becomes ''The patient is going to be discharged by the doctor.''', wrong = '{"1": "''Be'' is missing. The passive infinitive after ''going to'' must be ''to be discharged''.", "2": "''Is being discharged'' is the present continuous passive. It loses the future meaning of ''going to''.", "3": "After ''be'', the passive needs the past participle ''discharged'', not the base form ''discharge''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0715' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Is following'' is the present continuous. Its passive is am/is/are + being + past participle. The object ''us'' becomes the subject ''we'', which takes ''are'': ''We are being followed.'' The vague doer ''somebody'' is dropped.', wrong = '{"0": "''We are followed'' is the simple present passive and loses the idea that it is happening right now.", "1": "After ''being'', the passive needs the past participle ''followed'', not the base form ''follow''.", "3": "''We have been following'' is active and means we are the ones doing the following."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0716' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A polite request or command is put into the passive using ''You are requested to'' + base verb. ''Please'' shows politeness, so ''Please help me'' becomes ''You are requested to help me.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Requested'' must be followed by a to-infinitive. ''Requested help me'' is ungrammatical.", "1": "''Me be helped please'' is not correct English and does not follow any passive pattern.", "3": "''Were requested'' moves the request into the past. The original request is being made now."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0717' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only verbs that take an object and describe an action normally form a passive. ''Have'' meaning possess is a state verb, so ''A new laptop is had by her'' is not used. The other sentences have action verbs that can be made passive.', wrong = '{"1": "''Bought'' is an action verb with an object, so it can be passive: ''A new laptop was bought by her.''", "2": "''Repaired'' is an action verb with an object, so it can be passive: ''The laptop was repaired by her.''", "3": "''Sold'' is an action verb with an object, so it can be passive: ''The laptop was sold by her.''"}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0718' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A wish or prayer beginning with ''May'' is reported with a verb such as ''prayed'' or ''wished'', followed by ''that''. The word order becomes a statement, ''may'' changes to ''might'', and ''you'' becomes ''I'': ''The old man prayed that I might live long.''', wrong = '{"0": "This keeps the exclamatory order ''may I live'' and uses plain ''said to me'', which is wrong for a prayer.", "1": "''Prayed'' is right, but ''may you'' keeps the original order and pronoun. It should be ''I might''.", "3": "''Told me to live long'' turns a blessing into a command, which changes the meaning."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0719' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After a past reporting verb, ''may'' changes to ''might'' and time words shift: ''tomorrow'' becomes ''the next day''. The speaker refers to himself, so ''I'' becomes ''he'': ''He said that he might come the next day.''', wrong = '{"0": "Neither the modal nor the time word has been changed. ''May'' should be ''might'' and ''tomorrow'' should be ''the next day''.", "1": "''Might'' is correct, but ''tomorrow'' should change to ''the next day'' in indirect speech.", "2": "The speaker is talking about himself, so ''I'' must become ''he'', not stay as ''I''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0720' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A yes/no question is reported with ''asked'' + if/whether, and the words return to statement order. After the past verb ''asked'', the present perfect ''have finished'' shifts to the past perfect, and ''you/your'' become ''I/my'': ''She asked me if I had finished my assignment.''', wrong = '{"0": "This keeps the question order ''have I'' and the present tense, and it has no ''if'' or ''whether''.", "2": "The structure is right, but ''have finished'' should shift back to ''had finished'' after ''asked''.", "3": "A question is reported with ''asked'', not ''said to me that'', and ''had I finished'' keeps question order."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0721' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Forbid'' means to order someone not to do something, so the negative idea is already inside the verb. It is followed by object + positive to-infinitive: ''The doctor forbade me to eat sweets.'' ''The doctor told me not to eat sweets'' would also be correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Forbade'' already contains ''not'', so adding ''not to eat'' creates a double negative and reverses the sense.", "1": "A modal like ''must'' cannot follow ''to'', so ''to not must eat'' is ungrammatical.", "2": "''Said me'' is wrong because ''said'' does not take a direct personal object, and a command is not reported as a plain statement."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0722' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After a past reporting verb, the past continuous ''was reading'' shifts back to the past perfect continuous ''had been reading''. A past simple verb in a time clause, ''when the lights went out'', often stays unchanged because the time order is already clear.', wrong = '{"0": "''Has been reading'' is present perfect continuous, which is not a backshift of the past continuous.", "1": "The time clause cannot move into the present. ''When the lights go out'' clashes with a past story.", "3": "''Is reading'' moves the main verb into the present instead of shifting it back to the past."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0723' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Please'' shows a polite request, so the reporting verb becomes ''requested''. The request becomes object + to-infinitive, and the place word ''here'' changes to ''there'': ''He requested me to wait there.''', wrong = '{"0": "The reporting verb is right, but ''here'' should change to ''there'' in indirect speech.", "1": "''That'' is not used before an infinitive command. ''Requested me that wait'' is ungrammatical.", "3": "''Said me'' is incorrect, and ''please'' should be replaced by the reporting verb ''requested''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0724' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In indirect speech after a past reporting verb, ''will'' becomes ''would'', ''this'' becomes ''that'', and ''today'' becomes ''that day''. The speaker''s ''I'' becomes ''she'', giving ''She said that she would finish that work that day.''', wrong = '{"1": "''Will'' has changed to ''would'', but ''this'' and ''today'' still need to change to ''that'' and ''that day''.", "2": "''Will'' should change to ''would'' after the past reporting verb ''said''.", "3": "''Today'' should change to ''that day'' in indirect speech."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0725' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Changing indirect speech back to direct reverses the shifts: ''he'' (the speaker) becomes ''I'', the past perfect ''had seen'' becomes the past simple ''saw'', and ''the day before'' becomes ''yesterday''. So ''I saw the film yesterday'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "The words inside the quotation are still in indirect form (''had seen'', ''the day before''). They must change back.", "1": "The speaker is talking about himself, so inside the quotation he would say ''I'', not ''He''.", "2": "''I see the film tomorrow'' changes both the tense and the time. The event happened in the past."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0726' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the reported words state a universal or scientific truth, the tense usually does not shift back, even after a past reporting verb. Water boiling at 100 degrees Celsius at sea level is always true, so ''The teacher said that water boils at 100 degrees Celsius at sea level'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Boiled'' shifts a permanent fact into the past, as if it is no longer true. Universal truths keep the present tense.", "2": "''Had boiled'' turns a general fact into a single past event, which changes the meaning.", "3": "''Would boil'' suggests a condition or prediction, not a fact that is always true."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0727' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An exclamation is reported with a verb such as ''exclaimed'' and turned into a statement. ''How beautiful'' is replaced by ''very beautiful'', the word order becomes normal, and ''is'' shifts back to ''was'': ''She exclaimed that the garden was very beautiful.''', wrong = '{"0": "''How beautiful was the garden'' keeps inverted word order, which is not used in a reported statement.", "1": "''Asked'' reports a question, but the original is an exclamation of admiration, not a question.", "2": "''That'' and ''how'' cannot both introduce the clause, and the tense ''is'' has not shifted to ''was''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0728' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The main question ''Do you know'' is a yes/no question, so it is reported with ''if'', in statement order, with ''know'' shifting to ''knew''. The embedded part ''where the station is'' also shifts to ''where the station was''. Both clauses use statement word order.', wrong = '{"1": "This keeps question order (''do I know'') and present tenses. Reported questions need ''if'' and backshift.", "2": "The first part is right, but ''where was the station'' keeps question order. It should be ''where the station was''.", "3": "''Know'' should shift back to ''knew'' after the past reporting verb ''asked''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0729' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A wh-question is reported with ''asked'' + the same question word, followed by statement word order (subject before verb). The present continuous ''are doing'' shifts to the past continuous ''was doing'', and ''you'' becomes ''she'': ''He asked her what she was doing.''', wrong = '{"0": "''What was she doing'' keeps question order. A reported question needs statement order: ''what she was doing''.", "1": "''Is doing'' has not shifted back. After the past verb ''asked'', it should be ''was doing''.", "2": "''That'' is not used before a reported wh-question. The question word ''what'' introduces the clause."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0730' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Hurrah'' expresses joy, so it is replaced by ''exclaimed with joy''. The interjection itself is dropped, ''we'' (the speaker''s team) becomes ''they'', and ''have won'' shifts back to ''had won'': ''She exclaimed with joy that they had won the match.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Hurrah'' is a cry of joy, not sorrow. ''Alas'' would be reported as ''with sorrow''.", "2": "The interjection ''hurrah'' should be dropped and replaced by a phrase like ''with joy''.", "3": "The speaker''s ''we'' should become ''they'' in indirect speech, since the reporter is not part of the team."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0731' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Let me go home early'' asks for permission. Such a request is reported with ''requested'' (or ''asked'') + ''that he might be allowed to'', so ''He requested that he might be allowed to go home early'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "This simply repeats the direct words after ''said'', without changing pronouns or structure.", "2": "''Ordered'' turns a request for permission into a command, which changes the meaning.", "3": "''Let'' cannot follow ''that'' in this way. The idea of permission must be expressed with ''might be allowed to''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0732' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With ''neither ... nor'' and ''either ... or'', the verb agrees with the subject nearer to it. Here the nearer subject is ''his relatives'', which is plural, so the plural verb ''were'' is needed: ''Neither the patient nor his relatives were aware of the risk.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Was'' would agree with ''the patient'', but the verb follows the nearer subject, ''relatives'', which is plural.", "1": "''Is'' is singular and present. The nearer subject is plural, and ''were'' fits the past context.", "3": "''Has been'' is singular. The nearer subject ''relatives'' would need ''have been''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0733' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When ''each'' is the subject, it refers to every member of a group one at a time and is singular. ''Of the students'' does not change this, so the verb must be singular: ''Each of the students was given a certificate.''', wrong = '{"1": "''Were'' agrees with ''students'', but that word is inside the ''of'' phrase. The subject is ''each'', which is singular.", "2": "''Are'' is plural. ''Each'' needs a singular verb, and ''was given'' fits the past event.", "3": "''Have been'' is plural. ''Each'' needs a singular verb such as ''was'' or ''has been''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0734' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'There are two verbs to match. In the relative clause, ''that'' refers to ''reasons'' (plural), so it is ''that were given''. The main subject is ''One'', which is singular, so the main verb is ''was'': ''One of the reasons that were given was unconvincing.''', wrong = '{"0": "The first blank is in the relative clause, where ''that'' refers to the plural ''reasons'', so ''were'' is needed.", "2": "The second blank goes with the main subject ''One'', which is singular, so ''was'' is needed.", "3": "Both agreements are reversed: the relative clause needs plural ''were'', and the main verb needs singular ''was''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0735' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ''one of those doctors who ...'', the relative pronoun ''who'' refers to ''doctors'', not to ''one''. ''Doctors'' is plural, so the verb in the relative clause must be plural: ''She is one of those doctors who are never late.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Is'' would agree with ''one'', but ''who'' refers to the plural ''doctors'' just before it.", "1": "''Was'' is singular and past. The relative clause needs a plural present verb for a general habit.", "2": "''Has been'' is singular. The plural ''doctors'' would need ''have been'', and ''are'' suits a general habit."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0736' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A collective noun like ''committee'' takes a singular verb when the group acts as one unit, but a plural verb when its members act as individuals. ''Their opinions'' shows the members are thought of separately, so ''The committee are divided in their opinions'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "''Is'' treats the committee as one unit, but ''their opinions'' shows the members are being considered separately.", "2": "''Has divided'' would mean the committee divided something, which changes the meaning.", "3": "''Does divided'' is not a correct verb form. ''Divided'' here is an adjective and needs ''be''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0737' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With percentages and fractions, the verb agrees with the noun after ''of''. ''Population'' is a singular collective noun, so ''is vaccinated''. ''Patients'' is plural, so ''have recovered''. The correct pair is ''is / have''.', wrong = '{"0": "Both agreements are reversed: ''population'' is singular and needs ''is'', while ''patients'' is plural and needs ''have''.", "1": "''Is'' is right for ''population'', but ''patients'' is plural and needs ''have'', not ''has''.", "2": "''Have'' is right for ''patients'', but ''population'' is singular and needs ''is'', not ''are''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0738' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The subject is ''the pair'', which is singular. The phrase ''of shoes'' only describes the pair and does not change its number, so the verb is singular: ''The pair of shoes is under the bed.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Are'' agrees with ''shoes'', but ''shoes'' is inside the ''of'' phrase. The subject ''pair'' is singular.", "1": "''Were'' is plural and past. The subject ''pair'' is singular and the sentence is in the present.", "2": "''Have been'' is plural. The singular subject ''pair'' would need ''has been'', and ''is'' fits better."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0739' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After ''a lot of'', the verb agrees with the noun that follows. ''Water'' is uncountable, so it takes a singular verb: ''was wasted''. ''People'' is plural, so it takes a plural verb: ''were unhappy''. The correct pair is ''was / were''.', wrong = '{"1": "Both agreements are reversed: uncountable ''water'' needs ''was'', and plural ''people'' needs ''were''.", "2": "''Was'' is right for ''water'', but ''people'' is plural and needs ''were''.", "3": "''Were'' is right for ''people'', but ''water'' is uncountable and needs the singular ''was''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0740' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With ''either ... or'', the verb agrees with the subject nearer to it. The nearer subject is ''he'', which is third person singular, so the verb is ''is'': ''Either you or he is to blame.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Are'' would agree with ''you'', but the verb follows the nearer subject ''he'', which needs ''is''.", "1": "''Am'' is used only with ''I''. Neither subject here is ''I''.", "3": "''Were'' is plural or used with ''you'', and it is past. The nearer subject ''he'' needs present ''is''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0741' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Statistics'' as the name of a subject or field of study is singular, like mathematics or physics. So it takes a singular verb: ''Statistics is a compulsory subject.'' When it means a set of figures, as in ''The statistics show...'', it is plural.', wrong = '{"0": "''Are'' treats statistics as figures. As the name of a subject, it takes a singular verb.", "1": "''Were'' is plural and past. The subject is singular and the sentence states a present fact.", "3": "''Have been'' is plural. As the name of a subject, statistics needs a singular verb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0742' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With fractions, the verb agrees with the noun after ''of''. ''Syllabus'' is singular, so the verb is singular. The present perfect is needed with ''so far'', so ''Two-thirds of the syllabus has been covered so far'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "''Have'' is plural. The noun after ''of'', ''syllabus'', is singular, so ''has'' is needed.", "2": "''Are been'' is not a correct verb form. The passive perfect is ''has been covered''.", "3": "''Were been'' is not a correct verb form. The passive perfect needs ''has been covered''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0743' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a question, the verb comes before the subject. The subject here is ''the results'', which is plural; ''of yesterday''s test'' only describes them. So the plural verb is needed: ''Where are the results of yesterday''s test?''', wrong = '{"0": "''Is'' agrees with ''test'', but ''test'' is inside the ''of'' phrase. The subject ''results'' is plural.", "1": "''Was'' is singular. The subject ''the results'' comes after the verb and is plural.", "3": "''Has'' is singular and cannot act as the main verb here; ''Where has the results'' is ungrammatical."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0744' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the saying ''Slow and steady wins the race'', the two adjectives together name one idea: a slow, steady approach. Because they express a single idea, they take a singular verb. A general truth uses the simple present, so ''wins'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Win'' is plural. ''Slow and steady'' names one idea, so the verb must be singular.", "1": "''Are winning'' is plural and continuous. The saying states a general truth with one idea as its subject.", "3": "''Have won'' is plural. The single idea ''slow and steady'' needs a singular verb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0745' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A comma splice joins two complete sentences (independent clauses) with only a comma. ''The lab was closed'' and ''we went home'' can each stand alone, so joining them with just a comma is a comma splice. It needs a conjunction, a semicolon or a full stop.', wrong = '{"1": "The comma is followed by the conjunction ''so'', which correctly joins the two independent clauses.", "2": "''Because the lab was closed'' is a dependent clause, so a comma before the main clause is correct.", "3": "A semicolon can correctly join two closely related independent clauses without a conjunction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0746' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''The patient felt dizzy she sat down'' is a run-on: two independent clauses with no punctuation or linking word. One correct fix is a comma plus a coordinating conjunction before the second clause: ''The patient felt dizzy, so she sat down.''', wrong = '{"0": "Adding only a comma between two independent clauses turns the run-on into a comma splice, which is still an error.", "1": "The conjunction is right, but the comma must come before ''so'', not after it.", "3": "The comma wrongly separates the subject from its verb, and the two clauses are still not joined."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0747' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A dangling modifier is an opening phrase with no suitable subject to describe. In ''Walking into the ward, the smell of antiseptic was strong'', the subject after the comma is ''the smell'', so the sentence seems to say the smell was walking into the ward.', wrong = '{"0": "''I'' comes right after the phrase, and I am the one walking into the ward, so the modifier attaches correctly.", "1": "The opening is a full clause with its own subject, ''I'', so there is no dangling phrase.", "3": "The clause ''as I walked into the ward'' has its own subject, ''I'', so nothing dangles."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0748' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An opening participial phrase must describe the subject that comes right after the comma. The students are the ones who finished the experiment, so the main clause must start with them: ''Having finished the experiment, the students wrote their report.''', wrong = '{"1": "The subject after the comma is ''the report'', which makes the report the one that finished the experiment.", "2": "The subject is again ''the report'', so the sentence suggests the report finished the experiment.", "3": "The subject is ''it'', which cannot have finished an experiment, so the opening phrase dangles."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0749' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Redundancy means repeating an idea that is already contained in another word. ''Merge'' already means to come together and become one, so adding ''together'' or ''into one'' repeats the meaning. ''The two rivers merged'' says it clearly and briefly.', wrong = '{"0": "''Together'' repeats the meaning already contained in ''merged''.", "1": "Both ''together'' and ''into one'' repeat what ''merged'' already means.", "2": "''Both of the two'' repeats the idea of two, and ''merged together'' is also redundant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0750' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Several phrases in the original are padded. An opinion is always one''s own, so ''personal'' adds nothing, and an outcome is already final. ''Was a total surprise to everyone'' can become ''surprised everyone''. So ''I think the outcome surprised everyone'' keeps the meaning in the fewest words.', wrong = '{"0": "''Personal opinion'' and ''final outcome'' still contain redundant words.", "2": "''Final end result'' repeats the same idea three times, and ''personally'' is also unnecessary.", "3": "''Each and every one'' is a wordy way of saying ''everyone'', so this is not the most concise version."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0751' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Fewer'' is used with countable plural nouns, things we can count one by one, such as patients, books or days. ''Less'' is used with uncountable nouns, such as water or time. Patients can be counted, so ''fewer patients'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Less'' is used with uncountable nouns, as in ''less water''. ''Patients'' is a countable plural noun.", "2": "''Lesser'' means smaller in importance or degree, as in ''the lesser of two evils''. It does not compare numbers.", "3": "''Little'' is used with uncountable nouns and is not a comparative, so it cannot be followed by ''than''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0752' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''A number of'' is used with countable plural nouns, such as students, patients or books. ''An amount of'' and ''a great deal of'' are used with uncountable nouns. Students can be counted, so ''A large number of students applied'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Amount'' is used with uncountable nouns, as in ''a large amount of money''. Students are countable.", "1": "''A great deal of'' is used with uncountable nouns, as in ''a great deal of time''. It does not fit ''students''.", "2": "''Quantity'' is used for things that are measured or for goods, not for people like students."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0753' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Lie'' (lie, lay, lain) means to rest in a flat position and takes no object. ''Lay'' (lay, laid, laid) means to put something down and needs an object. The patient is to rest flat on the bed, with no object, so ''to lie down'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "''Lay'' means to put something down and needs an object, as in ''lay the baby on the bed''. Here there is no object.", "2": "''Laid'' is the past tense of ''lay'', and after ''to'' the base form is needed.", "3": "''Lain'' is the past participle of ''lie'' and cannot follow ''to''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0754' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Items in a list should have the same grammatical form, which is called parallel structure. ''Reading'', ''writing'' and ''swimming'' are all gerunds (-ing forms), so ''She likes reading, writing and swimming'' is balanced and correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''To swim'' is an infinitive, while the other two items are gerunds. The list is not parallel.", "1": "''To read'' is an infinitive, while ''writing'' and ''swimming'' are gerunds. The forms are mixed.", "3": "''Reading'' is a gerund, but ''to write'' and ''swim'' are infinitive forms. The list mixes forms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0755' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When several adjectives come before a noun, English usually follows this order: opinion, size, age, shape, colour, origin, material, purpose. ''Lovely'' is opinion, ''round'' is shape and ''wooden'' is material, so ''a lovely round wooden table'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "This reverses the natural order. Opinion (''lovely'') should come first and material (''wooden'') last.", "2": "Opinion (''lovely'') should come before shape (''round''), since opinion words come first.", "3": "Shape (''round'') should come before material (''wooden'')."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0756' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a question is placed inside a statement (an embedded question), it uses statement word order: subject before verb, with no auxiliary ''does''. So ''What time does the clinic open?'' becomes ''I don''t know what time the clinic opens.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Does the clinic open'' keeps question word order. Inside a statement, it should be ''the clinic opens''.", "1": "The subject ''the clinic'' must come before its verb ''opens''. ''Opens the clinic'' reverses them.", "2": "The words are scrambled, with ''does'' placed between ''what'' and ''time''. This is not English word order."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0757' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Scarcely'' at the start of a sentence needs inversion (''had she sat down'') and is paired with ''when'' (or ''before''), not ''than''. It shows one action followed immediately by another: ''Scarcely had she sat down when the phone rang.''', wrong = '{"0": "''Than'' is paired with ''No sooner'', not with ''Scarcely''. ''Scarcely'' takes ''when''.", "2": "After a fronted ''Scarcely'', the auxiliary must come before the subject: ''had she'', not ''she had''.", "3": "After ''had'', the past participle ''sat'' is needed, not the base form ''sit''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0758' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When comparing exactly two people or things, the comparative form is used, not the superlative. In the pattern ''the + comparative + of the two'', ''the'' comes before ''more''. So ''She is the more intelligent of the two sisters'' is correct.', wrong = '{"1": "''The most'' is a superlative, used for three or more. With only two sisters, the comparative is needed.", "2": "The pattern ''the more ... of the two'' needs ''the'' before ''more''.", "3": "''More intelligenter'' is a double comparative. Long adjectives use ''more'' only, with no -er ending."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0759' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Explain'' does not take a person as a direct indirect object, the way ''give'' or ''tell'' does. The person must be introduced with ''to''. So ''Please explain this problem to me'' is correct, and ''explain me'' is a common error.', wrong = '{"0": "''Explain me'' is a common error. The person must be introduced by ''to'': ''explain it to me''.", "1": "''About'' is unnecessary because ''explain'' takes the problem directly as its object.", "3": "''To'' is missing before ''me'', so ''explain this problem me'' is ungrammatical."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0760' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''The reason ... is that'' is the correct pattern. ''Reason'' already expresses cause, so following it with ''because'' repeats the same idea. So ''The reason he was late is that the bus broke down'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Reason'' and ''because'' both express cause, so ''the reason ... is because'' is redundant.", "1": "''Reason why'' and ''is because'' each add an unnecessary word of cause, making it doubly redundant.", "3": "A clause beginning with ''because'' cannot act as the subject of a sentence in standard English."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0761' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Discuss'' is a transitive verb that takes a direct object without any preposition. We discuss something, not ''discuss about'' it. So the error is in ''discussed about''; it should be ''The committee discussed the proposal at length yesterday.''', wrong = '{"1": "''The proposal'' is correct. It is the direct object of ''discussed''.", "2": "''At length'' is correct. It is a phrase meaning in detail or for a long time.", "3": "''Yesterday'' is correct. It fits the past tense verb ''discussed''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0762' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the reported clause, the subject is ''each of the boys''. ''Each'' is singular, so the verb must also be singular: ''each of the boys has submitted his assignment''. The plural ''have submitted'' is the error.', wrong = '{"0": "''The teacher said that'' is correct. It properly introduces the reported statement.", "1": "''Each of the boys'' is a correct subject. Only the verb after it needs to agree with ''each''.", "3": "''His assignment on time'' is correct. ''His'' agrees with the singular subject ''each''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0763' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''No sooner'' is always paired with ''than'', not ''when''. The correct sentence is ''No sooner did the doctor arrive than the patient began to feel better.'' So the error is in ''when the patient''.', wrong = '{"0": "''No sooner did the doctor arrive'' is correct. A fronted ''no sooner'' takes inversion with an auxiliary.", "2": "''Began to feel'' is correct. ''Begin'' can be followed by a to-infinitive.", "3": "''Better'' is correct. It is the comparative adjective after the linking verb ''feel''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0764' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Hardly'' already has a negative meaning (almost not). It must be followed by a positive word such as ''anyone'' or ''anything'', not a negative one. So ''Hardly anyone came to the meeting'' is correct and means almost nobody came.', wrong = '{"0": "''Hardly'' and ''no one'' are both negative, so ''hardly no one'' is a double negative.", "2": "''Hardly'' and ''nobody'' are both negative, so ''hardly nobody'' is a double negative.", "3": "''Not'' and ''hardly'' are both negative, so ''not hardly'' is a double negative."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0765' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The fixed combination is ''married to'' when saying who someone''s husband or wife is. So ''She is married to a doctor'' is correct. The same preposition is used in ''get married to someone'' and ''engaged to someone''.', wrong = '{"0": "''Married with'' is a common error. The standard preposition is ''to''.", "1": "''Married by'' would mean the doctor performed the marriage ceremony, which changes the meaning.", "2": "''Married from'' is not used in English to name a spouse."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0766' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The pattern ''too + adjective + to-infinitive'' means the quality is so great that something cannot happen. ''The patient is too weak to walk'' means he is so weak that he cannot walk.', wrong = '{"1": "''So'' pairs with ''that'', as in ''so weak that he cannot walk''. It does not take a to-infinitive.", "2": "''Too'' is followed by a to-infinitive, not a that-clause. ''Too weak that'' mixes two patterns.", "3": "''Very'' only adds strength and does not link to a to-infinitive of result. ''Very weak to walk'' is incorrect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0767' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Advice'' is an uncountable noun in English. It has no plural form and does not take ''a'' or ''an''. To speak of a quantity, use ''some advice'' or ''a piece of advice''. So ''She gave me some useful advice'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Advices'' is incorrect because ''advice'' is uncountable and has no plural form.", "1": "An uncountable noun does not take ''a''. Say ''a piece of advice'' or ''some advice''.", "2": "''Advice'' takes no article, and ''useful'' begins with a ''y'' consonant sound, so it would take ''a'', not ''an''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0768' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The correct spelling is ''millennium'', a period of a thousand years. It has a double ''l'' and a double ''n'': m-i-l-l-e-n-n-i-u-m. It comes from Latin ''mille'' (thousand, double l) and ''annus'' (year, double n).', wrong = '{"0": "''Millenium'' has only one ''n''. The word needs a double ''n'', from the Latin ''annus''.", "2": "''Milennium'' has only one ''l''. The word needs a double ''l'', from the Latin ''mille''.", "3": "''Milenium'' is missing both one ''l'' and one ''n''. Both letters must be doubled."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0769' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Before a singular countable noun with an adjective, the pattern is ''such a + adjective + noun''. ''Crowd'' is singular and countable, so ''I have never seen such a large crowd'' is correct.', wrong = '{"0": "''Crowd'' is a singular countable noun, so the article ''a'' is needed after ''such''.", "2": "''So'' is followed by an adjective alone (''so large''). Before an adjective + noun, it needs ''so large a crowd''.", "3": "The article comes after ''such'', not before it: ''such a'', not ''a such''."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'ENG-0770' AND answer = 1;
